(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4c.363a816d7e.js"]={"44":[{"id":"sdl44_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do large lesions restricted to the cerebellar cortex alone, sparing the deep nuclei, typically fail to produce lasting motor deficits?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum modulates and refines ongoing movement rather than generating motor commands itself, so cortex-only damage leaves the nuclear output relatively intact","B":"The cerebellar cortex has no functional connections to the deep nuclei, so cortical damage cannot possibly affect nuclear output","C":"The cerebellum regenerates cortical tissue rapidly after any injury sustained, restoring full function within just a couple of days after even a fairly extensive cortical lesion occurs","D":"The cerebellar cortex directly generates the corticospinal tract itself, so its loss is compensated by redundant corticospinal fibers located elsewhere","E":"The cerebellum has no role in movement, so any lesion there produces no motor consequence regardless of its location or size"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cerebellum functions as a coordination and error-correction system, comparing intended movement to actual movement and refining it in real time, rather than initiating movement itself, so damage limited to the cortex, without involving the nuclei, produces uncoordinated but not paralyzed movement. The cortex does have extensive connections to the deep nuclei, contradicting the first wrong choice. The cerebellum does not regenerate tissue to restore function within days. The cerebellar cortex does not generate the corticospinal tract, that arises from the cerebral motor cortex. The cerebellum clearly does influence movement, just not through generating commands directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the distinction between cortex-only and cortex-plus-nuclei cerebellar lesions is a classic testable nuance. COMLEX: this same logic explains why cerebellar strength testing is typically normal despite obvious incoordination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — the spinocerebellar pathway already compared to DCML, ALS, and corticospinal, now used to frame the cerebellum as a modulator rather than an initiator of movement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a cerebellar lesion has uncoordinated, asynergistic movements but normal strength on manual muscle testing. What does this dissociation best illustrate?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum generates muscle force directly through its own descending corticospinal-equivalent pathway to the spinal cord","B":"Manual muscle testing is simply an unreliable examination technique that fails to detect cerebellar-type weakness in most patients","C":"The cerebellum coordinates the timing and pattern of muscle activation rather than generating the force of contraction itself","D":"The patient must have a coexisting corticospinal tract lesion that happens to spare strength entirely despite the pathway damage","E":"Cerebellar lesions always produce weakness that is simply too mild to be detected by any standard bedside examination technique"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cerebellum coordinates the precise timing, sequencing, and pattern of muscle activation needed for smooth, accurate movement, rather than generating the force of contraction, so a cerebellar lesion produces incoordination, asynergistic movement, without measurable weakness on strength testing. The cerebellum does not generate force through its own corticospinal-equivalent pathway, force generation is a corticospinal and lower motor neuron function. Manual muscle testing is a valid and reliable technique for detecting true weakness. A coexisting corticospinal lesion is not required to explain this classic, well-documented dissociation. Cerebellar lesions do not produce a form of weakness too mild to detect, they produce no true weakness at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal strength with abnormal coordination is a hallmark distinguishing cerebellar from corticospinal tract disease on exam questions. COMLEX: always pair coordination testing with formal strength testing to make this distinction at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — the spinocerebellar pathway already compared to DCML, ALS, and corticospinal, now used to frame the cerebellum as a modulator rather than an initiator of movement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement best captures the cerebellum's role as an error-correction system during ongoing movement?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum plans the initial goal of a movement before any motor cortex activity occurs, then hands off execution entirely to the cortex","B":"The cerebellum stores permanent memories of every movement ever performed and simply replays them identically on each later attempt","C":"The cerebellum receives no sensory feedback during movement and operates purely on a fixed, pre-programmed motor sequence","D":"The cerebellum continuously compares intended movement, based on efferent copy signals, to actual movement, based on sensory feedback, and adjusts motor output accordingly throughout the task","E":"The cerebellum corrects errors only after a movement is completed, with no ability to influence the movement while it is still occurring"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cerebellum receives an efferent copy of the intended motor command alongside real-time sensory feedback about the movement actually occurring, and it continuously compares the two, adjusting motor output on the fly, the essence of its role as an error-correction system. The cerebellum does not plan the initial movement goal, that originates in association and motor planning cortices. It does not simply replay fixed memories unchanged, its function is adaptive and error-correcting. It very much receives extensive sensory feedback during movement, contrary to the false claim of none. It corrects errors during, not only after, movement, which is precisely what allows smooth, accurate real-time coordination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the efferent copy versus sensory feedback comparison model is the conceptual core of cerebellar function tested on exams. COMLEX: this real-time correction model explains why cerebellar lesions worsen with movement, unlike static, resting deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — the spinocerebellar pathway already compared to DCML, ALS, and corticospinal, now used to frame the cerebellum as a modulator rather than an initiator of movement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cerebellar functional zone receives its predominant input from the ipsilateral vestibular ganglion and nuclei, and what is its primary role?","choices":{"A":"The spinocerebellum, which controls posture, balance, and equilibrium","B":"The cerebrocerebellum, which controls posture, balance, and equilibrium","C":"The vestibulocerebellum, which controls fine, dexterous distal limb movement timing","D":"The vestibulocerebellum, which controls posture, balance, and equilibrium","E":"The cerebrocerebellum, which controls axial and proximal limb muscle tone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The vestibulocerebellum, the flocculonodular lobe and adjacent vermis, receives predominant vestibular input and, through vestibulospinal and reticulospinal projections, controls posture, balance, and equilibrium. The spinocerebellum receives predominant spinal input, not vestibular, and controls axial and limb muscle tone rather than being defined by vestibular afferents. The cerebrocerebellum receives predominant pontine input relayed from cerebral cortex, not vestibular input. Fine, dexterous distal limb movement timing is the cerebrocerebellum's role, not the vestibulocerebellum's. Axial and proximal limb muscle tone control is more characteristic of the spinocerebellum, not the cerebrocerebellum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each cerebellar zone to its defining input, vestibular, spinal, or pontine, and its primary function is essential exam content. COMLEX: vestibulocerebellar dysfunction classically produces truncal ataxia and nystagmus rather than limb dysmetria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information traced from hair cells to the cerebellum, now the anatomic entry point for the vestibulocerebellar module","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cerebellar functional zone receives its predominant input via the posterior and anterior spinocerebellar tracts and cuneocerebellar fibers, and what is its primary role?","choices":{"A":"The vestibulocerebellum, which controls axial musculature through vermal/fastigial connections and limb musculature through intermediate zone/interposed nuclei connections","B":"The cerebrocerebellum, which controls axial musculature through vermal/fastigial connections and limb musculature through intermediate zone/interposed nuclei connections","C":"The spinocerebellum, which controls axial musculature through vermal/fastigial connections and limb musculature through intermediate zone/interposed nuclei connections","D":"The spinocerebellum, which controls precise dexterous distal limb movement timing through dentate nucleus connections exclusively","E":"The vestibulocerebellum, which controls precise dexterous distal limb movement timing through dentate nucleus connections exclusively"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The spinocerebellum, comprising the vermal and intermediate zones, receives its predominant input from the spinocerebellar tracts and cuneocerebellar fibers, and controls axial musculature via the vermis/fastigial nucleus and limb musculature/tone via the intermediate zone/interposed nuclei. The vestibulocerebellum is defined by vestibular, not spinocerebellar tract, input. The cerebrocerebellum is defined by pontine, not spinocerebellar tract, input. Precise dexterous distal limb movement timing through the dentate nucleus is the cerebrocerebellum's role, not the spinocerebellum's, and the vestibulocerebellum does not use the dentate nucleus at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spinocerebellum's dual output, axial via vermis/fastigial and limb tone via intermediate zone/interposed nuclei, is frequently tested as a two-part fact. COMLEX: spinocerebellar dysfunction correlates with the ataxic, wide-based gait pattern seen in many degenerative ataxias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information traced from hair cells to the cerebellum, now the anatomic entry point for the vestibulocerebellar module","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cerebellar functional zone receives its predominant input via a crossed pontocerebellar projection relayed from the contralateral cerebral cortex, and what is its primary role?","choices":{"A":"The spinocerebellum, which plans and controls precise, dexterous, well-timed movements of the extremities via the dentate nucleus","B":"The vestibulocerebellum, which plans and controls precise, dexterous, well-timed movements of the extremities via the dentate nucleus","C":"The cerebrocerebellum/pontocerebellum, which instead controls posture, balance, and equilibrium via the fastigial nucleus rather than the dentate nucleus","D":"The spinocerebellum, which controls posture, balance, and equilibrium via the fastigial nucleus and its various downstream projections to other centers","E":"The cerebrocerebellum/pontocerebellum, which plans and controls precise, dexterous, well-timed movements of the extremities via the dentate nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cerebrocerebellum, also called the pontocerebellum, the large lateral zone, receives its predominant input via crossed pontocerebellar fibers relaying signals from the contralateral cerebral cortex, and through the dentate nucleus, plans and controls precise, dexterous, well-timed extremity movements. The spinocerebellum receives spinocerebellar tract input, not pontocerebellar, and lacks the dentate nucleus connection described. The vestibulocerebellum receives vestibular input, not pontocerebellar, and also does not use the dentate nucleus. Posture, balance, and equilibrium via the fastigial nucleus describes the vestibulocerebellum and portions of the spinocerebellum, not the cerebrocerebellum, which instead uses the dentate nucleus for fine limb control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cerebrocerebellum/pontocerebellum-dentate nucleus pairing for fine, dexterous limb movement is a classic three-zone comparison question. COMLEX: cerebrocerebellar dysfunction classically produces intention tremor and dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information traced from hair cells to the cerebellum, now the anatomic entry point for the vestibulocerebellar module","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cerebellar peduncle predominantly carries afferent fibers from the spinal cord and medulla, including the dorsal spinocerebellar and olivocerebellar fibers?","choices":{"A":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, the restiform and juxtarestiform bodies together","B":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, the brachium pontis","C":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, the brachium conjunctivum","D":"The anterior cerebellar peduncle, a structure not present in the human cerebellum","E":"The posterior cerebellar peduncle, a structure not present in the human cerebellum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, composed of the restiform and juxtarestiform bodies, carries dorsal spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, olivocerebellar, and vestibulocerebellar afferent fibers into the cerebellum. The middle cerebellar peduncle carries pontocerebellar fibers, not spinal or olivary afferents. The superior cerebellar peduncle is predominantly a cerebellar efferent pathway, not this afferent bundle. There is no anterior or posterior cerebellar peduncle in standard human neuroanatomy, only inferior, middle, and superior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each of the three true cerebellar peduncles to its predominant fiber content is essential exam anatomy. COMLEX: the inferior cerebellar peduncle's dual restiform/juxtarestiform composition explains why it carries both spinal and vestibular information.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the cerebellar pathways already traced through the pons, now organized by which peduncle carries them","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Most spinal input reaches the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle. Which spinocerebellar pathway instead enters the cerebellum in association with the superior cerebellar peduncle?","choices":{"A":"Anterior spinocerebellar tract","B":"Posterior spinocerebellar tract","C":"Cuneocerebellar tract","D":"Olivocerebellar tract","E":"Trigeminocerebellar tract"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anterior, or ventral, spinocerebellar fibers ascend to the rostral pons and enter the cerebellum along the superior cerebellar peduncle, along with rostral spinocerebellar fibers; they convey feedback about spinal interneuron activity. Posterior spinocerebellar and cuneocerebellar fibers enter through the restiform body, as do olivocerebellar climbing fibers and trigeminocerebellar fibers. The superior peduncle is otherwise dominated by efferent fibers from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inferior peduncle: posterior spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar; middle peduncle: pontocerebellar; superior peduncle: cerebellar efferents plus anterior spinocerebellar fibers. COMLEX: A superior peduncle lesion therefore affects both limb coordination output and some spinal input.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — the spinocerebellum and its spinocerebellar inputs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Fastigial nucleus neurons project bilaterally to the vestibular and reticular nuclei. The fibers destined for the opposite side cross within the cerebellar white matter and loop over the superior cerebellar peduncle before entering the brainstem. What is this crossing bundle called?","choices":{"A":"Decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle","B":"Medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Uncinate fasciculus of the cerebellum","D":"Lateral olfactory stria","E":"Ventral tegmental decussation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crossed fastigial efferents form the uncinate fasciculus, or hook bundle, as they loop over the superior cerebellar peduncle, then enter the contralateral vestibular complex through the juxtarestiform body; uncrossed fastigial fibers pass directly through the ipsilateral juxtarestiform body. The decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle in the caudal midbrain carries dentate, emboliform, and globose output. The medial longitudinal fasciculus links vestibular and ocular motor nuclei, the lateral olfactory stria is an olfactory pathway, and the ventral tegmental decussation carries rubrospinal fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fastigial output reaches vestibular and reticular nuclei via the juxtarestiform body, with crossed fibers in the uncinate fasciculus; fastigiovestibular fibers are excitatory, Purkinje corticovestibular fibers inhibitory. COMLEX: Midline vermal and fastigial lesions produce truncal ataxia, titubation, and nystagmus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular nuclei and their cerebellar connections","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do lesions of one cerebellar hemisphere typically produce motor deficits on the ipsilateral, rather than contralateral, side of the body?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar efferents cross once at the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation, then influence contralateral corticospinal and rubrospinal fibers that cross again before reaching the spinal cord","B":"Cerebellar efferents never cross the midline at any single point along their entire course, so the ipsilateral pattern simply reflects an entirely uncrossed pathway","C":"Cerebellar efferents cross three completely separate times before ever finally reaching the spinal cord itself, producing a net ipsilateral effect through this specific triple decussation process","D":"The cerebellum has no efferent output to the corticospinal or rubrospinal systems, so laterality is instead determined entirely by unrelated brainstem factors elsewhere","E":"Cerebellar lesions actually produce contralateral, not ipsilateral, deficits, so the ipsilateral rule is a common misconception"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cerebellar efferent fibers cross once at the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle to reach the contralateral red nucleus and thalamus/motor cortex, and these targets then send corticospinal and rubrospinal fibers that cross a second time before reaching the spinal cord, so the two crossings cancel out and produce a net ipsilateral effect relative to the original cerebellar lesion. The pathway does cross, once at the peduncle, so calling it entirely uncrossed is incorrect. There are two crossings, not three, involved in this net effect. The cerebellum clearly does have efferent output to these systems, contrary to the false claim of none. Cerebellar hemisphere lesions do genuinely produce ipsilateral, not contralateral, deficits, so this is not a misconception to correct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the double-decussation explanation for cerebellar ipsilaterality is one of the most conceptually important and frequently tested facts in this content area. COMLEX: this same double-crossing logic explains why a lesion distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation instead produces contralateral signs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the corticospinal decussation at the pyramids, now paired with a second cerebellar-efferent decussation to explain net ipsilaterality","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion occurs distal to the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle, within the midbrain, after cerebellar efferents have already crossed. On which side would resulting cerebellar-type signs appear?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral to the midbrain lesion, following the usual pattern of cerebellar hemisphere lesions","B":"Bilateral, since any midbrain lesion at this level affects crossed and uncrossed fibers equally","C":"There would be no cerebellar signs, since a midbrain lesion at this level cannot affect cerebellar efferent pathways","D":"The laterality is unpredictable and cannot be determined from any known anatomic principle","E":"Contralateral to the midbrain lesion, since the fibers have already crossed and still await their second crossing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A lesion beyond the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation interrupts efferent fibers that have already crossed from the opposite cerebellar hemisphere, so the resulting signs appear contralateral to the midbrain lesion, on the side of the cerebellar hemisphere of origin, because the second (rubrospinal/corticospinal) crossing is still to come. The usual ipsilateral pattern applies to lesions of the cerebellar hemisphere or proximal to the decussation, not to a lesion distal to it. A midbrain lesion at this point selectively affects already-crossed fibers rather than producing bilateral signs, it clearly can interrupt cerebellar efferents, and its laterality is predictable from where it sits relative to the decussation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing the contralateral exception for lesions distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation is a high-value nuance beyond the basic ipsilateral rule. COMLEX: this exception is occasionally tested with a midbrain lesion vignette that otherwise resembles a straightforward cerebellar case.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the corticospinal decussation at the pyramids, now paired with a second cerebellar-efferent decussation to explain net ipsilaterality","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do midline cerebellar lesions, involving the vermis or fastigial nucleus, produce bilateral truncal/axial deficits rather than a clear lateralized pattern?","choices":{"A":"Midline structures project exclusively to the contralateral side only, producing a purely contralateral, strictly lateralized deficit pattern instead of what is actually seen","B":"Midline structures project bilaterally through the vestibular and reticular nuclei to influence axial musculature on both sides, so a single midline lesion affects both sides","C":"Midline structures project exclusively to the ipsilateral side only, producing a purely ipsilateral, strictly lateralized deficit pattern instead of what is actually seen","D":"Midline lesions actually never produce any detectable clinical deficit, regardless of the specific structures involved in the lesion itself","E":"Midline lesions produce deficits only in cranial nerve function specifically, entirely sparing the axial trunk and limb musculature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The fastigial nucleus and vermal cortex project bilaterally to the vestibular and reticular nuclei, which in turn influence axial and truncal musculature on both sides of the body, so a single midline lesion produces bilateral, non-lateralized truncal ataxia rather than a one-sided pattern. Exclusive contralateral-only projection would produce a lateralized deficit, contradicting the bilateral truncal ataxia actually seen with midline lesions. Exclusive ipsilateral-only projection would likewise produce a lateralized rather than bilateral pattern. Midline lesions very much produce detectable deficits, truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, and titubation are all well-documented findings. Midline lesions produce prominent truncal and gait findings, not merely isolated cranial nerve deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral fastigial/vestibular projections explaining truncal ataxia without lateralization is the key exception that completes the ipsilaterality framework for this objective. COMLEX: a wide-based, lurching gait without clear one-sided limb findings should raise suspicion for a midline, rather than hemispheric, cerebellar lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the corticospinal decussation at the pyramids, now paired with a second cerebellar-efferent decussation to explain net ipsilaterality","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient sways markedly when standing with eyes closed but is relatively steady with eyes open. Which type of ataxia does this Romberg-positive pattern suggest?","choices":{"A":"Sensory ataxia from impaired proprioceptive input, since visual input can compensate for lost proprioception only when the eyes are open","B":"Cerebellar ataxia, since cerebellar lesions classically produce a strongly Romberg-positive pattern that worsens dramatically with eyes closed","C":"Sensory ataxia, but the underlying mechanism is unrelated to proprioception and instead reflects a primary visual processing deficit","D":"Cerebellar ataxia from a vermal lesion, since vermal lesions are known to produce visually dependent instability","E":"Neither cerebellar nor sensory ataxia, since Romberg testing cannot meaningfully distinguish between any of these categories of instability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A positive Romberg sign, marked worsening of balance specifically with eyes closed, indicates sensory ataxia from impaired proprioceptive/dorsal column input, since the patient was relying on vision to compensate for lost position sense and loses that compensation once the eyes close. Cerebellar ataxia is classically Romberg-negative, or only mildly worse with eyes closed, because the instability is present with eyes open as well and does not depend heavily on visual compensation. This sensory ataxia pattern is specifically proprioceptive in origin, not a primary visual processing problem. Vermal cerebellar lesions produce a Romberg-negative pattern with instability present regardless of eye closure, not this visual-dependent pattern. Romberg testing is specifically useful and validated for distinguishing sensory from cerebellar ataxia, contrary to the claim that it cannot.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romberg-positive equals sensory ataxia (proprioceptive/dorsal column loss), Romberg-negative equals cerebellar ataxia, is one of the most reliably tested bedside distinctions in neurology. COMLEX: always test Romberg with the patient's feet together and be ready to catch a fall, since sensory ataxia patients can sway dramatically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — Romberg testing already built for dorsal column disease, now used to separate sensory from cerebellar ataxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is unsteady and wide-based whether the eyes are open or closed, with no significant change in stability when vision is removed. Which type of ataxia does this Romberg-negative pattern suggest?","choices":{"A":"Sensory ataxia, since sensory ataxia is classically Romberg-negative and shows no meaningful change when the eyes are closed","B":"Cerebellar ataxia, but only if the lesion is confined strictly to the lateral hemisphere and never involves the vermis","C":"Sensory ataxia from a lesion of the anterolateral system rather than the dorsal columns, producing this specific visual-independent pattern","D":"Cerebellar ataxia, since the instability arises from a coordination deficit that is independent of visual compensation","E":"A normal, physiologic finding with no diagnostic significance, since some baseline unsteadiness is expected in all patients tested this way"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cerebellar ataxia produces incoordination that is present regardless of visual input, since the deficit lies in cerebellar processing of movement rather than in proprioceptive sensory loss, so removing vision does not meaningfully worsen the instability, a Romberg-negative pattern. Sensory ataxia is classically Romberg-positive, worsening substantially with eyes closed, the opposite of what is described here. Sensory ataxia specifically results from dorsal column, not anterolateral system, proprioceptive loss, and the anterolateral system carries pain and temperature, not proprioception. Cerebellar ataxia with this Romberg-negative pattern can arise from vermal, hemispheric, or combined lesions, not exclusively the lateral hemisphere. This finding, unsteadiness independent of vision, is a genuine, clinically significant abnormal finding, not a normal variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romberg-negative instability strongly favors cerebellar over sensory ataxia, the mirror image of the Romberg-positive rule. COMLEX: pair Romberg testing with finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing to further localize cerebellar involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — Romberg testing already built for dorsal column disease, now used to separate sensory from cerebellar ataxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Beyond the Romberg test itself, which additional bedside finding would best support a diagnosis of sensory, rather than cerebellar, ataxia?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intention tremor and dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing that is present and unchanged regardless of whether the eyes are open or closed","B":"Dysdiadochokinesia on rapid alternating movement testing, present and unchanged whether the patient's eyes are open or closed during testing","C":"A wide-based, unsteady gait that is equally severe with eyes open and eyes closed, with no meaningful visual dependence noted","D":"Impaired joint position sense and vibration sense on formal proprioceptive testing, with normal finger-to-nose accuracy when vision is available","E":"Scanning, staccato dysarthria with normal grammar and word use, a finding that is also independent of whether the eyes are open or closed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory ataxia is characterized by impaired proprioception, joint position and vibration sense, that can be directly demonstrated on formal testing, and importantly, finger-to-nose accuracy remains relatively normal when vision compensates for the lost proprioceptive input, distinguishing it from true cerebellar dysmetria. Intention tremor and dysmetria present regardless of vision are cerebellar findings, not sensory ataxia findings, since cerebellar dysmetria does not depend on visual compensation the way sensory ataxia does. Dysdiadochokinesia unaffected by vision is likewise a cerebellar, not sensory ataxia, finding. A gait that is equally unsteady with eyes open and closed describes the vision-independent pattern of cerebellar, not sensory, ataxia. Scanning dysarthria unaffected by vision is a cerebellar finding, not part of the sensory ataxia examination picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pairing formal proprioceptive testing, vibration and joint position sense, with the Romberg test builds a complete case for sensory ataxia. COMLEX: dorsal column disease such as subacute combined degeneration or tabes dorsalis classically produces this exact sensory ataxia pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — Romberg testing already built for dorsal column disease, now used to separate sensory from cerebellar ataxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion is confined to the flocculonodular lobe. Which clinical picture is most characteristic of this specific lesion location?","choices":{"A":"Isolated distal limb dysmetria and intention tremor, with a completely normal, steady gait and no nystagmus present","B":"Contralateral spastic hemiparesis with an extensor plantar response, entirely sparing coordination and balance testing","C":"Dysarthria and facial weakness alone, with entirely normal limb coordination and completely normal gait testing","D":"Pure sensory loss to pain and temperature on the contralateral body, with entirely normal motor and coordination findings","E":"Truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, titubation, and nystagmus, with relatively preserved limb coordination"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The flocculonodular lobe is the vestibulocerebellum, so its lesions produce truncal ataxia, a wide-based lurching gait, titubation, and nystagmus, while limb coordination, governed by other zones, remains relatively preserved. Isolated distal limb dysmetria with a normal gait describes a lateral hemisphere lesion, not a flocculonodular one. Contralateral spastic hemiparesis with an extensor plantar response describes a corticospinal tract lesion, not a cerebellar lesion at all. Isolated dysarthria and facial weakness without coordination or gait findings does not fit any classic cerebellar lesion pattern. Pure contralateral pain/temperature loss describes an anterolateral system lesion, unrelated to cerebellar pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: flocculonodular lesions producing truncal ataxia and nystagmus while sparing limb coordination is a classic localizing pattern. COMLEX: this pattern is often tested alongside pediatric medulloblastoma, which classically arises in this location.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the same five cerebellar lesion sites revisited here with sharper vermis/hemisphere/nuclear distinctions and board-style framing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion is confined to the lateral cerebellar hemisphere. Which clinical picture is most characteristic of this specific lesion location?","choices":{"A":"Prominent truncal ataxia and titubation with a wide-based gait, sparing limb coordination and finger-to-nose testing almost entirely throughout","B":"Bilateral truncal instability and nystagmus, with entirely normal, symmetric limb coordination noted on both sides of the body when examined","C":"Ipsilateral limb dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, and hypotonia, with a relatively stable gait apart from lateral drift toward the lesion side","D":"Contralateral limb dysmetria and intention tremor, with an entirely normal gait and no lateral drift found on formal tandem walking testing","E":"Isolated vertigo and nystagmus with no detectable limb or gait abnormality on any single component of the entire, complete, full neurologic examination performed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lateral cerebellar hemisphere lesions produce ipsilateral limb dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, and hypotonia, with a gait that shows lateral drift or a tendency to fall toward the lesion side but is not as dramatically wide-based as with midline lesions. Prominent truncal ataxia sparing limb coordination describes a flocculonodular or vermal lesion, not a lateral hemisphere lesion. Bilateral truncal instability with normal limb coordination also describes a midline, not lateral, lesion pattern. Contralateral, rather than ipsilateral, limb dysmetria would be atypical for a straightforward lateral hemisphere lesion given the standard ipsilateral rule. Isolated vertigo and nystagmus without any limb or gait finding describes a vestibulocerebellar, not lateral hemisphere, presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lateral hemisphere lesions producing ipsilateral limb signs with relatively preserved axial stability completes the three-way vermis/hemisphere/flocculonodular comparison. COMLEX: this pattern is the most common presentation tested for isolated cerebellar stroke or tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the same five cerebellar lesion sites revisited here with sharper vermis/hemisphere/nuclear distinctions and board-style framing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion involves the cerebellar cortex plus the underlying dentate nucleus on one side. How does this combined cortex-plus-nucleus lesion differ from an equivalent cortex-only lesion?","choices":{"A":"The combined lesion produces identical severity and duration of deficits compared to an equivalent cortex-only lesion in the same exact location","B":"The combined lesion produces more severe and more persistent deficits, since nuclear output, not just cortical modulation, is directly disrupted","C":"The combined lesion produces milder deficits than a cortex-only lesion, since the nucleus partially compensates for the cortical loss","D":"The combined lesion produces deficits only in visceral, autonomic function, sparing all somatic motor coordination almost entirely from involvement","E":"The combined lesion produces no additional deficit beyond what a cortex-only lesion produces, since the nucleus has no independent function"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because most cerebellar output ultimately funnels through the deep nuclei, a lesion that includes the dentate nucleus, not just overlying cortex, directly disrupts nuclear output itself, producing more severe and longer-lasting motor deficits than a cortex-only lesion, which leaves nuclear output relatively intact. The two lesion types do not produce identical severity, that is precisely the distinction this objective and the broader chapter emphasize. The nucleus does not partially compensate for cortical loss, if anything, losing the nucleus removes the very output pathway the cortex depends on. Combined cortex-plus-nucleus lesions produce somatic motor coordination deficits as their primary feature, not isolated visceral effects. The nucleus clearly has an essential independent function as the final common output pathway, so its involvement is not inconsequential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cortex-only versus cortex-plus-nuclei severity distinction is a foundational concept that should be applied to every cerebellar lesion vignette. COMLEX: always ask whether imaging shows nuclear involvement when a cerebellar deficit seems unexpectedly severe or persistent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the same five cerebellar lesion sites revisited here with sharper vermis/hemisphere/nuclear distinctions and board-style framing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) most characteristically produces which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Lateral medullary/Wallenberg syndrome findings plus truncal/vermal cerebellar ataxia, from combined posterolateral medullary and caudomedial cerebellar territory loss","B":"Isolated lateral pontine syndrome findings with facial weakness and ipsilateral limb ataxia, sparing medullary structures","C":"Isolated severe, persistent deep-nuclear cerebellar signs plus contralateral body pain/temperature loss, sparing the medulla","D":"Isolated contralateral spastic hemiparesis from corticospinal tract involvement, sparing cerebellar and brainstem sensory structures","E":"Isolated bilateral vestibular hypofunction with no accompanying cranial nerve, sensory, or cerebellar findings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PICA supplies the posterolateral medulla, producing lateral medullary/Wallenberg syndrome findings such as dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral facial and contralateral body pain/temperature loss, and Horner syndrome, and also supplies the caudomedial inferior cerebellar surface including the vermis, adding truncal and gait ataxia to the presentation. Isolated lateral pontine syndrome findings describe AICA, not PICA, territory. Isolated severe deep-nuclear cerebellar signs with contralateral pain/temperature loss describes SCA territory, not PICA. Isolated contralateral spastic hemiparesis describes a corticospinal, not PICA cerebellar/medullary, territory infarct. Isolated bilateral vestibular hypofunction is not a vascular syndrome pattern at all and does not correspond to PICA territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PICA occlusion producing combined lateral medullary syndrome plus vermal/truncal cerebellar ataxia links two previously separate topics into one vignette. COMLEX: \"don't pick a PICA lame horse that can't eat\" is the classic mnemonic for the PICA/Wallenberg symptom cluster.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 7 — AICA and SCA syndromes already introduced, now joined by PICA and organized by full vascular territory logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) most characteristically produces which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Lateral medullary/Wallenberg syndrome findings plus truncal/vermal cerebellar ataxia, from combined posterolateral medullary and caudomedial cerebellar territory loss","B":"Isolated severe, persistent deep-nuclear cerebellar signs plus contralateral body pain/temperature loss and ipsilateral Horner syndrome","C":"Pure motor hemiparesis without any sensory, cranial nerve, or cerebellar findings on neurologic examination","D":"Lateral pontine syndrome findings, including ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, plus caudolateral cerebellar and middle peduncle involvement","E":"Bilateral, symmetric cerebellar ataxia affecting the vermis and both hemispheres equally, with no lateralizing findings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AICA supplies the lateral pons, including facial and vestibulocochlear nerve fascicles, producing lateral pontine syndrome with ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, and also supplies the caudolateral inferior cerebellar surface and caudal middle cerebellar peduncle, adding cerebellar signs to the presentation. Lateral medullary/Wallenberg syndrome plus vermal ataxia describes PICA, not AICA, territory. Isolated severe deep-nuclear cerebellar signs with contralateral pain/temperature loss and Horner syndrome describes SCA territory, not AICA. Pure motor hemiparesis without any other findings describes a corticospinal territory infarct, not this brainstem/cerebellar vascular syndrome. Bilateral, symmetric cerebellar ataxia is not the expected pattern from a unilateral AICA occlusion, which produces lateralized findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AICA occlusion producing lateral pontine syndrome with facial and hearing involvement, distinct from PICA's medullary picture, is a classic three-vessel comparison point. COMLEX: facial nerve palsy plus ipsilateral hearing loss should raise suspicion for AICA territory rather than a peripheral Bell palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 7 — AICA and SCA syndromes already introduced, now joined by PICA and organized by full vascular territory logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the caudal part of the middle cerebellar peduncle, the choroid plexus extending out of the foramen of Luschka, and the caudolateral inferior surface of the cerebellum?","choices":{"A":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"Superior cerebellar artery","D":"Posterior cerebral artery","E":"Anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery serves the caudal middle cerebellar peduncle, the choroid plexus protruding from the foramen of Luschka, and the caudolateral inferior surface, and may supply small caudal parts of the dentate nucleus. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the posterolateral medulla including the restiform body, the choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle, and the caudomedial inferior surface including the vermis. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the whole superior surface, most of the cerebellar nuclei, the rostral middle peduncle, and the superior peduncle. The posterior cerebral and anterior spinal arteries do not supply the cerebellum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PICA from the vertebral artery; AICA and SCA from the basilar artery. COMLEX: AICA infarcts add ipsilateral hearing loss and facial weakness to limb ataxia because the artery also supplies the lateral pons and labyrinth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 7 — AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA patterns involving the pons and cerebellum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with ataxia. Using a stepwise localization approach, what is the first branch point question to ask before considering specific cerebellar zones or vessels?","choices":{"A":"Is the deficit an upper motor neuron pattern or a lower motor neuron pattern on formal strength testing?","B":"Is the patient's cranial nerve examination entirely normal or entirely abnormal?","C":"Is there a clear sensory level present on the trunk consistent with a discrete spinal cord lesion at that level?","D":"Is this a Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia or a Romberg-positive sensory ataxia?","E":"Is the patient's mental status entirely normal or entirely abnormal on formal cognitive screening at the bedside?"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first branch point in any ataxia vignette is distinguishing cerebellar (Romberg-negative) from sensory (Romberg-positive) ataxia, since this immediately separates a cerebellar circuit problem from a dorsal column/peripheral proprioceptive problem before any further cerebellar zone or vascular territory localization is attempted. UMN versus LMN pattern testing is not the discriminating first step for an ataxia vignette specifically. Cranial nerve findings can add localizing detail later but are not the very first branch point in an ataxia-focused vignette. A sensory level points toward spinal cord pathology and is not the first branch point when ataxia, not weakness or numbness, is the presenting complaint. Mental status is not the discriminating first step for localizing ataxia specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: starting every ataxia vignette with the cerebellar-versus-sensory branch point via Romberg testing rapidly narrows the differential before considering specific zones or vessels. COMLEX: apply this same stepwise logic before moving on to vermis-versus-hemisphere-versus-flocculonodular localization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 10 — the stepwise localization algorithm already built for basal nuclei movement disorders, now adapted to cerebellar sign vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has confirmed Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia. What is the appropriate second branch point question in the stepwise localization algorithm?","choices":{"A":"Is the onset of symptoms acute, subacute, or chronic in nature, since this question should always be asked before the truncal-versus-limb distinction is made","B":"Are the findings truncal/gait-predominant, suggesting a midline lesion, or limb-predominant with relative gait preservation, suggesting a hemispheric lesion?","C":"Is there a family history of a similar movement disorder present, since genetic causes should always be excluded before considering zone localization","D":"Is the patient currently taking any new medications, since medication review should always precede any anatomic localization step taken in this algorithm","E":"Is there an associated psychiatric symptom, since psychiatric comorbidity should be assessed before anatomic localization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After confirming cerebellar ataxia, the next branch point is distinguishing truncal/gait-predominant findings, suggesting a midline vermal or flocculonodular lesion, from limb-predominant findings with relatively preserved gait, suggesting a lateral hemisphere lesion, since this directly guides zone-specific localization. Onset timing is a useful later step but is not the standard second branch point immediately following the cerebellar-versus-sensory distinction in this specific algorithm. Family history is relevant for certain chronic ataxias but is not the mandatory second step before basic anatomic zone localization. Medication review is important clinically but is not the anatomic second branch point in this specific stepwise framework. Psychiatric symptoms are not part of the core anatomic localization algorithm for cerebellar ataxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the truncal-versus-limb-predominant branch point is the anatomic backbone connecting Romberg testing to specific cerebellar zone localization. COMLEX: this second step should be completed before moving on to vascular territory or etiology considerations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 10 — the stepwise localization algorithm already built for basal nuclei movement disorders, now adapted to cerebellar sign vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has confirmed limb-predominant cerebellar ataxia with relatively preserved gait, localizing to the lateral hemisphere. What is the appropriate next step to complete the stepwise localization?","choices":{"A":"Immediately conclude the entire workup is complete, since zone localization alone is always sufficient to finalize both the diagnosis and its etiology","B":"Assume a vascular cause exclusively, since lateral hemisphere lesions always result from vascular etiologies","C":"Determine whether the time course and associated findings fit a vascular PICA/AICA/SCA, structural, or degenerative etiology","D":"Assume a degenerative cause exclusively, since lateral hemisphere lesions always result from degenerative etiologies","E":"Order genetic testing immediately without any further clinical correlation, since genetic causes are by far the single most common etiology"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After zone localization, the stepwise algorithm requires correlating the time course, acute favoring vascular, subacute/structural favoring tumor or demyelination, and chronic favoring degenerative, with associated findings to narrow the etiology, since zone localization alone identifies where but not why the deficit occurred. Concluding the workup is complete after zone localization skips this essential etiologic step. Lateral hemisphere lesions can result from vascular, structural, degenerative, or other causes, not exclusively vascular ones. They likewise are not exclusively degenerative, vascular and structural causes are equally plausible depending on the clinical context. Ordering genetic testing immediately without correlating the clinical time course and findings skips necessary clinical reasoning steps and is not justified by genetic causes being the most common etiology overall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: completing localization requires pairing anatomic zone with time course and associated findings to reach a specific etiology, not stopping at anatomy alone. COMLEX: this final step is where PICA/AICA/SCA vascular pattern recognition becomes clinically actionable in a board vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 10 — the stepwise localization algorithm already built for basal nuclei movement disorders, now adapted to cerebellar sign vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":2,"stem":"44-year-old man -> presents 2 days after a small cerebellar cortical infarct confirmed on MRI, sparing the deep nuclei -> exam shows mild finger-to-nose dysmetria on the affected side but normal strength throughout, including 5/5 on formal manual muscle testing. Which principle best explains the normal strength despite the confirmed infarct?","choices":{"A":"The patient is voluntarily masking true weakness during the examination, a phenomenon common after any newly diagnosed cerebral or cerebellar infarct","B":"The infarct is too small to be clinically significant, and larger cortical infarcts would be expected to produce measurable weakness","C":"The cerebellar cortex modulates the coordination of movement rather than generating motor commands, so a cortex-only lesion does not reduce muscle strength at all","D":"Cerebellar cortical infarcts always spare the corticospinal tract anatomically, which is the only reason strength can ever be preserved in any patient","E":"The deep cerebellar nuclei have already fully compensated for the cortical loss within just 2 days, restoring completely normal function throughout the nucleus itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the cerebellar cortex's role is coordination and error correction rather than motor command generation, a lesion confined to the cortex, sparing the nuclei, produces incoordination without measurable weakness, exactly this patient's findings. Infarct size is not the reason strength is preserved, even larger cortex-only infarcts would be expected to spare strength by this same principle. There is no expectation or mechanism for voluntary weakness-masking as a standard post-infarct phenomenon in this context. The preservation of strength is not simply because the corticospinal tract is anatomically distant, it is because the cerebellar cortex does not directly generate motor commands regardless of anatomic proximity. Rapid full compensation by the nuclei within 2 days is not the explanation, the nuclei were never damaged in the first place since the lesion was cortex-only.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a cortex-only cerebellar infarct with dysmetria but normal strength is a classic vignette testing the coordination-versus-command distinction. COMLEX: always confirm imaging extent, cortex alone versus cortex-plus-nuclei, to predict expected deficit severity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — This same cortex-versus-nucleus distinction predicts why a nuclear infarct here would have produced more severe, persistent deficits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":2,"stem":"30-year-old woman -> healthy volunteer in a motor control research study -> reaches for a target while wearing prism goggles that shift her visual field -> initial reaches are inaccurate, but accuracy improves rapidly over repeated trials as her cerebellum adapts the motor output. Which cerebellar function does this experiment best demonstrate?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum's role in generating the initial motor command for reaching toward the target","B":"The cerebellum's error-correction role, continuously comparing intended and actual movement to adaptively refine motor output over successive trials","C":"The cerebellum's role in permanently storing a single fixed reaching program that never changes regardless of any visual feedback provided","D":"The cerebral cortex's exclusive role in this adaptation, with the cerebellum contributing essentially nothing measurable to the improvement observed","E":"A purely spinal reflex phenomenon unrelated to any supraspinal structure, including the cerebellum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This prism adaptation paradigm classically demonstrates the cerebellum's error-correction function, it compares the intended reach trajectory to the visually perceived, prism-shifted outcome and progressively adjusts motor output across trials until accuracy improves, a hallmark of cerebellar-dependent motor learning. The cerebellum does not generate the initial motor command for reaching, that originates in motor planning and primary motor cortex, though the cerebellum still contributes to refining it. The cerebellum does not store one permanently fixed program, its output is dynamically adaptive, which is exactly what allows the improvement seen here. The cerebellum, not the cerebral cortex alone, is specifically implicated in this type of visuomotor adaptation, contrary to the claim of no cerebellar contribution. This is a well-established supraspinal, cerebellum-dependent phenomenon, not a purely spinal reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prism adaptation is a classic experimental paradigm used to demonstrate cerebellar motor learning and error correction. COMLEX: patients with cerebellar lesions show impaired prism adaptation, a research and clinical correlate of this same principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — This adaptive error-correction function is distributed across the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum depending on the movement type being learned","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":2,"stem":"52-year-old man -> presents with progressive gait unsteadiness -> neurologist explains that the cerebellum's role is fundamentally different from that of the primary motor cortex. The patient asks for a simple analogy. Which analogy best captures this distinction accurately?","choices":{"A":"The motor cortex and cerebellum are like two identical drivers taking turns steering the same car, with no functional difference between them","B":"The motor cortex is like the car's engine providing raw power, while the cerebellum is like the car's fuel tank simply storing energy for later use only","C":"The motor cortex is like a driver steering the car, while the cerebellum is like a skilled co-pilot continuously fine-tuning the steering based on how the car is actually moving","D":"The motor cortex has no role in steering the car, and the cerebellum alone is responsible for every aspect of the car's movement","E":"The cerebellum is like a passenger with no influence on the car's movement, while the motor cortex alone does all of the driving"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The motor cortex initiates and directs voluntary movement, like a driver steering, while the cerebellum continuously monitors the movement's actual execution and makes real-time corrective adjustments, like a co-pilot fine-tuning the steering, capturing the coordination and error-correction role without implying the cerebellum generates the movement itself. Describing them as identical, interchangeable drivers misses the fundamentally different roles each structure plays. The engine/fuel tank analogy does not capture the coordination and feedback relationship that defines cerebellar function. Claiming the motor cortex has no role at all contradicts its clear role in initiating voluntary movement. Describing the cerebellum as having no influence contradicts its well-established role in refining ongoing movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: using an analogy that preserves the initiator-versus-refiner distinction, rather than implying redundancy or reversal, is a useful way to solidify this concept for exam recall. COMLEX: this same distinction is often explained to patients using simpler language when discussing cerebellar disease prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 4 — This refining role is precisely why cerebellar hemisphere damage produces ipsilateral incoordination rather than the contralateral paralysis seen with motor cortex or corticospinal tract damage","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"68-year-old woman -> presents with a lurching, wide-based gait, unable to walk heel-to-toe, with a tendency to fall backward, nystagmus, and episodic vertigo -> finger-to-nose testing is entirely normal bilaterally, and there is no dysarthria. Which cerebellar functional zone is most likely involved, and what is the input source that defines it?","choices":{"A":"The vestibulocerebellum, defined by predominant input from the ipsilateral vestibular ganglion and vestibular nuclei","B":"The spinocerebellum, defined by predominant input from the posterior and anterior spinocerebellar tracts and cuneocerebellar fibers","C":"The cerebrocerebellum, defined by predominant input from crossed pontocerebellar fibers relaying contralateral cerebral cortex signals","D":"The vestibulocerebellum, but defined instead by predominant input from the posterior spinocerebellar tract rather than vestibular afferents","E":"The spinocerebellum, but defined instead by predominant input from crossed pontocerebellar fibers rather than spinal afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient's isolated truncal/gait ataxia with nystagmus, vertigo, entirely preserved limb coordination, and no dysarthria is the classic vestibulocerebellar pattern, and the vestibulocerebellum is specifically defined by its predominant ipsilateral vestibular ganglion and nuclei input. The spinocerebellum, defined by spinocerebellar and cuneocerebellar input, would not explain the nystagmus and vertigo, which point to the flocculonodular lobe. The cerebrocerebellum, defined by pontocerebellar input, governs fine limb movement and would produce limb dysmetria, which is absent in this patient. The vestibulocerebellum is specifically defined by vestibular, not spinocerebellar tract, input, so that pairing is incorrect. The spinocerebellum is defined by spinal, not pontocerebellar, input, so that pairing is also incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isolated gait/truncal ataxia with nystagmus, spared limb coordination, and no dysarthria should suggest vestibulocerebellar, flocculonodular involvement. COMLEX: this pattern is classically associated with midline cerebellar tumors such as medulloblastoma in pediatric patients, though this vignette describes an adult presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — This same input-defined vestibulocerebellum corresponds anatomically to the flocculonodular lobe lesion pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"55-year-old man -> presents with slow, imprecise reaching movements of the right hand, overshooting targets, with a mild kinetic tremor that worsens as his finger approaches his nose -> gait is essentially normal, and there is no vertigo or nystagmus. Which cerebellar functional zone is most likely involved, and what is its output pathway?","choices":{"A":"The vestibulocerebellum, whose output travels via the dentate nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral thalamus and motor cortex","B":"The spinocerebellum, whose output travels via the dentate nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral thalamus and motor cortex","C":"The cerebrocerebellum, whose output travels via the dentate nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral thalamus and motor cortex","D":"The cerebrocerebellum, whose output travels via the fastigial nucleus through the juxtarestiform body to the bilateral vestibular nuclei","E":"The vestibulocerebellum, whose output travels via the fastigial nucleus through the juxtarestiform body to the bilateral vestibular nuclei"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient's isolated distal limb dysmetria and intention tremor with a normal gait and no vestibular symptoms is the classic cerebrocerebellar (pontocerebellar) pattern, and this zone's output travels from the dentate nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral VLpc thalamus and motor cortex, ultimately influencing distal limb musculature. The vestibulocerebellum does not use the dentate nucleus/superior peduncle output route described, and its lesions produce truncal, not distal limb, findings. The spinocerebellum's intermediate zone does partly use the dentate's neighboring interposed nuclei, but the classic dentate/superior-peduncle/fine-limb-timing pathway described here is specifically cerebrocerebellar. The cerebrocerebellum does not use the fastigial nucleus/juxtarestiform body output route, that describes the vestibulocerebellum instead. The vestibulocerebellum does correctly use the fastigial/juxtarestiform route, but its clinical picture would be truncal ataxia and nystagmus, not this isolated limb dysmetria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal limb dysmetria and intention tremor with a normal gait localizes cleanly to the cerebrocerebellum/dentate nucleus pathway. COMLEX: this pattern is the most frequently tested isolated cerebellar hemisphere presentation on board examinations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — This same dentate-to-superior-cerebellar-peduncle output route is the specific peduncle pathway carrying this zone's efferent signal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"60-year-old man -> presents with a wide-based, unsteady gait and difficulty maintaining truncal posture, but with relatively preserved fine finger movements and no nystagmus or vertigo -> exam shows mild hypotonia in the lower extremities bilaterally. Which cerebellar functional zone is most likely involved, and what output structure controls the axial findings specifically?","choices":{"A":"The vestibulocerebellum, whose axial output is controlled through the fastigial nucleus and its vestibular/reticular projections","B":"The cerebrocerebellum, whose axial output is controlled through the fastigial nucleus and its vestibular/reticular projections","C":"The spinocerebellar vermal zone, but with axial output controlled instead through the dentate nucleus and its thalamic projections","D":"The spinocerebellar vermal zone, whose axial output is controlled through the fastigial nucleus and its vestibular/reticular projections","E":"The spinocerebellar intermediate zone specifically, whose axial output is controlled instead through the emboliform and globose nuclei alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient's gait/truncal instability with preserved fine finger movements and no vestibular symptoms fits the spinocerebellar vermal pattern, whose axial musculature control is mediated through the fastigial nucleus and its bilateral vestibular and reticular projections, similar in output route to the vestibulocerebellum but driven by spinal, not vestibular, afferent input. The vestibulocerebellum does share the fastigial output route but would be expected to also produce nystagmus and vertigo, which are absent here, making it a less precise fit than the spinocerebellar vermal zone given the described input pattern. The cerebrocerebellum does not use the fastigial nucleus at all, it uses the dentate nucleus, and it governs fine distal limb movement, not axial control. The spinocerebellar vermal zone's axial output specifically uses the fastigial nucleus, not the dentate nucleus, so that pairing is incorrect. The intermediate zone's emboliform and globose nuclei control limb, not primarily axial, musculature, making that description less accurate for this truncal-predominant presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that both the vestibulocerebellum and spinocerebellar vermal zone share the fastigial nucleus output route, but differ in afferent source and associated findings, is a nuanced but testable nuance. COMLEX: hypotonia is a recognized but less emphasized finding in spinocerebellar lesions, worth remembering alongside the more classic ataxia findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — This vermal, fastigial-nucleus-mediated pattern corresponds to the midline lesion category associated with bilateral, non-lateralized truncal deficits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"47-year-old woman -> undergoes MRI for unrelated headache -> incidental finding of a small lesion isolated to the restiform body on one side, with no other structures involved -> she is entirely asymptomatic. Which category of inferior-peduncle fibers would most likely be spared by a lesion confined to the restiform body?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal spinocerebellar fibers, which travel through the restiform body itself and would be expected to be directly affected by this lesion","B":"Vestibulocerebellar fibers, which travel instead through the juxtarestiform body, a distinct component of the inferior cerebellar peduncle","C":"Olivocerebellar fibers, which travel through the restiform body itself and would be expected to be directly affected by this lesion","D":"Cuneocerebellar fibers, which travel through the restiform body itself and would be expected to be directly affected by this lesion","E":"Trigeminocerebellar fibers, which travel through the restiform body itself and would be expected to be directly affected by this lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The restiform body and juxtarestiform body are distinct components of the inferior cerebellar peduncle, the restiform body carries dorsal spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, olivocerebellar, and trigeminocerebellar fibers, while vestibulocerebellar fibers specifically travel through the separate juxtarestiform body, so a lesion isolated to the restiform body would spare vestibular input. Dorsal spinocerebellar fibers do travel through the restiform body and would be directly affected, not spared, by this lesion. Olivocerebellar fibers likewise travel through the restiform body and would be affected. Cuneocerebellar fibers also travel through the restiform body and would be affected. Trigeminocerebellar fibers travel through the restiform body as well and would be affected, not spared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing the restiform body from the juxtarestiform body within the inferior cerebellar peduncle is a detailed but testable anatomic nuance. COMLEX: incidental small peduncle lesions with minimal symptoms are a realistic clinical scenario worth understanding anatomically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — The juxtarestiform body specifically carries the vestibulocerebellar input that defines this functional zone","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"35-year-old man -> undergoes elective surgery for a midbrain lesion near the level of the inferior colliculus -> surgeon must carefully identify and avoid the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle during the approach. Damage to this specific decussation would be expected to disrupt which category of fibers?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar afferent fibers from the contralateral basilar pontine nuclei entering the cerebellum via the middle cerebellar peduncle","B":"Cerebellar afferent fibers from the spinal cord and medulla entering the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle restiform body","C":"Vestibulocerebellar afferent fibers entering the cerebellum via the juxtarestiform body component of the inferior cerebellar peduncle","D":"Cerebellar efferent fibers from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei en route to the contralateral thalamus and red nucleus","E":"Corticospinal fibers descending from the motor cortex, which also decussate at this midbrain level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle, located in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus, carries cerebellar efferent fibers from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei traveling toward the contralateral thalamus and red nucleus, so damage here disrupts this specific efferent outflow. Pontocerebellar afferent fibers travel through the middle, not superior, cerebellar peduncle and are unrelated to this decussation. Spinal/medullary afferent fibers travel through the inferior cerebellar peduncle restiform body, a different structure entirely from the superior peduncle decussation. Vestibulocerebellar afferents travel through the juxtarestiform body, also part of the inferior, not superior, peduncle. Corticospinal fibers decussate at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, an entirely different location from the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation in the midbrain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: precisely localizing the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation to the midbrain, carrying efferent, not afferent, fibers, is a detail surgeons and exam writers both care about. COMLEX: this decussation's location is the anatomic basis for the ipsilateral-versus-contralateral lesion distinction covered under a separate objective.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 4 — Damage at this exact decussation site produces the contralateral exception to the usual ipsilateral cerebellar lesion rule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"58-year-old woman -> presents with new-onset limb ataxia and imaging shows a lesion confined to the middle cerebellar peduncle on one side -> no vestibular, spinal sensory, or truncal findings are present. Which pathway's disruption best explains this relatively isolated presentation?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal spinocerebellar fibers relaying proprioceptive limb position information through the restiform body into the cerebellum","B":"Vestibulocerebellar fibers relaying head position and balance information through the juxtarestiform body into the cerebellum","C":"Pontocerebellar fibers relaying contralateral cerebral cortex signals through the basilar pons into the cerebellum","D":"Superior cerebellar peduncle efferent fibers relaying cerebellar nuclear output toward the contralateral thalamus and red nucleus","E":"Trigeminocerebellar fibers relaying facial proprioceptive and mechanoreceptive information through the restiform body into the cerebellum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A lesion confined to the middle cerebellar peduncle disrupts pontocerebellar fibers, which relay cortically derived movement-planning information through the basilar pons into the cerebellum, correlating with the cerebrocerebellar limb ataxia described while sparing the vestibular, spinal, and truncal functions carried by the separate inferior and superior peduncles. Dorsal spinocerebellar fibers travel through the restiform body of the inferior, not middle, peduncle and would more likely produce spinal-pattern rather than isolated pontocerebellar findings if affected. Vestibulocerebellar fibers travel through the juxtarestiform body of the inferior peduncle, and their involvement would be expected to produce truncal/vestibular findings, which are absent here. Superior cerebellar peduncle efferent fibers are a distinct structure from the middle peduncle and their involvement would affect nuclear outflow broadly, not this relatively isolated pontocerebellar-type presentation. Trigeminocerebellar fibers also travel through the restiform body, not the middle peduncle, making them an unlikely explanation for this specific lesion location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a middle cerebellar peduncle lesion isolated to pontocerebellar fibers producing limb ataxia without vestibular or truncal findings tests precise peduncle-to-symptom correlation. COMLEX: middle cerebellar peduncle lesions are also classically associated with certain hereditary ataxias and fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Pontocerebellar fibers through the middle peduncle are the defining afferent input of the cerebrocerebellum","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has a right lateral cerebellar hemisphere infarct. Asked to press her right arm against the examiner's hand, her arm flies past the starting point when the hand is suddenly removed, and on walking she tends to lean. Toward which side does she most likely lean or fall, and why?","choices":{"A":"Toward the left, because cerebellar output crosses once to the spinal cord","B":"Toward the left, because the lesion releases the right vestibular nuclei","C":"Toward no consistent side, because hemisphere lesions spare the legs","D":"Toward the right, because cerebellar output is crossed once in the pons","E":"Toward the right, because lateral cerebellar lesions impair the same side"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral cerebellar lesions produce ipsilateral limb ataxia, so leg ataxia and impaired check on the right give an unsteady gait with a tendency to lean or fall toward the side of the lesion; the rebound overshoot she shows is the inability of agonist and antagonist muscles to adapt to a sudden change in load. Cerebellar output crosses twice, in the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation and again in the corticospinal and rubrospinal pathways, so a single crossing to the left is wrong, and the crossing is in the midbrain rather than the pons. Hemisphere lesions affect the leg as well as the arm, and release of the vestibular nuclei is not the mechanism of hemispheric gait deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral hemisphere lesions: ipsilateral dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, rebound, hypotonia, and falling toward the lesion. COMLEX: The motor expression of cerebellar injury runs through corticospinal and rubrospinal pathways, which is why it appears on the same side.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — lateral hemisphere lesion patterns, including rebound and dysmetria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"41-year-old woman -> presents with left-sided limb dysmetria and intention tremor -> MRI shows two separate lesions, one in the left cerebellar hemisphere and a second, smaller lesion in the right midbrain tegmentum distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation. Which statement about the expected combined laterality is correct?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellar lesion produces left-sided deficits and the midbrain lesion right-sided deficits, so the patient should have bilateral limb ataxia","B":"Both lesions would produce purely right-sided deficits, since cerebellar-related lesions always act on the same side regardless of the decussation","C":"The midbrain lesion location, distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation, is anatomically impossible, so this combination cannot occur","D":"Only the cerebellar hemisphere lesion would produce a deficit, since a small midbrain tegmental lesion is always asymptomatic whatever fibers it interrupts","E":"Both lesions produce left-sided deficits: the left hemisphere lesion by the ipsilateral rule, and the right midbrain lesion because, distal to the decussation, it acts opposite its own side"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The left cerebellar hemisphere lesion follows the ipsilateral rule and produces left-sided deficits. The right midbrain lesion lies distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation, where it interrupts fibers that have already crossed from the left cerebellum, so it produces deficits on the side opposite itself, again the left; the two lesions summate on the same side, matching this patient's purely left-sided findings. Predicting right-sided or bilateral findings applies the ipsilateral rule to a lesion beyond the decussation, where it no longer holds. A lesion distal to the decussation is anatomically plausible, and a tegmental lesion on this efferent pathway would be expected to cause deficits rather than remain silent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a lesion distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation produces deficits opposite the lesion, on the side of the cerebellar hemisphere whose crossed fibers it interrupts. COMLEX: multifocal lesions like this appear in demyelinating disease or multiple emboli; place each lesion relative to the decussation before predicting laterality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 8 — Working through this combined lesion scenario is itself an application of the stepwise localization algorithm used for board-style cerebellar vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"57-year-old man -> presents with a wide-based, unsteady gait, titubation, and a tendency to fall in multiple directions, without any clearly lateralized limb findings -> MRI shows a lesion isolated to the vermis and fastigial nucleus. How does this presentation relate to the general ipsilateral rule for cerebellar hemisphere lesions?","choices":{"A":"This presentation directly follows the standard ipsilateral rule described earlier, and the absence of clear lateralization is simply an atypical, unexplained variant of that same general rule","B":"This presentation directly follows the standard contralateral pattern seen with lesions located distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation specifically, rather than a proximal lesion","C":"This midline lesion is the recognized exception to the ipsilateral rule, since bilateral fastigial/vestibular projections produce bilateral truncal deficits","D":"This presentation is entirely inconsistent with any known cerebellar lesion pattern described anywhere in the literature and should prompt full reconsideration of the underlying diagnosis altogether","E":"This presentation reflects a corticospinal tract lesion mimicking cerebellar disease rather than true cerebellar dysfunction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Midline vermal/fastigial lesions are the recognized exception to the ipsilateral rule, since the fastigial nucleus projects bilaterally to vestibular and reticular nuclei controlling axial musculature on both sides, producing bilateral, non-lateralized truncal ataxia and titubation rather than the one-sided limb pattern seen with lateral hemisphere lesions. This is not simply an unexplained atypical variant of the ipsilateral rule, it is a well-defined, anatomically explained exception. This presentation does not match the pattern for lesions distal to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation, which produce contralateral limb, not bilateral truncal, findings. This presentation is entirely consistent with well-established midline cerebellar lesion patterns, not inconsistent with known anatomy. This is a genuine cerebellar presentation, not a corticospinal tract lesion mimicking cerebellar disease, given the truncal ataxia and titubation described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing midline vermal lesions as the bilateral exception, distinct from both the standard ipsilateral rule and the distal-decussation contralateral exception, completes the full laterality framework. COMLEX: titubation with multidirectional falling, rather than a clear one-sided drift, should specifically raise suspicion for vermal/fastigial, not hemispheric, pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — This bilateral, non-lateralized presentation corresponds to the vermal/midline lesion category within the broader lesion-pattern framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":2,"stem":"66-year-old man -> long-standing type 2 diabetes -> progressive gait unsteadiness -> exam shows markedly reduced vibration and joint position sense in both feet, absent ankle reflexes, and a positive Romberg sign with marked worsening of sway when the eyes are closed -> finger-to-nose testing is accurate when performed with vision available. What is the most likely diagnosis, and which finding most directly supports it over a cerebellar cause?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar ataxia from diabetic cerebellar degeneration; the Romberg-positive pattern with accurate visually guided finger-to-nose testing best supports this diagnosis","B":"Sensory ataxia from diabetic sensory polyneuropathy, but the absent ankle reflexes alone are the single finding that best supports this over a cerebellar cause","C":"Cerebellar ataxia from diabetic cerebellar degeneration, but the reduced vibration sense alone is the single finding that best supports this diagnosis","D":"A combined cerebellar and sensory ataxia of equal severity, with the Romberg test result uninterpretable","E":"Sensory/peripheral ataxia from diabetic sensory polyneuropathy; the Romberg-positive pattern with accurate visually guided finger-to-nose testing best supports this over a cerebellar cause"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient's reduced vibration/joint position sense, absent ankle reflexes, and Romberg-positive pattern, worsening dramatically with eyes closed, point to sensory (peripheral) ataxia from diabetic polyneuropathy affecting large-fiber proprioceptive input, and the combination of Romberg-positivity with normal visually guided coordination, rather than true cerebellar dysmetria, is what best distinguishes this from a cerebellar cause. Diabetic cerebellar degeneration is not the classic diabetic neurologic complication described here, diabetic sensory polyneuropathy is far more characteristic, and cerebellar ataxia would typically be Romberg-negative, not positive as described. While absent ankle reflexes support a peripheral neuropathy, the Romberg-positive pattern combined with normal visually guided finger-to-nose testing is the more specific, defining feature that distinguishes sensory from cerebellar ataxia overall. Reduced vibration sense alone does not specifically diagnose cerebellar disease, and diabetic cerebellar degeneration is not the correct underlying diagnosis in this classic vignette. This is not a combined ataxia of equal severity, and the Romberg test is very much interpretable and diagnostically useful here, not uninterpretable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetic sensory polyneuropathy causing a Romberg-positive sensory ataxia is a classic, frequently tested vignette pairing peripheral neuropathy with proprioceptive loss. COMLEX: always test vibration sense with a 128 Hz tuning fork and joint position sense at the great toe in suspected peripheral neuropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — This case illustrates why sensory ataxia must be excluded before attributing gait unsteadiness to a specific cerebellar lesion zone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":2,"stem":"38-year-old woman -> subacute onset of gait unsteadiness over 3 weeks -> exam shows normal vibration and joint position sense, a Romberg-negative pattern with instability present regardless of eye position, and bilateral dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing along with dysdiadochokinesia. Which diagnosis is best supported, and what is the key distinguishing feature from a sensory cause?","choices":{"A":"Sensory ataxia from a dorsal column lesion; the Romberg-negative pattern combined with true dysmetria and dysdiadochokinesia best distinguishes this from a cerebellar cause","B":"Cerebellar ataxia, but the normal vibration and joint position sense is the single finding that best distinguishes this from a sensory cause","C":"Sensory ataxia from a dorsal column lesion, but the subacute 3-week time course is the single finding that best distinguishes this from a cerebellar cause","D":"Cerebellar ataxia; the Romberg-negative pattern combined with true dysmetria and dysdiadochokinesia best distinguishes this from a sensory cause","E":"An indeterminate ataxia type that cannot be classified using any of the bedside findings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal vibration and proprioception, a Romberg-negative pattern, and true dysmetria/dysdiadochokinesia present regardless of visual input together point clearly to cerebellar ataxia, and it is specifically this combination, Romberg-negativity plus persistent dysmetria independent of vision, that distinguishes it from a sensory cause. Sensory ataxia from a dorsal column lesion would be expected to show impaired, not normal, vibration/proprioception and a Romberg-positive, not negative, pattern, the opposite of what is described. While normal vibration/proprioception is consistent with a non-sensory cause, it is the combination with the Romberg-negative, vision-independent dysmetria pattern, not vibration sense alone, that most specifically confirms cerebellar ataxia. Time course alone, subacute over 3 weeks, does not reliably distinguish sensory from cerebellar ataxia, both can present subacutely depending on etiology. This vignette provides sufficient bedside findings, normal proprioception, Romberg-negativity, and true dysmetria, to confidently classify this as cerebellar, not indeterminate, ataxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pairing normal proprioceptive testing with a Romberg-negative pattern and true dysmetria builds a complete, confident case for cerebellar rather than sensory ataxia. COMLEX: subacute cerebellar ataxia in a young adult should prompt consideration of demyelinating disease, paraneoplastic syndrome, or toxic/metabolic causes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 8 — Distinguishing cerebellar from sensory ataxia is the essential first branch point in the stepwise localization algorithm for any ataxia vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":2,"stem":"70-year-old woman -> vitamin B12 deficiency confirmed on laboratory testing -> presents with progressive gait unsteadiness, tingling in both feet, and a positive Romberg sign -> exam also shows mild spasticity and extensor plantar responses bilaterally. How should the ataxia component of this presentation be classified, and why might additional findings beyond a pure cerebellar-versus-sensory distinction be present?","choices":{"A":"Sensory ataxia from dorsal column demyelination, subacute combined degeneration; the coexisting spasticity and extensor plantar responses reflect lateral corticospinal tract involvement in the same disease","B":"Cerebellar ataxia from a distinct vitamin B12-related cerebellar degeneration process; the coexisting spasticity and extensor plantar responses reflect simultaneous lateral corticospinal tract involvement in the same disease","C":"Sensory ataxia from dorsal column demyelination, but the coexisting spasticity and extensor plantar responses are unrelated findings from a separate disease process","D":"Cerebellar ataxia from vitamin B12-related cerebellar degeneration, but the coexisting spasticity and extensor plantar responses are unrelated findings from a separate disease process","E":"An ataxia type that simply cannot be determined from vitamin B12 deficiency alone, since this specific nutritional condition never produces either a positive or negative Romberg sign"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin B12 deficiency classically causes subacute combined degeneration, which produces sensory ataxia from dorsal column demyelination, hence the Romberg-positive pattern and tingling, while simultaneously affecting the lateral corticospinal tracts, explaining the coexisting spasticity and extensor plantar responses within this same unified disease process, rather than representing two unrelated conditions. Vitamin B12 deficiency does not classically cause a primary cerebellar degeneration, dorsal column and corticospinal tract involvement is the hallmark pattern instead. The spasticity and extensor plantar responses are directly explained by the same subacute combined degeneration process, not an unrelated separate disease, since B12 deficiency is well known to affect both the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts together. This is not a cerebellar degeneration, and again, the corticospinal findings are part of the same unified B12 deficiency pathology, not a coincidental separate process. Vitamin B12 deficiency very much can and classically does produce a positive Romberg sign through its dorsal column effects, contrary to the claim that it never does.","boardPrep":"Step 1: subacute combined degeneration with combined dorsal column (sensory ataxia, Romberg-positive) and lateral corticospinal tract (spasticity, upgoing toes) involvement is a classic, high-yield vitamin B12 deficiency vignette. COMLEX: check methylmalonic acid and homocysteine levels alongside B12 level when this combined pattern is suspected clinically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — Recognizing this as sensory, not cerebellar, ataxia prevents misapplying vermis/hemisphere/flocculonodular lesion-pattern reasoning to a non-cerebellar cause","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"8-year-old boy -> 6 weeks of morning headaches and vomiting -> new-onset truncal ataxia with a wide-based gait, titubation, and nystagmus, with relatively preserved limb coordination on formal testing -> MRI shows a midline posterior fossa mass. Which lesion location and cerebellar structure best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A lateral cerebellar hemisphere mass compressing the dentate nucleus, producing limb dysmetria as the dominant finding rather than truncal ataxia","B":"A midline flocculonodular/vermal mass compressing the fourth ventricle, producing vermal dysfunction with obstructive hydrocephalus","C":"An isolated, unilateral middle cerebellar peduncle mass, producing pure pontocerebellar dysfunction without truncal or gait involvement","D":"A mass isolated to the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation within the midbrain, producing contralateral limb findings rather than truncal ataxia","E":"A mass isolated to the restiform body bilaterally, producing pure proprioceptive afferent loss without cerebellar dysfunction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This classic pediatric presentation, truncal ataxia, titubation, nystagmus, and headache/vomiting from obstructive hydrocephalus, correlates with a midline flocculonodular/vermal mass, classically medulloblastoma in this age group, compressing the fourth ventricle and producing vestibulocerebellar/vermal dysfunction while sparing limb coordination. A lateral hemisphere mass would be expected to produce limb dysmetria as the dominant finding, not the truncal-predominant picture described here. An isolated middle cerebellar peduncle mass would produce pontocerebellar, limb-related findings, not this truncal/vestibular picture, and would be less likely to cause the described degree of hydrocephalus from midline compression. A mass at the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation would be expected to produce contralateral limb findings, not the truncal ataxia and hydrocephalus described. A bilateral restiform body lesion would affect spinal/olivary afferents, not primarily produce this truncal, vestibular-predominant clinical picture with hydrocephalus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric medulloblastoma classically presents with midline vermal/flocculonodular truncal ataxia plus signs of increased intracranial pressure from fourth ventricle obstruction. COMLEX: always consider posterior fossa mass and obtain neuroimaging in a child with new ataxia and morning headaches/vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — This midline mass affects the same vestibulocerebellar and vermal spinocerebellar zones responsible for truncal and gait control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"55-year-old man -> chronic alcohol use disorder -> progressive gait unsteadiness over months, out of proportion to relatively mild upper extremity dysmetria -> MRI shows selective atrophy of the anterior superior vermis. Which lesion pattern and structure best correlate with this alcohol-related presentation?","choices":{"A":"Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the anterior vermis, producing gait/truncal ataxia disproportionate to milder upper extremity findings","B":"Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the flocculonodular lobe, producing prominent vertigo and nystagmus disproportionate to any gait finding","C":"Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the lateral hemispheres bilaterally, producing severe upper extremity findings disproportionate to any gait finding","D":"Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the dentate nuclei bilaterally, producing severe, persistent deficits from direct nuclear involvement","E":"Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the middle cerebellar peduncles bilaterally, producing pure limb dysmetria without any gait involvement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic alcohol-related cerebellar degeneration classically and selectively targets the anterior superior vermis, producing gait and truncal ataxia that is disproportionately severe compared to relatively milder upper extremity findings, exactly this patient's presentation and imaging findings. Flocculonodular lobe involvement would be expected to produce prominent vertigo and nystagmus as dominant features, not the gait-predominant, vermal-localized pattern described and confirmed on imaging. Lateral hemisphere involvement would produce severe upper extremity findings as the dominant feature, the opposite of the gait-predominant, mild-upper-extremity pattern described here. Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration is not classically described as selectively targeting the dentate nuclei bilaterally, and that pattern would produce severe, persistent deficits inconsistent with this milder upper extremity involvement. Middle cerebellar peduncle involvement would produce pontocerebellar, limb-predominant findings, not the gait-dominant picture with confirmed vermal atrophy on imaging.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcoholic cerebellar degeneration selectively affecting the anterior vermis with gait-predominant ataxia is a classic, frequently tested toxic/nutritional cerebellar syndrome. COMLEX: thiamine deficiency should also be considered and treated in this population given its overlapping and compounding effects on cerebellar and other neurologic function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Anterior vermal involvement here falls within the spinocerebellar module responsible for axial and gait control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a metastasis in the right lateral cerebellar hemisphere has a coarse horizontal nystagmus. Careful recording shows that the slow conjugate component of the eye movements drifts in one consistent direction. Which pattern is characteristic of this lateral cerebellar lesion?","choices":{"A":"Slow drift toward the lesion, as in labyrinthine lesions","B":"Slow drift downward, as in a pure vertical gaze palsy","C":"Slow drift away from the lesion, opposite vestibular lesions","D":"Slow drift toward the lesion, matching the fast phase side","E":"No slow drift, with purely torsional quick jerks of the eyes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nystagmus from a lateral cerebellar lesion most commonly consists of a slow conjugate movement away from the side of the lesion, the opposite of the pattern seen with lesions of the vestibular receptors, primary vestibular fibers, or vestibular nuclei, where the eyes drift toward the damaged side. Some patients instead have pendular nystagmus with equal velocity in both directions. A vertical gaze palsy is a midbrain finding, and a cerebellar hemisphere lesion does not produce a purely torsional nystagmus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral cerebellar ocular signs: nystagmus with slow drift away from the lesion, impaired pursuit, and unstable fixation; midline lesions add truncal ataxia and titubation. COMLEX: Ocular motor findings help separate a cerebellar mass from a peripheral vestibular cause of vertigo.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 6 — pathologic nystagmus from asymmetric vestibular input, the contrasting pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":2,"stem":"An elderly man has a hemorrhage in the territory of the left superior cerebellar artery that damages the cerebellar nuclei on that side, without signs of raised intracranial pressure. During attempted movement of his left limbs he develops tremor, and during each tremor his pupils dilate and his face flushes and feels warm, stopping when the movement ends. What best explains the concurrent visceral signs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of nuclear output to the hypothalamus through the superior cerebellar peduncle","B":"Compression of the medulla by a raised intracranial pressure from the mass","C":"Interruption of vestibular input carried through the juxtarestiform body of the peduncle","D":"Damage to the descending sympathetic fibers in the lateral medulla of PICA territory","E":"Loss of pontocerebellar input carried through the middle cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cerebellar nuclei send a mainly crossed cerebellohypothalamic projection through the superior cerebellar peduncle and receive hypothalamocerebellar input, so damage to the nuclei in the SCA territory can produce visceral changes, here pupillary dilation and facial flushing tied to the movement tremor. Raised intracranial pressure is explicitly absent, and medullary compression would not track with each attempted movement. Juxtarestiform vestibular fibers and middle peduncle pontocerebellar fibers are not the visceral pathway, and lateral medullary sympathetic fibers lie in PICA rather than SCA territory and would cause a fixed Horner syndrome with a small pupil.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCA supplies the superior cerebellar surface, most cerebellar nuclei, and the superior peduncle, so SCA strokes give severe, persistent ipsilateral ataxia. COMLEX: Small fastigial lesions have also been linked to falls in heart rate and blood pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — hypothalamic coordination of autonomic responses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":2,"stem":"58-year-old woman -> sudden-onset left facial weakness, left-sided hearing loss and tinnitus, left limb ataxia, and mild dysarthria -> no dysphagia or hoarseness, and no crossed sensory pattern is present -> MRI confirms an infarct. Which vessel is most likely occluded, and what distinguishes this presentation from a PICA territory infarct?","choices":{"A":"PICA; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, without the dysphagia, hoarseness, or crossed sensory pattern typically seen with this vessel, points to this territory","B":"AICA; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, without the dysphagia, hoarseness, or crossed sensory pattern seen with PICA, points to this lateral pontine territory instead","C":"SCA; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss is a classic, defining feature of this specific vessel's vascular territory","D":"The anterior spinal artery; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss is a classic, defining feature of this specific vessel's territory","E":"The posterior cerebral artery; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss is a classic, defining feature of this specific vessel's territory"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, ipsilateral facial weakness, hearing loss/tinnitus, limb ataxia, and dysarthria without dysphagia, hoarseness, or a crossed sensory pattern, is classic for AICA occlusion, which supplies the lateral pons, including facial and vestibulocochlear nerve fascicles, plus the caudolateral cerebellum, distinguishing it from PICA's more medullary, crossed-sensory presentation. PICA territory infarcts classically include dysphagia, hoarseness, vertigo, and a crossed facial/body pain-temperature pattern, none of which dominate this vignette, making PICA an incorrect choice here despite the ataxia. SCA territory infarcts classically produce severe cerebellar signs with Horner syndrome and contralateral pain/temperature loss, not facial weakness and hearing loss as defining features. The anterior spinal artery supplies paramedian medullary structures and does not classically produce facial weakness or hearing loss as defining features, nor does it typically involve cerebellar territory. The posterior cerebral artery supplies occipital and thalamic territory and does not classically produce facial weakness or hearing loss as defining features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ipsilateral facial weakness plus hearing loss with ataxia, but without bulbar/crossed-sensory findings, should specifically suggest AICA rather than PICA territory. COMLEX: this presentation can mimic peripheral Bell palsy with vestibular neuritis, so the combination with limb ataxia is the key clue pointing to a central, AICA-territory cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — The caudolateral cerebellar involvement here overlaps with lateral hemisphere lesion territory, contributing to the limb ataxia described","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":2,"stem":"49-year-old man -> atrial fibrillation, not anticoagulated -> sudden-onset severe, disabling ataxia of the left limbs and trunk, ipsilateral (left) ptosis and miosis, and contralateral (right) body pain/temperature loss -> no facial weakness, hearing loss, dysphagia, or hoarseness. Which vessel is most likely occluded, and which anatomic features explain the unusually severe ataxia and the Horner syndrome?","choices":{"A":"PICA; direct involvement of the deep cerebellar nuclei explains the unusually severe, persistent ataxia, and proximity to descending sympathetic fibers explains the ipsilateral Horner syndrome","B":"AICA; direct involvement of the deep cerebellar nuclei explains the unusually severe, persistent ataxia, and proximity to descending sympathetic fibers explains the ipsilateral Horner syndrome","C":"PICA; the notable absence of facial weakness, hearing loss, dysphagia, and hoarseness is fully consistent with this particular vessel's typical, classic presentation","D":"AICA; the notable absence of facial weakness, hearing loss, dysphagia, and hoarseness is fully consistent with this particular vessel's typical, classic presentation","E":"SCA; direct involvement of the deep cerebellar nuclei explains the unusually severe, persistent ataxia, and proximity to the descending sympathetic fibers in the lateral tegmentum explains the ipsilateral Horner syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation, embolic source, severe persistent limb/truncal ataxia, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and contralateral pain/temperature loss without bulbar or facial nerve findings, is classic for SCA occlusion, since SCA supplies most of the cerebellar nuclei directly, explaining the unusual severity, and courses near the lateral tegmental descending sympathetic fibers and spinothalamic tract, explaining the Horner syndrome and crossed pain/temperature loss. PICA occlusion classically produces dysphagia and hoarseness from nucleus ambiguus involvement, which are notably absent here, making PICA an incorrect choice despite it also being capable of producing a Horner syndrome and cerebellar signs in some cases. AICA occlusion classically produces facial weakness and hearing loss, which are absent here, and AICA does not typically produce the severe, nuclear-predominant ataxia severity described. PICA's classic presentation typically does include dysphagia and hoarseness, so their absence here argues against, not for, a typical PICA presentation. AICA's classic presentation typically does include facial weakness and hearing loss, so their absence here argues against, not for, a typical AICA presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: severe, disproportionate ataxia plus Horner syndrome and crossed pain/temperature loss, without bulbar or facial nerve findings, points specifically to SCA territory. COMLEX: atrial fibrillation without anticoagulation is a classic embolic risk factor that should be actively sought in any acute cerebellar stroke vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 6 — Direct nuclear involvement in SCA territory produces the same increased severity and persistence associated with cortex-plus-nucleus lesions generally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":2,"stem":"52-year-old woman -> presents with 2 days of unsteady gait and clumsiness -> walk through the full stepwise localization algorithm, from initial ataxia classification through final vascular territory, to reach the most likely diagnosis, given exam findings of Romberg-negative gait ataxia, titubation, nystagmus, and entirely normal limb coordination.","choices":{"A":"Romberg-positive sensory ataxia, truncal/gait-predominant with spared limb coordination, midline localization, acute time course, localizing to PICA territory","B":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, truncal/gait-predominant with spared limb coordination, midline vermal/flocculonodular localization, acute time course, localizing to PICA vermal-branch territory given the classic vestibulocerebellar pattern","C":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, but limb-predominant with preserved gait, lateral hemisphere localization, acute time course, localizing to AICA territory","D":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia as expected, truncal/gait-predominant with spared limb coordination, but lateral hemisphere localization instead of midline, acute time course, localizing incorrectly to SCA territory given this pattern","E":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, truncal/gait-predominant with spared limb coordination, midline localization, but a chronic degenerative time course, localizing to no specific vascular territory"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Working through the algorithm correctly: Romberg-negative confirms cerebellar, not sensory, ataxia; truncal/gait-predominant findings with spared limb coordination localize to the midline vermal/flocculonodular zone; the acute 2-day time course favors a vascular cause; and this classic vestibulocerebellar pattern localizes most consistently to PICA's vermal branch territory. Labeling this Romberg-positive/sensory ataxia contradicts the explicitly stated Romberg-negative finding. Describing this as limb-predominant with preserved gait and lateral hemisphere localization contradicts the truncal/gait-predominant findings with normal limb coordination actually described. Correctly identifying midline/truncal localization but then assigning it to the lateral hemisphere and SCA territory is an internal contradiction, since SCA and the lateral hemisphere are associated with limb-predominant, not truncal-predominant, findings. Correctly localizing the findings but then assigning a chronic degenerative time course contradicts the explicitly acute, 2-day onset described in the vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: working through every step of the algorithm in sequence, ataxia type, then predominant findings, then zone, then time course, then vessel, is exactly how comprehensive board vignettes should be approached. COMLEX: this same sequential approach applies equally well to non-vascular cerebellar presentations by simply adjusting the final etiology step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — PICA territory is the specific vascular structure this algorithm localizes to when the vermal/truncal pattern combines with an acute time course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":2,"stem":"64-year-old man -> presents with sudden-onset right facial weakness, right hearing loss, and right limb ataxia -> walk through the full stepwise localization algorithm, from initial ataxia classification through final vascular territory, to reach the most likely diagnosis.","choices":{"A":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, limb-predominant with cranial nerve VII/VIII involvement, lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine localization, acute time course, localizing to PICA territory","B":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, limb-predominant with cranial nerve VII/VIII involvement, lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine localization, acute time course, localizing to AICA territory given the facial and hearing findings","C":"Sensory ataxia, limb-predominant with cranial nerve VII/VIII involvement, lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine localization, acute time course, localizing to AICA territory","D":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, truncal-predominant with cranial nerve VII/VIII involvement, midline localization, acute time course, localizing to AICA territory","E":"Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, limb-predominant with cranial nerve VII/VIII involvement, lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine localization, acute time course, localizing to SCA territory"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Working through the algorithm: the ataxia is cerebellar in type; the presence of ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss alongside limb ataxia points to lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine localization; the acute onset favors a vascular cause; and the specific combination of facial and cochleovestibular nerve involvement with cerebellar signs localizes precisely to AICA territory. Correctly working through the algorithm but assigning PICA territory ignores that PICA classically produces dysphagia/hoarseness, not facial weakness and hearing loss, making this an incorrect final step. Correctly identifying cranial nerve involvement but then calling the pattern truncal-predominant and midline contradicts the limb-predominant, lateral pontine features actually described, which do not fit a midline localization. Labeling this sensory, rather than cerebellar, ataxia is inconsistent with the vignette, which describes a vascular cranial nerve/cerebellar syndrome, not a proprioceptive sensory ataxia with Romberg testing described. Correctly localizing to the lateral hemisphere/lateral pontine pattern but then assigning SCA territory ignores that SCA does not classically produce facial weakness and hearing loss, which are the defining features here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pairing specific cranial nerve findings, facial weakness and hearing loss, with the ataxia localization step is what correctly distinguishes AICA from the other two cerebellar vessels in this algorithm. COMLEX: always integrate cranial nerve examination findings into cerebellar vascular syndrome localization rather than relying on ataxia pattern alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — AICA territory is the specific vascular structure this algorithm localizes to when facial and cochleovestibular findings combine with limb ataxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":2,"stem":"46-year-old woman -> atrial fibrillation -> sudden-onset severe, disabling right limb and truncal ataxia, right ptosis/miosis, and left body pain/temperature loss, without dysphagia, hoarseness, facial weakness, or hearing loss. Walk through the full stepwise localization algorithm, from initial ataxia classification through final vascular territory, to reach the most likely diagnosis.","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar ataxia, combined truncal and limb involvement of unusual severity, deep nuclear localization with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral pain/temperature loss, acute embolic time course, localizing to PICA territory","B":"Cerebellar ataxia, combined truncal and limb involvement of unusual severity, deep nuclear localization with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral pain/temperature loss, acute embolic time course, localizing to AICA territory","C":"Sensory ataxia, combined truncal and limb involvement, deep nuclear localization, acute embolic time course, localizing to SCA territory","D":"Cerebellar ataxia, limb-predominant with preserved truncal stability, lateral hemisphere localization, acute embolic time course, localizing to SCA territory","E":"Cerebellar ataxia, combined truncal and limb involvement of unusual severity, deep nuclear localization with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral pain/temperature loss, acute embolic time course, localizing to SCA territory"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Working through the algorithm: this is cerebellar ataxia of unusually severe, combined truncal and limb type, consistent with deep nuclear involvement; the ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral pain/temperature loss localize to the lateral tegmentum; the acute embolic time course fits the atrial fibrillation history; and the absence of bulbar or facial/cochleovestibular findings, combined with these specific features, localizes precisely to SCA territory. Correctly working through the algorithm but assigning PICA territory ignores that PICA classically includes dysphagia and hoarseness, which are explicitly absent here, making this an incorrect final step. Correctly working through the algorithm but assigning AICA territory ignores that AICA classically includes facial weakness and hearing loss, which are explicitly absent here as well, making this also incorrect. Labeling this sensory ataxia is inconsistent with the vascular, Horner-syndrome-associated presentation described, which is a cerebellar/brainstem vascular syndrome, not a proprioceptive sensory ataxia. Describing this as limb-predominant only with preserved truncal stability contradicts the vignette's explicit description of combined truncal and limb ataxia of unusual severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: working through the full algorithm to exclude PICA and AICA based on the specific absence of their defining bulbar and facial/cochleovestibular findings, before confirming SCA, models rigorous board-style differential reasoning. COMLEX: this final vignette ties together every step of the algorithm, ataxia type, predominant pattern, zone, time course, and vessel, into one comprehensive board-style question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — SCA territory is the specific vascular structure this algorithm localizes to when severe combined ataxia, Horner syndrome, and crossed pain/temperature loss occur together without bulbar or facial nerve findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":3,"stem":"A batter has only a fraction of a second to swing at a fastball, far too little time for visual or proprioceptive feedback to adjust the swing once it begins, so accurate contact depends on a predictive, feedforward motor plan computed before the movement starts. Which examination finding would best support the cerebellum specifically supplying this feedforward, rather than purely reactive, function?","choices":{"A":"A patient with an isolated primary motor cortex lesion shows dysmetria only on fast reaching and fully normal strength testing","B":"A patient with cerebellar disease shows dysmetria that is exactly equal in severity on both fast and slow reaching tasks","C":"A patient with cerebellar disease shows normal fast reaching but impaired slow reaching, the reverse pattern of what feedforward loss predicts","D":"A patient with an isolated dorsal column lesion shows dysmetria only on fast reaching tasks performed with the eyes closed","E":"A patient with cerebellar disease shows dysmetria that is worse on fast, ballistic reaching than on slow, visually guided reaching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because sensory feedback loops are too slow to correct a fastball-speed swing in real time, accurate ballistic movement depends on the cerebellum pre-computing the trajectory before feedback can arrive; when this predictive, feedforward function is lost, the resulting dysmetria is disproportionately worse on fast movements than on slow ones, where visual feedback can still contribute mid-course correction. Equal impairment on both speeds does not isolate a feedforward-specific deficit. The reverse pattern is not what cerebellar disease produces. Dorsal column and motor cortex lesions produce sensory ataxia and weakness, not this speed-dependent dysmetria pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the forward-model, feedforward-control theory of cerebellar function extends the classic coordination framework and is increasingly tested as an integrative concept. COMLEX: explains why finger-to-nose testing performed briskly exposes cerebellar dysmetria more clearly than testing performed slowly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — the coordination-not-command framework, tested here as a board-style feedforward-control vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient recovering from resection of a large posterior-lobe and vermal cerebellar tumor has entirely normal limb strength, normal finger-to-nose testing, and a normal gait, yet the family reports new difficulty with planning, blunted affect, and socially disinhibited behavior. Which concept best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum has no anatomic connections to prefrontal or limbic cortex, so this must reflect an unrelated coincidental illness","B":"Cerebellar lesions always produce ataxia before any other sign appears, so the absent motor findings rule out cerebellar involvement","C":"These changes represent an expected, self-limited surgical stress reaction that is unrelated to the specific tumor location removed","D":"The posterior cerebellum also participates in cognitive and affective processing, producing a nonmotor cognitive affective syndrome without ataxia","E":"These findings indicate a separate new lesion in frontal cortex that developed independently of the cerebellar surgical site itself"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beyond its motor-coordination role, the posterior cerebellum and vermis connect reciprocally with prefrontal, parietal, and limbic cortex through cerebro-cerebellar loops, so lesions confined to this region can produce cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome, cognitive and affective disturbance with little or no ataxia, since the classically tested motor circuits were spared. The cerebellum does have extensive nonmotor cortical connections. Ataxia is not a mandatory first sign of every cerebellar lesion. A generic stress reaction and a coincidental new frontal lesion are both far less likely than a recognized, location-specific syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome is a testable nonmotor cerebellar presentation, especially after posterior fossa or vermal surgery. COMLEX: reinforces that a normal neurologic motor exam does not exclude cerebellar pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — the coordination-not-command framework, tested here as a board-style feedforward-control vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system rather than a primary motor-command system.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients each have a similarly sized lesion involving the right cerebellar hemisphere: one from a stroke three days ago, the other from a slow-growing tumor discovered incidentally after several years of growth. Which comparison is expected on today's exam?","choices":{"A":"The tumor patient shows more severe ataxia than the stroke patient, because chronic mass effect always outweighs any benefit from gradual compensation over time","B":"Both patients show identical ataxia severity, because lesion size alone determines cerebellar deficit regardless of how quickly it developed","C":"Neither patient shows any ataxia, because cerebellar hemisphere lesions of this size never produce a clinically detectable deficit","D":"The stroke patient shows more severe ataxia than the tumor patient, because a slowly progressive lesion allows more time for compensatory reorganization","E":"The stroke patient shows less severe ataxia than the tumor patient, because acute lesions are compensated for far more quickly than chronic ones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cerebellum modulates rather than generates movement, and the brain can partially recruit other systems, cerebral cortex, vestibular pathways, and the unaffected cerebellum, to compensate for a slowly growing lesion over years; an acute stroke of comparable size allows no time for this reorganization, so it typically produces more severe apparent deficit despite similar lesion volume. Chronic mass effect from a slow-growing lesion does not automatically outweigh the benefit of gradual compensation. Deficit severity is not determined by size alone, time course matters. A lesion of this size in the cerebellar hemisphere is expected to produce a detectable deficit in at least one patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the concept of cerebellar reserve, time-dependent compensation for slowly progressive lesions, is a useful integrative addition to the coordination-not-command framework. COMLEX: explains why an incidentally found chronic cerebellar mass can coexist with a near-normal exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — the coordination-not-command framework, tested here as a board-style feedforward-control vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"An anatomy text uses the older phylogenetic terms archicerebellum, paleocerebellum, and neocerebellum alongside the newer functional terms vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum. Which pairing is correct?","choices":{"A":"Archicerebellum pairs with vestibulocerebellum, paleocerebellum pairs with spinocerebellum, and neocerebellum pairs with cerebrocerebellum","B":"Archicerebellum pairs with cerebrocerebellum, paleocerebellum pairs with vestibulocerebellum, and neocerebellum pairs with spinocerebellum","C":"Archicerebellum pairs with spinocerebellum, paleocerebellum pairs with cerebrocerebellum, and neocerebellum pairs with vestibulocerebellum","D":"All three phylogenetic terms map equally onto every one of the three functional zones, so no single fixed pairing actually exists","E":"The phylogenetic terms describe cerebellar blood supply territories, an entirely separate classification unrelated to functional zones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The archicerebellum, the oldest region evolutionarily, corresponds to the flocculonodular lobe and vestibulocerebellum; the paleocerebellum corresponds to the vermis and anterior lobe, the spinocerebellum; and the neocerebellum, the phylogenetically newest and largest region in humans, corresponds to the lateral hemispheres, the cerebrocerebellum. The other pairings scramble this evolutionary-to-functional correspondence. The terms do not map onto all three zones equally, each has one specific functional counterpart. They describe developmental origin, not vascular territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching phylogenetic and functional cerebellar terminology is a classic recall-integration pairing on anatomy-heavy exams. COMLEX: reinforces that the neocerebellum, largest in humans, underlies the fine dexterous limb control most relevant to clinical exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — the three functional zones, tested here as a board-style nomenclature and fine-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"Beyond posture and gait, the flocculus, part of the vestibulocerebellum, adjusts the gain of a specific reflex so that the eyes remain fixed on a target during head movement. Which reflex is being calibrated?","choices":{"A":"The pupillary light reflex, whose gain the flocculus continuously recalibrates to keep the pupil size stable during changing light","B":"The vestibulo-ocular reflex, whose gain the flocculus continuously recalibrates to keep gaze stable during head motion","C":"The corneal blink reflex, whose gain the flocculus continuously recalibrates to keep eyelid closure timing stable and consistent","D":"The accommodation reflex, whose gain the flocculus continuously recalibrates to keep near-vision focus stable during convergence","E":"The oculocardiac reflex, whose gain the flocculus continuously recalibrates to keep heart rate stable during eye pressure changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vestibulo-ocular reflex generates eye movements equal and opposite to head movements so gaze stays fixed on a target; the flocculus, a vestibulocerebellar structure, continuously adjusts this reflex's gain based on visual feedback, a fine calibration function distinct from the vestibulocerebellum's broader postural and truncal balance role. The pupillary light, corneal blink, accommodation, and oculocardiac reflexes are each mediated by separate brainstem circuits that the flocculus does not calibrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: flocculus-mediated vestibulo-ocular reflex gain adaptation is a well-tested example of cerebellar motor learning distinct from limb coordination. COMLEX: the bedside head impulse test is the clinical readout of VOR gain; a corrective refixation saccade signals reduced gain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — the three functional zones, tested here as a board-style nomenclature and fine-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the functional roles of the vestibulocerebellum, spinocerebellum, and cerebrocerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"Within the spinocerebellum's anterior lobe, the leg representation lies more medially near the vermis while the arm representation lies more laterally toward the intermediate zone. This arrangement is best described as which organizing principle?","choices":{"A":"A purely random distribution within the anterior lobe, with no reproducible medial-to-lateral limb representation pattern present","B":"A strictly chronological map within the anterior lobe, arranged by the order each limb's movements were originally learned developmentally","C":"A somatotopic map within the anterior lobe, with the lower limb represented medially and the upper limb represented more laterally","D":"A vascular territory map within the anterior lobe, arranged strictly according to which cerebellar artery supplies each region","E":"A purely sensory-modality map within the anterior lobe, arranged by touch, pain, and temperature rather than by limb identity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Like the cerebral motor cortex, the spinocerebellar anterior lobe contains a reproducible somatotopic map, with leg input represented medially near the vermis and arm input represented more laterally toward the intermediate zone, allowing focal anterior-lobe lesions to produce limb-selective rather than global ataxia. The arrangement is not random, not organized by learning chronology, not organized by arterial territory, and not organized by sensory modality; it is organized by body part.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatotopic organization within the spinocerebellar anterior lobe parallels the cortical homunculus and is a testable extension of basic somatotopy concepts. COMLEX: helps explain why some ataxic patients show disproportionate leg-versus-arm involvement on exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — the three functional zones, tested here as a board-style nomenclature and fine-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A tracer injected into Purkinje cells of the flocculonodular cortex labels axon terminals outside the cerebellum, showing that some cortical output bypasses the deep nuclei. Where do these Purkinje axons end, and through which route do they leave the cerebellum?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral red nucleus, through the superior cerebellar peduncle","B":"Ipsilateral vestibular nuclei, through the juxtarestiform body","C":"Contralateral ventral lateral thalamus, through the superior peduncle","D":"Ipsilateral basilar pontine nuclei, through the middle peduncle","E":"Contralateral inferior olive, through the restiform body"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Purkinje cells of the flocculonodular cortex project through the juxtarestiform body directly to the ipsilateral vestibular nuclei as GABAergic cerebellar corticovestibular fibers; this is the main cortical output that does not first synapse in a deep nucleus. Projections to the contralateral red nucleus and ventral lateral thalamus arise from the dentate and interposed nuclei and leave through the superior peduncle. The middle peduncle carries pontocerebellar afferents into the cerebellum, and the restiform body carries olivocerebellar climbing fibers inward from the contralateral olive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex and are inhibitory; most target deep nuclei, but flocculonodular and some vermal Purkinje cells reach the vestibular nuclei directly. COMLEX: Loss of this inhibition contributes to the nystagmus and truncal ataxia of flocculonodular lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — the vestibulocerebellum and its vestibular connections","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"An MRI obtained for a patient with early falls, vertical gaze palsy, and axial rigidity shows selective atrophy of the efferent cerebellar peduncle, distinct from the classic degenerative ataxia pattern of diffuse cerebellar cortical atrophy. Which peduncle's atrophy is a recognized imaging hallmark of this disorder?","choices":{"A":"The optic radiation, whose atrophy is a recognized MRI hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy","B":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, whose atrophy is a recognized MRI hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy","C":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, whose atrophy is a recognized MRI hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy","D":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, whose atrophy is a recognized MRI hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy","E":"The internal capsule, whose atrophy is a recognized MRI hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This vignette, early falls, vertical gaze palsy, and axial rigidity, describes progressive supranuclear palsy, an atypical parkinsonian tauopathy in which selective superior cerebellar peduncle atrophy, the sole efferent peduncle, is a recognized and clinically useful MRI finding, distinguishing it from the diffuse cortical atrophy pattern seen in primary degenerative ataxias. The inferior and middle peduncles are predominantly afferent and are not the classic atrophy site in this disease. The optic radiation and internal capsule are unrelated structures not implicated in this imaging pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: superior cerebellar peduncle atrophy is a specific, testable imaging clue for progressive supranuclear palsy, complementing the midbrain hummingbird sign. COMLEX: reinforces recognizing atypical parkinsonism red flags on exam and imaging together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — the three cerebellar peduncles, tested here as a board-style imaging and disease-correlate vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate cerebellar peduncles to their major input and output pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A student organizes the three cerebellar peduncles by predominant direction of information flow rather than by name. Which grouping is anatomically correct?","choices":{"A":"The inferior and superior peduncles are predominantly afferent into the cerebellum, while the middle peduncle is predominantly efferent out of it","B":"The middle and superior peduncles are predominantly afferent into the cerebellum, while the inferior peduncle is predominantly efferent out of it","C":"All three peduncles carry a strictly equal, balanced mixture of afferent and efferent fibers with no predominant direction","D":"The inferior and middle peduncles are predominantly afferent into the cerebellum, while the superior peduncle is predominantly efferent out of it","E":"All three peduncles are purely efferent structures, since the cerebellum receives its only input directly through the fourth ventricle itself"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle carries spinal, olivary, and vestibular afferents, and the middle cerebellar peduncle carries pontocerebellar afferents, so together they account for the great majority of cerebellar input; the superior cerebellar peduncle, by contrast, carries the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclear output and is predominantly efferent. The other groupings reverse or scramble this afferent-versus-efferent split. The three peduncles are not equally balanced, and cerebellar input does not arrive through the fourth ventricle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: framing the peduncles as two afferent-dominant plus one efferent-dominant is a useful synthesis for rapid recall under exam pressure. COMLEX: this same logic predicts which peduncle lesion produces predominantly input-loss versus output-loss deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — the three cerebellar peduncles, tested here as a board-style imaging and disease-correlate vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"The double-decussation rule for cerebellar ipsilaterality is usually taught using the corticospinal tract as the second crossing partner. A student asks whether the rubrospinal tract, which also originates from a cerebellar-influenced structure, follows the same crossing logic. Which statement is correct?","choices":{"A":"The rubrospinal tract never decussates at any level, remaining entirely ipsilateral throughout its full course from the red nucleus downward","B":"The rubrospinal tract also decussates, at the ventral tegmental decussation, so it shares the second crossing that restores ipsilaterality","C":"The rubrospinal tract decussates twice, once in the midbrain and again separately in the medulla, unlike any other descending motor pathway known","D":"The rubrospinal tract decussates only in the spinal cord itself, at the same level where the corticospinal tract also finally crosses over","E":"The rubrospinal tract is purely an ascending sensory pathway and therefore has no decussation pattern relevant to descending motor output"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Like the corticospinal tract, the rubrospinal tract decussates, at the ventral tegmental decussation in the midbrain shortly after leaving the red nucleus, so a cerebellar hemisphere lesion that first crosses at the superior cerebellar peduncle decussation and then reaches either the red nucleus or motor cortex still undergoes a second crossing downstream, reinforcing the same net-ipsilateral outcome regardless of which descending pathway is emphasized. The rubrospinal tract does not remain uncrossed, does not cross twice, does not cross in the spinal cord, and is a descending motor pathway, not an ascending sensory one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that multiple descending pathways, not just the corticospinal tract, participate in the second crossing strengthens understanding of the double-decussation principle. COMLEX: reinforces red nucleus and rubrospinal anatomy as an extension of corticospinal teaching.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 4 — the double-decussation ipsilaterality rule, tested here as a board-style boundary-case vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"A surgeon resects a mass from the left cerebellar hemisphere. Applying the ipsilaterality rule, on which side should the surgical team expect new postoperative limb dysmetria to appear, and why?","choices":{"A":"On the right side, opposite the resection cavity, because cerebellar hemisphere output crosses only once and therefore stays fully contralateral in every instance","B":"On both sides equally, because any cerebellar hemisphere resection produces bilateral, symmetric limb dysmetria regardless of laterality","C":"On neither side, because cerebellar hemisphere resection cavities never produce any detectable postoperative limb dysmetria","D":"On the right side only if the vermis was also removed, otherwise the resection produces no lateralized limb dysmetria","E":"On the left side, the same side as the resection cavity, because cerebellar hemisphere output undergoes a second crossing that restores net ipsilaterality"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Just as with any other cerebellar hemisphere lesion, a resection cavity in the left hemisphere is expected to produce ipsilateral, left-sided limb dysmetria, because the crossed cerebellar efferent pathway undergoes a second decussation downstream that restores net ipsilaterality; this same predictive rule applies whether the lesion is a stroke, tumor, or surgical resection cavity. Contralateral, bilateral, or absent dysmetria are not the expected postoperative pattern, and vermal involvement is not required to see this ipsilateral hemispheric sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: applying the ipsilaterality rule to a postoperative, rather than vascular, scenario tests whether the underlying logic, not just the classic stroke vignette, was actually understood. COMLEX: relevant to postoperative neurologic exam documentation after posterior fossa surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 4 — the double-decussation ipsilaterality rule, tested here as a board-style boundary-case vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain why cerebellar limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with known left-sided peripheral vestibular neuritis, unrelated and fully resolved months ago, now develops a new right cerebellar hemisphere lesion. A student tries to apply the double-decussation crossing framework to predict which side the old vestibular finding should have appeared on. Which statement correctly addresses this reasoning?","choices":{"A":"The double-decussation framework applies equally to peripheral vestibular structures, so the old left-sided lesion should have produced right-sided findings","B":"The double-decussation framework applies equally to peripheral vestibular structures, so the old left-sided lesion should have produced bilateral findings","C":"The double-decussation framework cannot be applied to any lesion in this patient, because two separate lesions can never be analyzed within the same framework","D":"The double-decussation framework predicts that the new right cerebellar lesion will simply reverse the laterality of the old resolved vestibular finding","E":"The double-decussation framework applies only to central efferent pathways, so the peripheral vestibular lesion produces ipsilateral, left-sided findings"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The double-decussation rule specifically describes central cerebellar efferent output crossing twice on its way to influencing limb movement; a peripheral vestibular structure, such as the labyrinth or vestibular nerve, sits entirely outside this central crossing pathway, so a peripheral vestibular lesion simply produces straightforward ipsilateral findings, unsteadiness and falling toward the affected left side, with nystagmus whose fast phase beats away from it, with no crossing involved at all. The framework does not apply equally to peripheral structures, does not force bilateral findings, is not inapplicable simply because two lesions coexist, and the new cerebellar lesion has no bearing on the old, already-resolved peripheral finding's laterality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing which structures do and do not participate in the double-decussation framework prevents over-applying a central-pathway rule to peripheral vestibular disease. COMLEX: reinforces peripheral-versus-central vestibular localization as a distinct clinical skill from cerebellar localization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 4 — the double-decussation ipsilaterality rule, tested here as a board-style boundary-case vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has increased sway with eyes closed, but unlike the straight, symmetric swaying of classic sensory ataxia, this patient consistently pulls and falls toward one particular side, and a head impulse test toward that same side reveals a corrective saccade. Which category of ataxia, beyond the standard sensory-versus-cerebellar dichotomy, best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Classic sensory ataxia, a category in which Romberg testing shows increased sway with a consistent directional pull toward one particular side","B":"Classic cerebellar ataxia, a category in which Romberg testing shows increased sway with a consistent directional pull toward one particular side","C":"Vestibular ataxia, a third category in which Romberg testing shows increased sway with a consistent directional pull toward the side of a peripheral vestibular deficit","D":"Combined sensory and cerebellar ataxia, a category in which Romberg testing shows increased sway with a consistent directional pull toward one side each time","E":"Psychogenic ataxia, a category in which Romberg testing shows increased sway with a consistent, fully reproducible directional pull toward one particular side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Standard teaching contrasts Romberg-positive sensory ataxia with Romberg-negative cerebellar ataxia, but peripheral vestibular disease forms a third, less commonly emphasized category: it can also increase sway with eyes closed, yet the sway is directional, pulling toward the affected side, and is confirmed by a corrective refixation saccade after a head impulse toward the affected side, a finding not expected in either pure sensory or pure cerebellar ataxia. Classic sensory ataxia is typically symmetric, not directional. Classic cerebellar ataxia is Romberg-negative. Combined ataxia and psychogenic ataxia do not classically produce this specific directional, head-impulse-positive pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing vestibular ataxia as a distinct third Romberg pattern, beyond the binary sensory-versus-cerebellar teaching, is a higher-yield integrative nuance. COMLEX: the head impulse test is a key bedside maneuver for localizing peripheral vestibular dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 5 — cerebellar versus sensory ataxia, tested here as a board-style expanded-differential vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a broad-based, unsteady gait, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and pupils that constrict with accommodation but not to light; treponemal serology is positive. Although the gait looks superficially cerebellar, which testing distinction points toward this being a sensory, rather than a true cerebellar, ataxia?","choices":{"A":"Romberg testing is negative, and proprioception is fully preserved, consistent with dorsal column and dorsal root disease rather than a cerebellar lesion","B":"Romberg testing is positive, and proprioception is impaired, consistent with dorsal column and dorsal root disease rather than a cerebellar lesion","C":"Deep tendon reflexes are markedly increased, and tone is spastic, consistent with dorsal column and dorsal root disease rather than a cerebellar lesion","D":"Intention tremor and dysdiadochokinesia are prominent, consistent with dorsal column and dorsal root disease rather than a cerebellar lesion","E":"Nystagmus is prominent and truncal ataxia is absent, consistent with dorsal column and dorsal root disease rather than a cerebellar lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is tabes dorsalis: degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots in tertiary syphilis causes a progressive sensory ataxia from impaired proprioception, with a positive Romberg sign, absent reflexes, and Argyll Robertson pupils (First Aid 2025 p544; Bootcamp Neurology lists broad-based ataxia, positive Romberg, loss of proprioception, areflexia). Bootcamp places sensory ataxia in the peripheral nervous system and dorsal column–medial lemniscus pathway (vitamin B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis) and cerebellar ataxia in the cerebellum. The course text explains why the Romberg test separates them: it tests position sense of the legs and trunk with vision removed, and patients may be steady with eyes open but sway or fall once the eyes close (Haines, The Neurologic Examination, course Anatomy 4 PDF p39–40). A negative Romberg with preserved proprioception would argue against a sensory ataxia. Brisk reflexes and spasticity are upper motor neuron signs, the opposite of the areflexia here. Intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, and nystagmus are cerebellar findings, so they would point toward, not away from, a cerebellar lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tabes dorsalis = dorsal column and dorsal root degeneration in tertiary syphilis, with sensory ataxia, positive Romberg, areflexia, and Argyll Robertson pupils (First Aid). COMLEX: unsteadiness that clearly worsens when the eyes close points to a sensory (or vestibular) ataxia rather than a cerebellar one (Bootcamp Romberg table; Haines).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 5 — cerebellar versus sensory ataxia, tested here as a board-style expanded-differential vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare cerebellar ataxia with sensory ataxia using Romberg testing and proprioceptive findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a mild, early cerebellar degeneration has a completely normal standard gait but becomes unsteady and must reach for support when asked to walk heel-to-toe in a straight line. Which principle explains why this particular bedside test detected an abnormality that standard gait observation missed?","choices":{"A":"Tandem gait narrows the base of support and removes the compensatory widening that can mask subtle cerebellar ataxia during ordinary walking","B":"Tandem gait widens the base of support and removes the compensatory narrowing that can mask subtle cerebellar ataxia during ordinary walking","C":"Tandem gait tests proprioceptive sensation exclusively and has no meaningful relationship to cerebellar coordination or truncal stability","D":"Tandem gait is a purely vestibular test and therefore cannot detect any form of cerebellar dysfunction","E":"Tandem gait is less sensitive than standard gait for detecting mild cerebellar disease, so this finding would be considered an unusual result"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A wide base of support is a natural compensatory strategy that can mask mild truncal or gait instability during ordinary walking; tandem, heel-to-toe gait deliberately narrows that base, removing the compensation and unmasking subtle cerebellar dysfunction that standard gait observation alone would miss, making it a more sensitive bedside screening tool. Tandem gait narrows, not widens, the base. It is not purely a proprioceptive or purely a vestibular test, it specifically stresses truncal and limb coordination. It is more, not less, sensitive than standard gait for mild cerebellar disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tandem gait as a sensitive bedside maneuver for subtle cerebellar dysfunction is a practical, frequently tested clinical-skills concept. COMLEX: a standard component of the neurologic and osteopathic structural gait examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 5 — cerebellar versus sensory ataxia, tested here as a board-style expanded-differential vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A lesion is confined to the intermediate zone and its associated interposed nuclei, the emboliform and globose nuclei, sparing both the vermis with its fastigial nucleus and the lateral hemisphere with its dentate nucleus. Which deficit is most specifically expected?","choices":{"A":"Isolated truncal ataxia with nystagmus and preserved limb coordination, reflecting the intermediate zone's distinct role separate from fine limb timing","B":"Contralateral limb dysmetria with intention tremor, reflecting the interposed nuclei's crossed output that never recrosses on its way down to the spinal cord motor neurons","C":"Abnormal limb muscle tone and altered stretch-reflex regulation, reflecting the intermediate zone's distinct role separate from truncal balance or fine limb timing","D":"Complete bilateral limb paralysis with preserved sensation, reflecting the intermediate zone's distinct role separate from truncal balance and timing","E":"Isolated language and speech production deficits, reflecting the intermediate zone's distinct role separate from truncal balance and limb timing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The intermediate zone and its interposed nuclei form a functionally distinct division from the vermis and fastigial nucleus, truncal balance, and the lateral hemisphere and dentate nucleus, fine dexterous timing, instead controlling limb musculature and, through the ventral lateral thalamus and motor cortex, influencing movements of the extremities and muscle tone; an isolated lesion here therefore disturbs ipsilateral limb tone rather than producing the classic truncal pattern. Isolated truncal ataxia with nystagmus describes flocculonodular or vermal disease, not intermediate-zone disease. Contralateral dysmetria is wrong because interposed output, like dentate output, crosses in the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle and again in the rubrospinal and corticospinal tracts, so the deficits are ipsilateral. Complete paralysis and isolated language deficits are not expected cerebellar findings at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the intermediate zone and interposed nuclei represent a distinct third division worth naming alongside the more commonly tested vermis and lateral hemisphere. COMLEX: tone abnormalities on exam should prompt consideration of this specific cerebellar subdivision.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — vermis, flocculonodular, intermediate, and hemispheric lesion patterns, tested here as a board-style structural-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"Several days after a pediatric patient undergoes resection of a vermal posterior fossa tumor, the child becomes unable to initiate speech despite intact comprehension and normal facial and tongue strength, accompanied by emotional lability. This transient postoperative syndrome most directly reflects disruption near which structure?","choices":{"A":"The lateral hemisphere and dentate nucleus, whose disruption produces transient postoperative cerebellar mutism and posterior fossa syndrome","B":"The middle cerebellar peduncle alone, whose isolated disruption produces transient postoperative cerebellar mutism and posterior fossa syndrome","C":"The inferior olivary nucleus, whose isolated disruption produces transient postoperative cerebellar mutism and posterior fossa syndrome","D":"The primary motor cortex for speech, whose disruption during this surgery produces transient postoperative cerebellar mutism and posterior fossa syndrome","E":"The vermis and its associated deep nuclei, whose disruption produces transient postoperative cerebellar mutism and posterior fossa syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cerebellar mutism, also called posterior fossa syndrome, is a recognized transient complication most strongly associated with surgery involving the vermis and its deep nuclear connections in pediatric patients, producing delayed-onset mutism, emotional lability, and sometimes ataxia, despite intact strength and comprehension, with gradual recovery over weeks. The lateral hemisphere and dentate nucleus are more associated with limb dysmetria than with mutism. The middle cerebellar peduncle alone and the inferior olivary nucleus are not the classic anatomic correlate of this syndrome. The surgery is confined to the posterior fossa and does not involve the cerebral motor cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cerebellar mutism, posterior fossa syndrome, is a distinctive pediatric postoperative complication tied specifically to vermal and deep nuclear disruption. COMLEX: relevant to postoperative counseling and expectation-setting after pediatric posterior fossa tumor resection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — vermis, flocculonodular, intermediate, and hemispheric lesion patterns, tested here as a board-style structural-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict lesion patterns involving the vermis, flocculonodular lobe, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A prenatal ultrasound identifies an enlarged, cystic fourth ventricle together with partial or complete absence of the cerebellar vermis. Which congenital diagnosis best fits this structural pattern?","choices":{"A":"Chiari II malformation, a congenital anomaly of vermal agenesis or hypoplasia with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle","B":"Medulloblastoma, an acquired posterior fossa tumor producing vermal agenesis or hypoplasia with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle over time","C":"Joubert syndrome, a congenital anomaly defined by cerebellar peduncle hypertrophy with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle in this pattern","D":"Dandy-Walker malformation, a congenital anomaly of vermal agenesis or hypoplasia with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle","E":"Arnold-Chiari malformation type I, a congenital anomaly of vermal agenesis or hypoplasia with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dandy-Walker malformation is a congenital hindbrain anomaly defined by agenesis or hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis together with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle and an enlarged posterior fossa, distinguishing it from acquired lesions such as tumors and from other congenital hindbrain anomalies. Chiari II malformation classically involves downward displacement of cerebellar tissue and the brainstem with myelomeningocele, not primary vermal agenesis with cystic fourth ventricle dilation. Medulloblastoma is an acquired tumor, not a congenital malformation. Joubert syndrome is defined by the molar tooth sign, cerebellar peduncle and vermian hypoplasia, not hypertrophy. Chiari I malformation involves tonsillar herniation without this vermal-cystic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dandy-Walker malformation is a classic congenital posterior fossa anomaly frequently paired on exams with Chiari malformations for differentiation. COMLEX: relevant to prenatal and pediatric developmental anomaly recognition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — vermis, flocculonodular, intermediate, and hemispheric lesion patterns, tested here as a board-style structural-lesion vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a large PICA-territory cerebellar infarct develops progressively worsening headache, decreased alertness, and new upgaze palsy over the following 24 hours. Which principle explains why this specific vascular territory carries a heightened risk of this evolving picture?","choices":{"A":"A large cerebellar infarct in the PICA territory cannot produce any mass effect, since the posterior fossa has essentially unlimited room to expand","B":"A large cerebellar infarct in the PICA territory always resolves spontaneously without intervention, since collateral flow fully replaces the occluded vessel","C":"A large cerebellar infarct in the PICA territory exclusively affects cortical gray matter, sparing white matter tracts that could otherwise cause swelling","D":"A large cerebellar infarct in the PICA territory produces this picture only if the contralateral hemisphere is also simultaneously infarcted at once","E":"A large cerebellar infarct, especially in the sizable PICA territory, can produce enough mass effect to compress the brainstem or obstruct CSF outflow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior fossa is a small, rigid, bony compartment, so a large cerebellar infarct, particularly in the relatively extensive PICA territory, can swell enough to compress the brainstem directly or obstruct cerebrospinal fluid outflow, producing hydrocephalus, decreased alertness, and brainstem signs such as upgaze palsy, a presentation that requires urgent neurosurgical evaluation for possible decompression. The posterior fossa does not have unlimited room to expand. Large PICA infarcts do not always resolve spontaneously without close monitoring or intervention. Ischemic infarcts affect both gray and white matter and can still swell. Contralateral involvement is not required to produce this posterior fossa mass-effect picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing cerebellar infarct as a neurosurgical emergency due to posterior fossa mass effect is a critical, high-stakes extension of pure vascular localization. COMLEX: reinforces urgent escalation and neurosurgical consultation thresholds for large cerebellar strokes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — PICA, AICA, and SCA syndromes, tested here as a board-style vascular-anatomy vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":3,"stem":"A student compares the parent vessels of the three cerebellar arteries. Which pairing of parent vessel to artery is correct?","choices":{"A":"PICA arises from the basilar artery, while both AICA and SCA arise from the vertebral artery","B":"PICA and SCA arise from the basilar artery, while AICA alone arises separately from the vertebral artery","C":"All three arteries, PICA, AICA, and SCA, arise directly from the vertebral artery before it joins to form the basilar artery","D":"All three arteries, PICA, AICA, and SCA, arise directly from the basilar artery after the two vertebral arteries have already joined","E":"PICA arises from the vertebral artery, while both AICA and SCA arise from the basilar artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery arises from the vertebral artery before the vertebrals join to form the basilar artery, while both the anterior inferior cerebellar artery and the superior cerebellar artery arise from the basilar artery itself, reflecting their more rostral, pontine and midbrain-level origin. The reversed pairing, all three from one single vessel, and the AICA-only variant all misstate this specific parent-vessel relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: knowing PICA's vertebral origin versus AICA and SCA's basilar origin helps predict which proximal vessel disease, vertebral versus basilar, produces which cerebellar syndrome. COMLEX: relevant to interpreting vascular imaging reports describing posterior circulation disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — PICA, AICA, and SCA syndromes, tested here as a board-style vascular-anatomy vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Recognize high-yield cerebellar vascular syndromes involving PICA, AICA, and SCA.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with basilar artery thrombosis develops severe, symmetric, bilateral limb and truncal ataxia rather than the usual unilateral pattern seen with a single cerebellar vascular syndrome. Which mechanism best explains this bilateral presentation?","choices":{"A":"Basilar artery thrombosis can occlude both posterior inferior cerebellar arteries simultaneously, since each PICA arises from the same basilar trunk directly","B":"Basilar artery thrombosis produces bilateral ataxia only through a purely coincidental, unrelated second embolic event occurring in the opposite vertebral artery","C":"Basilar artery thrombosis can occlude both superior cerebellar arteries at once, since each SCA arises from the same basilar trunk","D":"Basilar artery thrombosis cannot produce any bilateral cerebellar findings, since each cerebellar hemisphere has entirely separate, non-overlapping blood supply","E":"Basilar artery thrombosis produces bilateral ataxia exclusively through secondary global hypoxia rather than through any specific arterial territory"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because both superior cerebellar arteries branch from the same basilar artery trunk, a basilar thrombosis can occlude both simultaneously, producing bilateral SCA-territory infarction with severe, symmetric, deep cerebellar nuclear ataxia rather than the usual unilateral picture from a single-vessel occlusion. PICA arises from the vertebral arteries, not the basilar trunk, so basilar thrombosis does not directly explain bilateral PICA infarction. A coincidental second embolic event is a far less likely explanation than the shared basilar origin already present in this vignette. Overlapping supply does exist at the basilar trunk level, and global hypoxia is not the best explanation when a specific bilateral arterial mechanism is available.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral SCA infarction from basilar thrombosis is a recognized, testable exception to the usual unilateral cerebellar vascular syndrome pattern. COMLEX: reinforces basilar artery disease as a cause of severe, rapidly progressive bilateral brainstem and cerebellar findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — PICA, AICA, and SCA syndromes, tested here as a board-style vascular-anatomy vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old presents with a two-year history of gradually progressive gait unsteadiness and clumsy hand movements. Romberg testing is negative. There is no truncal-selective pattern, both gait and limb coordination are affected together. There is no headache, no acute time course, and a parent had a similar illness starting in mid-adulthood. Applying the stepwise localization algorithm, which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"The negative Romberg confirms a cerebellar rather than sensory process, and the combined truncal-and-limb pattern with a slow, hereditary course points toward a genetic spinocerebellar ataxia","B":"The negative Romberg confirms a sensory rather than cerebellar process, the combined truncal-and-limb pattern with a slow, hereditary time course points toward a genetic, degenerative spinocerebellar ataxia","C":"The negative Romberg confirms a cerebellar rather than sensory process, but the slow, hereditary time course actually points most strongly toward an acute PICA territory stroke","D":"The negative Romberg confirms a cerebellar rather than sensory process, but the family history is irrelevant to a localization algorithm","E":"The negative Romberg is inconclusive on its own, so no localization step can be taken without first obtaining advanced imaging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Following the algorithm, a negative Romberg first establishes a cerebellar rather than sensory process; the combined, non-truncal-selective pattern and, most importantly, the slow, multi-year progression in a patient with an affected parent then point away from any single acute focal lesion, vascular or otherwise, and toward a hereditary, degenerative spinocerebellar ataxia, illustrating that the algorithm's final time-course-and-etiology branch can terminate in a genetic diagnosis, not only a vascular one. A negative Romberg confirms cerebellar, not sensory, disease. A two-year gradual course is inconsistent with an acute stroke. Family history is a central, not irrelevant, input to the etiology branch. The negative Romberg is itself a meaningful, actionable localization step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stepwise localization algorithm applies equally well to chronic hereditary ataxias as to acute vascular syndromes, and spinocerebellar ataxia is a recognized autosomal dominant cause of adult-onset progressive ataxia. COMLEX: reinforces taking a family history as an integral part of ataxia workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 8 — the stepwise localization algorithm, tested here as a board-style alternate-endpoint vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient on phenytoin for seizure control develops gait unsteadiness, nystagmus, and dysarthria over several days, symmetric on exam, with a supratherapeutic drug level confirmed on testing. Applying the stepwise localization algorithm, which endpoint is best supported?","choices":{"A":"A positive Romberg with symmetric, bilateral, subacute findings and a clear temporal link to a supratherapeutic drug level points toward a toxic-metabolic cause","B":"A negative Romberg with symmetric, bilateral, subacute findings and this same drug level points most strongly toward an acute unilateral PICA territory stroke","C":"A negative Romberg with symmetric, bilateral, subacute findings and a clear temporal link to a supratherapeutic drug level points toward a toxic-metabolic cause","D":"A negative Romberg with symmetric, bilateral, subacute findings and this same drug level points most strongly toward an isolated flocculonodular lobe tumor","E":"The supratherapeutic drug level is coincidental and should be disregarded when applying the stepwise localization algorithm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Phenytoin toxicity produces a dose-dependent cerebellar syndrome, gait ataxia, nystagmus, and dysarthria, that is Romberg-negative, symmetric, and bilateral, and the algorithm's etiology branch should recognize that a subacute, symmetric, bilateral picture with a clear temporal link to a known cerebellotoxic drug level points toward a toxic-metabolic cause rather than forcing a focal structural or vascular diagnosis. A positive Romberg would suggest a sensory rather than cerebellar process, not fitting this picture. A unilateral vascular syndrome or an isolated flocculonodular tumor would be expected to produce asymmetric or truncal-selective findings, not this symmetric, bilateral, drug-linked picture. The elevated drug level is a key, not a disregardable, piece of etiologic evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: phenytoin toxicity is a classic, reversible cause of cerebellar-pattern ataxia and nystagmus, and recognizing it prevents unnecessary vascular workup. COMLEX: reinforces medication review as an essential step before pursuing structural imaging in new ataxia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 8 — the stepwise localization algorithm, tested here as a board-style alternate-endpoint vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl44_bloom_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply cerebellar signs to board-style localization questions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient presents with new, symmetric, bilateral limb and gait ataxia that does not fit cleanly into the flocculonodular, vermal, or lateral hemisphere lesion patterns, and does not localize to a single PICA, AICA, or SCA territory. What should this mismatch prompt within the stepwise localization algorithm?","choices":{"A":"Reconsidering the etiology branch toward a single, undetected PICA territory lesion, since PICA is statistically the most common cerebellar vascular syndrome","B":"Abandoning the stepwise localization algorithm entirely, since a bilateral, symmetric presentation means the algorithm no longer applies to this specific patient","C":"Concluding that the exam findings must be inaccurate or unreliable, since a true cerebellar lesion always fits one of the three named vascular territories","D":"Reconsidering the etiology branch toward a toxic-metabolic, genetic, or otherwise non-focal cause, rather than forcing the findings into a single vascular territory","E":"Assuming the patient has two simultaneous, coincidentally symmetric unilateral strokes, since this is statistically the most likely explanation for symmetric findings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When a presentation is symmetric and bilateral and does not cleanly fit any single anatomic zone or vascular territory, this mismatch is itself a meaningful algorithm output, a red flag prompting reconsideration toward toxic-metabolic, genetic, or other non-focal causes rather than forcing an artificial single-territory diagnosis. Forcing the findings into a single PICA territory contradicts the bilateral, non-focal pattern described. The algorithm should be adapted, not abandoned, when findings are atypical. Genuine bilateral, symmetric findings are not simply inaccurate exam data. Two simultaneous coincidental strokes is a far less statistically likely explanation than a single non-focal, systemic cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing when a presentation does not fit a focal vascular pattern is itself a testable localization skill, paralleling atypical-parkinsonism red-flag reasoning from the basal nuclei algorithm. COMLEX: reinforces broadening the differential to toxic-metabolic and genetic causes when focal localization fails.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 8 — the stepwise localization algorithm, tested here as a board-style alternate-endpoint vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"45":[{"id":"sdl45_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which class of eye movement is a rapid, conjugate movement that redirects the fovea to a new target and briefly suppresses visual input during the movement itself?","choices":{"A":"Smooth pursuit, the slow conjugate class","B":"Vergence, the disconjugate class","C":"Saccade, the rapid conjugate class","D":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, the compensatory class","E":"Optokinetic response, the compensatory class"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A saccade is a rapid, conjugate eye movement, 100-700 degrees/second, that redirects the fovea to a new target, with visual input suppressed during the movement so the resulting blur is not consciously perceived. Smooth pursuit is a slower, conjugate movement that follows a moving target rather than rapidly redirecting to a new one. Vergence is a disconjugate movement adjusting for target distance, not a rapid conjugate redirect. The vestibulo-ocular reflex is a compensatory reflex driven by head acceleration, not a voluntary target-redirecting movement. The optokinetic response compensates for continued whole-field visual motion, not a discrete rapid redirect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each eye movement class to its defining speed, conjugacy, and function is classic first-pass exam content. COMLEX: saccades are sedative-sensitive, a useful bedside clue when testing for toxic/metabolic causes of slowed eye movements.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information already traced from hair cells to the ocular motor nuclei, now one of six eye-movement classes organized here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which class of eye movement is disconjugate, adjusting the angle between the eyes as target distance changes, and is coupled with changes in lens curvature and pupil size?","choices":{"A":"Saccade, the rapid conjugate class","B":"Vergence, the disconjugate class","C":"Smooth pursuit, the slow conjugate class","D":"Optokinetic response, the compensatory class","E":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, the compensatory class"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vergence movements are disconjugate, the eyes move in opposite directions, adjusting the angle between them as target distance changes, and are coupled with lens accommodation and pupillary constriction as part of the near triad. Saccades and smooth pursuit are both conjugate movements, the eyes move together in the same direction, not disconjugately. The optokinetic response is a conjugate compensatory movement responding to whole-field retinal slip, not a disconjugate distance-adjusting movement. The vestibulo-ocular reflex is likewise a conjugate compensatory movement driven by head acceleration, not a disconjugate one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vergence is the only disconjugate eye movement class, a frequently tested distinguishing feature. COMLEX: test vergence at the bedside by moving a target toward and away from the patient's nose while watching for convergence and pupillary constriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information already traced from hair cells to the ocular motor nuclei, now one of six eye-movement classes organized here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which class of eye movement compensates for continued, whole-field movement of the visual scene across the retina, alternating slow compensatory phases with fast resetting saccades?","choices":{"A":"Optokinetic response, the visually-driven compensatory class","B":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, the vestibular-driven compensatory class","C":"Smooth pursuit, the slow conjugate tracking class","D":"Vergence, the disconjugate distance-adjusting class","E":"Saccade, the rapid conjugate redirecting class"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The optokinetic response compensates for continued whole-field retinal slip by alternating slow compensatory phases that match scene velocity with fast resetting saccades, together termed optokinetic nystagmus. The vestibulo-ocular reflex instead compensates for head acceleration sensed by the vestibular labyrinth, not whole-field visual motion directly. Smooth pursuit follows a single small moving target rather than compensating for whole-field scene movement. Vergence adjusts for target distance rather than compensating for retinal slip. A saccade is a single rapid redirect rather than an alternating slow-fast compensatory pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: optokinetic nystagmus, alternating slow following and fast resetting phases, is tested clinically with a rotating striped drum. COMLEX: nystagmus direction is always named for its fast phase, a convention that applies to optokinetic and vestibular nystagmus alike.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — vestibular information already traced from hair cells to the ocular motor nuclei, now one of six eye-movement classes organized here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"When the frontal eye field initiates a rightward horizontal saccade, which structure receives this crossed cortical input and directly drives the excitatory and inhibitory burst neurons of the horizontal gaze circuit?","choices":{"A":"The left paramedian pontine reticular formation","B":"The right rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"The left oculomotor nucleus directly, bypassing the pontine gaze center","D":"The right nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, acting as the primary saccade-generating structure","E":"The right paramedian pontine reticular formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The frontal eye field sends crossed projections to the contralateral paramedian pontine reticular formation, PPRF, the horizontal gaze center, so a rightward saccade is driven by the right PPRF, which contains the excitatory and inhibitory burst neurons that directly activate and suppress the appropriate abducens nucleus neurons. The left PPRF would drive a leftward, not rightward, saccade given the crossed frontal eye field projection. The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF is the vertical, not horizontal, gaze center and would not be the direct target here. The oculomotor nucleus is not bypassed to directly receive frontal eye field input, the PPRF and abducens nucleus are the intermediate horizontal gaze structures. The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi provides the tonic position-holding signal, not the primary saccade-generating burst signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the crossed frontal eye field to contralateral PPRF projection is the anatomic basis for testing horizontal gaze pathways. COMLEX: a destructive frontal eye field lesion produces transient gaze deviation toward the lesion side, the opposite of what irritative activity would produce.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the abducens nucleus already localized within the pons, now placed within its full horizontal gaze circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the horizontal gaze pathway, which structure transmits the signal from the ipsilateral abducens nucleus to the contralateral medial rectus subdivision of the oculomotor nucleus to achieve conjugate horizontal gaze?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi via the spinothalamic tract pathway","B":"Excitatory burst neurons via the medial lemniscus pathway","C":"Inhibitory burst neurons via the corticospinal tract pathway","D":"Omnipause cells via the tectospinal tract pathway","E":"Abducens internuclear neurons via the medial longitudinal fasciculus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Abducens internuclear neurons, located within the abducens nucleus itself, send their axons across the midline and ascend within the medial longitudinal fasciculus to reach the contralateral medial rectus subdivision of the oculomotor nucleus, coordinating the lateral rectus of one eye with the medial rectus of the other for conjugate gaze. Excitatory burst neurons drive the abducens nucleus directly but do not use the medial lemniscus, a sensory pathway unrelated to this motor circuit. Inhibitory burst neurons suppress the antagonist abducens nucleus but do not travel via the corticospinal tract, a descending motor pathway unrelated to this gaze circuit. Omnipause cells gate the burst neurons during fixation but do not use the tectospinal tract for this specific internuclear connection. The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi provides the tonic position signal but does not use the spinothalamic tract, a sensory pathway, to do so.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the abducens internuclear neuron-to-MLF-to-oculomotor nucleus pathway is the exact anatomic substrate tested by internuclear ophthalmoplegia questions. COMLEX: this pathway explains why a lesion here selectively impairs adduction of one eye during horizontal gaze while sparing convergence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the abducens nucleus already localized within the pons, now placed within its full horizontal gaze circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the tonic, position-holding signal that converts the phasic pulse of horizontal saccadic activity into the sustained step needed to maintain the new eye position?","choices":{"A":"The paramedian pontine reticular formation, the horizontal gaze center itself","B":"The interstitial nucleus of Cajal, the vertical gaze tonic integrator","C":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, the vertical gaze burst center","D":"The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, adjacent to the horizontal gaze center","E":"The superior colliculus, a supranuclear saccade-related structure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi transforms the phasic pulse signal from the PPRF into the tonic step signal required to hold the eyes steady at their new horizontal position, functioning as a neural integrator for horizontal gaze. The PPRF contributes the phasic pulse of activity but not the tonic step that maintains position. The interstitial nucleus of Cajal provides this same tonic step function, but specifically for vertical, not horizontal, gaze. The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF provides the phasic burst signal for vertical saccades, not the tonic horizontal position signal. The superior colliculus contributes supranuclear saccade-related signals but is not the source of the tonic position-holding signal for either horizontal or vertical gaze.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pairing the nucleus prepositus hypoglossi with horizontal tonic gaze holding and the interstitial nucleus of Cajal with vertical tonic gaze holding is a classic parallel-structure exam question. COMLEX: lesions of the neural integrator produce gaze-evoked nystagmus, since the eyes drift back toward primary position between corrective saccades.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the abducens nucleus already localized within the pons, now placed within its full horizontal gaze circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient cannot abduct the left eye, and no other eye movement or gaze abnormality is present on the left or right. Which lesion location best explains this isolated finding?","choices":{"A":"Left abducens nucleus","B":"Left medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Left abducens nerve","D":"Right abducens nucleus","E":"Right medial longitudinal fasciculus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An isolated abducens nerve lesion paralyzes abduction of the ipsilateral eye alone, since the nerve carries only lateral rectus motor axons and does not contain the internuclear neurons that also coordinate the contralateral medial rectus. A left abducens nucleus lesion would additionally impair the contralateral medial rectus during conjugate gaze, since the nucleus contains internuclear neurons as well as motor neurons, producing more than an isolated abduction deficit. A left MLF lesion produces impaired ipsilateral adduction on contralateral gaze, internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not isolated abduction failure. A right abducens nucleus lesion would affect the right eye's abduction and the left eye's adduction, not isolated left abduction. A right MLF lesion would produce right-sided adduction failure on leftward gaze, not this isolated left abduction deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated, pure abduction deficit with nothing else abnormal localizes specifically to the abducens nerve, not the nucleus. COMLEX: always test all directions of gaze in both eyes to distinguish a pure nerve palsy from a nuclear or internuclear lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve findings already used to localize nerve-versus-brainstem lesions, now applied specifically to CN VI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient cannot abduct the left eye, and additionally, the right eye cannot adduct when the patient attempts to look to the left. Which lesion location best explains this combined finding?","choices":{"A":"Left abducens nerve","B":"Left medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Right abducens nerve","D":"Right medial longitudinal fasciculus","E":"Left abducens nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An abducens nucleus lesion impairs both the ipsilateral lateral rectus, via loss of motor neurons, and the contralateral medial rectus during conjugate gaze, via loss of the internuclear neurons housed within the same nucleus, producing this combined ipsilateral abduction and contralateral adduction failure on attempted gaze toward the lesion side. An isolated abducens nerve lesion would impair only ipsilateral abduction, sparing the contralateral eye's adduction, since the nerve lacks internuclear neurons. A left MLF lesion would impair left eye adduction on rightward gaze, not left abduction combined with right adduction failure on leftward gaze. A right abducens nerve lesion would impair right, not left, abduction, the wrong side for this presentation. A right MLF lesion would impair right eye adduction on leftward gaze, not the left-sided abduction deficit described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combined ipsilateral abduction failure plus contralateral adduction failure on gaze toward the lesion side is the signature of an abducens nucleus, not nerve, lesion. COMLEX: this distinction hinges entirely on whether the contralateral eye is also affected during conjugate gaze testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve findings already used to localize nerve-versus-brainstem lesions, now applied specifically to CN VI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":1,"stem":"After exiting near the midline at the pontomedullary junction, the abducens nerve enters the dura and ascends the clivus. It then passes beneath the petroclinoid ligament to reach the cavernous sinus. What is this passage beneath the ligament called?","choices":{"A":"Meckel cave","B":"Dorello canal","C":"Foramen ovale","D":"Optic canal","E":"Hypoglossal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The abducens nerve ascends the clivus and passes beneath the petroclinoid ligament through the Dorello canal before entering the cavernous sinus and then the orbit through the superior orbital fissure. Meckel cave holds the trigeminal ganglion, the foramen ovale transmits the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, the optic canal transmits the optic nerve and ophthalmic artery, and the hypoglossal canal transmits cranial nerve XII.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abducens course: nucleus in the facial colliculus, exit at the pontomedullary junction, clivus, Dorello canal, cavernous sinus, superior orbital fissure, lateral rectus. COMLEX: The long intracranial course explains why an isolated sixth nerve palsy can be a false localizing sign of raised intracranial pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the pontomedullary junction and the abducens exit point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does a lesion confined to the medial longitudinal fasciculus between the abducens and oculomotor nuclei spare convergence even though it impairs adduction during conjugate horizontal gaze?","choices":{"A":"Convergence is driven by the abducens nucleus directly and exclusively, so an MLF lesion cannot ever affect it","B":"Convergence does not require medial rectus contraction, so MLF damage is irrelevant to this class of movement","C":"Convergence is driven by the midbrain near-response circuitry acting directly on an intact oculomotor nucleus and nerve, bypassing the damaged internuclear pathway entirely","D":"The MLF regenerates rapidly enough between testing convergence and testing conjugate gaze on the same clinical visit to appear falsely intact each time","E":"Convergence is a brainstem reflex that does not involve the oculomotor nucleus, unlike conjugate horizontal gaze"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Convergence is generated by near-response premotor neurons in the midbrain, the supraoculomotor area and adjacent reticular formation, that project directly onto medial rectus motor neurons within an intact oculomotor nucleus and travel out via an intact oculomotor nerve, entirely bypassing the abducens internuclear pathway that is damaged in an MLF lesion, so convergence remains normal while conjugate adduction fails. Convergence is not driven by the abducens nucleus, which controls only the lateral rectus and internuclear neurons, not the medial rectus. Convergence very much requires medial rectus contraction, that is precisely what points both foveae at a near target, so this claim is false. The MLF does not regenerate rapidly enough within the timeframe of a single examination to explain this sparing. Convergence does involve the oculomotor nucleus, since its motor neurons drive the medial rectus contraction required for the near response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dissociation between convergence, driven by an independent midbrain circuit, and conjugate adduction, driven by the MLF, is the single most tested concept for internuclear ophthalmoplegia. COMLEX: always test convergence explicitly when internuclear ophthalmoplegia is suspected, since this is what confirms the lesion spares the oculomotor nucleus and nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 5 — the MLF already introduced as the VOR relay, now the anatomic substrate for internuclear ophthalmoplegia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"A large right abducens nucleus lesion damages not only the local motor and internuclear neurons but also the crossing internuclear fibers arriving from the contralateral abducens nucleus. What clinical picture, known as one-and-a-half syndrome, results?","choices":{"A":"The right eye cannot move horizontally in either direction, but the left eye moves entirely normally in both directions of horizontal gaze","B":"The right eye cannot move horizontally in either direction, and the left eye can only abduct, with neither eye able to adduct","C":"Both eyes are completely immobile in every direction of gaze, including vertical movements, due to this single isolated lesion","D":"The right eye can abduct entirely normally, but the left eye cannot move horizontally in either direction","E":"Both eyes retain normal abduction bilaterally, but neither eye is able to adduct in either direction of horizontal gaze"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In one-and-a-half syndrome, the right abducens nucleus lesion abolishes right-eye abduction and, through loss of its own internuclear neurons, left-eye adduction on rightward gaze, a full horizontal gaze palsy; damage to the crossed internuclear fibers from the left abducens nucleus, ascending in the right MLF, also abolishes right-eye adduction on leftward gaze. Only left-eye abduction survives, one and a half lost movements. Claiming the left eye moves normally ignores the gaze palsy, which removes left-eye adduction on rightward gaze. Complete immobility including vertical gaze is far more extensive than this lesion produces, since vertical gaze circuitry is untouched. Claiming the right eye abducts normally contradicts the abducens nucleus lesion, and claiming both eyes retain abduction ignores the loss of right-eye abduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: one-and-a-half syndrome, one full horizontal gaze palsy plus one internuclear ophthalmoplegia, leaving only contralateral abduction intact, is a classic, highly testable brainstem localization pattern. COMLEX: this pattern classically results from a single paramedian pontine lesion affecting both the abducens nucleus and the adjacent, already-crossed MLF fibers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 5 — the MLF already introduced as the VOR relay, now the anatomic substrate for internuclear ophthalmoplegia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding, if present, would argue against a diagnosis of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and instead suggest a peripheral neuromuscular cause of the adduction deficit, such as myasthenia gravis?","choices":{"A":"Impaired adduction of one eye on conjugate horizontal gaze with preserved convergence in that same eye","B":"Fatigability of the adduction deficit with sustained gaze and variability of the findings over the course of the day","C":"A consistent, reproducible pattern of adduction failure that does not change in severity throughout the examination","D":"Bilateral involvement in a young adult patient with other findings suggestive of central nervous system demyelination","E":"Nystagmus of the abducting eye that accompanies the impaired adduction on conjugate gaze testing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fatigability and variability of the deficit over time are hallmarks of a neuromuscular junction disorder such as myasthenia gravis, which can mimic internuclear ophthalmoplegia by impairing medial rectus function, and this fluctuating pattern is not expected from a fixed structural MLF lesion, making it the key clue favoring a peripheral rather than central cause. Impaired adduction with preserved convergence is the classic, expected pattern of true internuclear ophthalmoplegia itself, not evidence against it. Nystagmus of the abducting eye is likewise a classic, expected accompanying feature of true internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not a finding that argues against it. A consistent, non-fluctuating pattern is exactly what is expected from a fixed structural lesion like true internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not evidence for a neuromuscular cause. Bilateral involvement in a young adult with other demyelinating findings is classic for multiple sclerosis-related true internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not evidence against it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"myasthenia gravis, the great masquerader\" can mimic internuclear ophthalmoplegia, and fatigability/fluctuation is the key distinguishing clinical clue. COMLEX: always ask about symptom variability throughout the day and with sustained effort when an apparent INO does not fit the expected fixed, structural pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 5 — the MLF already introduced as the VOR relay, now the anatomic substrate for internuclear ophthalmoplegia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure serves as the vertical gaze center, providing the phasic burst signal for vertical saccades, and is located at the rostral end of the medial longitudinal fasciculus?","choices":{"A":"The interstitial nucleus of Cajal, the tonic vertical integrator","B":"The paramedian pontine reticular formation, the horizontal gaze center","C":"The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, the tonic horizontal integrator","D":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus","E":"The posterior commissure, a crossing connective pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, riMLF, sits at the rostral end of the MLF and contains burst neurons that provide the phasic, saccade-related pulse of activity for vertical gaze motor neurons, making it the vertical analog of the PPRF. The interstitial nucleus of Cajal provides the tonic step signal for vertical gaze, not the phasic burst signal, a distinct but related function. The paramedian pontine reticular formation is the horizontal, not vertical, gaze center. The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi provides the tonic step signal for horizontal, not vertical, gaze. The posterior commissure is a crossing pathway connecting the two interstitial nuclei of Cajal, not itself a burst-generating structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the riMLF as the vertical gaze burst center, paired with the interstitial nucleus of Cajal for the tonic step, mirrors the PPRF/nucleus prepositus hypoglossi pairing for horizontal gaze. COMLEX: lesions at the midbrain-diencephalon junction affecting this region classically produce vertical gaze palsies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — Weber, Benedikt, and Claude syndromes already built for the rostral midbrain, now joined by the vertical gaze pathway sharing this same territory","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":1,"stem":"Why must the projections of the riMLF and interstitial nucleus of Cajal on both sides of the brainstem work together during a pure vertical saccade, rather than one side acting alone?","choices":{"A":"Because the superior and inferior rectus muscles are innervated exclusively by the contralateral oculomotor nucleus, requiring this bilateral coordination for any movement","B":"Because unilateral riMLF activation alone would produce no eye movement, so bilateral input is needed to generate any signal","C":"Because the interstitial nucleus of Cajal is a single midline structure with no left or right subdivision, making bilateral cooperation anatomically entirely unnecessary","D":"Because vertical eye movements do not actually require coordinated bilateral input, a common misconception about midbrain gaze circuitry","E":"Because the superior and inferior rectus muscles act in bilateral pairs during vertical eye movements, and unilateral activation alone produces torsion rather than pure vertical movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vertical eye movements require the superior and inferior rectus muscles of both eyes to act together in pairs, so bilateral riMLF and interstitial nucleus of Cajal activity must be coordinated, since unilateral activation alone introduces an asymmetric torsional component rather than producing a clean, pure vertical movement. The superior and inferior recti are not innervated exclusively by the contralateral oculomotor nucleus in a way that mandates this specific bilateral coordination, the oculomotor nucleus has both crossed and uncrossed projections for different muscle groups. Unilateral riMLF activation does produce a measurable eye movement, just with an added torsional component, not a complete absence of any movement. The interstitial nucleus of Cajal does have distinguishable left and right populations despite lying near the midline, contrary to the claim of no lateral subdivision. Vertical eye movements do genuinely require this coordinated bilateral input, so this is not a misconception but an accurate description of the underlying circuitry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: understanding why unilateral vertical gaze center activation produces torsion, not pure vertical movement, is a nuanced but testable extension of basic vertical gaze anatomy. COMLEX: this same bilateral-pairing logic explains why posterior commissure lesions, disrupting the crossed connections between the two sides, produce vertical gaze deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — Weber, Benedikt, and Claude syndromes already built for the rostral midbrain, now joined by the vertical gaze pathway sharing this same territory","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":1,"stem":"A pineal region tumor compresses the posterior commissure. Which functional consequence best explains why this specific structure's compression produces vertical gaze deficits?","choices":{"A":"The posterior commissure carries the crossed connections linking the two interstitial nuclei of Cajal, so its compression disrupts the bilateral coordination vertical gaze requires","B":"The posterior commissure is itself the primary burst generator for vertical saccades, so its compression eliminates the phasic pulse signal","C":"The posterior commissure carries the entire horizontal gaze pathway, so its compression produces horizontal, not vertical, gaze deficits","D":"The posterior commissure has no known function related to eye movements, so any vertical gaze deficit here must be a coincidence","E":"The posterior commissure carries only pupillary light reflex fibers, so its compression affects pupillary responses but never eye movements"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior commissure carries the crossed connections linking the two interstitial nuclei of Cajal and their onward projections to the oculomotor nucleus, so compression here, as with a pinealoma, disrupts the coordinated bilateral signaling that vertical gaze requires, producing vertical gaze deficits, part of the classic dorsal midbrain syndrome. The posterior commissure is not itself the burst-generating structure, that role belongs to the riMLF, so this claim misattributes the mechanism. The posterior commissure does not carry the horizontal gaze pathway, which instead relies on the PPRF and MLF, so this claim is incorrect. The posterior commissure clearly does have a well-established function related to vertical eye movements, contrary to the claim of no known function. While the posterior commissure does carry some decussating pupillary light reflex fibers, its compression is not exclusively limited to pupillary effects, since it also disrupts vertical gaze as described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinealoma compressing the posterior commissure producing vertical gaze palsy is the anatomic basis of Parinaud syndrome, a very high-yield board topic. COMLEX: this same dorsal midbrain compression can also produce light-near dissociation of the pupils by disrupting the decussating pretectal fibers traveling through this same commissure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — Weber, Benedikt, and Claude syndromes already built for the rostral midbrain, now joined by the vertical gaze pathway sharing this same territory","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":1,"stem":"Trace the afferent limb of the pupillary light reflex from the retina to the point where it reaches the neurons that will ultimately drive pupillary constriction. Which pathway is correct?","choices":{"A":"Retinal ganglion cells project via the optic nerve and optic tract to the pretectal olivary nucleus, which projects bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus","B":"Retinal ganglion cells project via the optic radiations to the primary visual cortex first, which then relays the signal all the way back down to the oculomotor nucleus itself","C":"Retinal ganglion cells project via the optic nerve directly to the ipsilateral Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus, bypassing the pretectum","D":"Retinal ganglion cells project via the optic nerve to the lateral geniculate nucleus, which then relays the signal directly onward to the ciliary ganglion itself","E":"Retinal ganglion cells project via the optic nerve to the superior colliculus, which relays the signal onward to the trochlear nucleus for pupillary control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The afferent limb begins with luminance-sensitive retinal ganglion cells whose axons travel via the optic nerve and optic tract to terminate in the pretectal olivary nucleus, which then projects bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus on both sides, the anatomic basis for both the direct and consensual responses. The pathway does not bypass the pretectum to reach the Edinger-Westphal nucleus directly, the pretectal olivary nucleus is an obligatory relay station. The pupillary light reflex does not route through the optic radiations or primary visual cortex, that pathway subserves conscious visual perception, not this subcortical reflex. The lateral geniculate nucleus is part of the visual sensory relay to cortex and does not itself relay pupillary reflex signals to the ciliary ganglion. The superior colliculus is involved in saccadic eye movement control, not the pupillary light reflex, and does not relay to the trochlear nucleus for this purpose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the retina-to-pretectal olivary nucleus-to-bilateral Edinger-Westphal pathway is the exact anatomic basis for testing both direct and consensual pupillary responses. COMLEX: the bilateral pretectal projection is why light in one eye produces constriction in both eyes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the pupillary light reflex pathway already introduced alongside other cranial nerve reflexes, now traced in full anatomic detail","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":1,"stem":"Postganglionic cholinergic neurons of the ciliary ganglion reach the globe through the short ciliary nerves. Which muscle receives the great majority of these postganglionic neurons?","choices":{"A":"Dilator pupillae muscle of the iris","B":"Superior tarsal muscle of the lid","C":"Sphincter pupillae of the iris","D":"Ciliary muscle of the ciliary body","E":"Levator palpebrae superioris"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Postganglionic ciliary ganglion neurons supply both the ciliary muscle and the pupillary constrictor, but the great majority supply the ciliary muscle, which changes lens curvature for accommodation. The sphincter pupillae receives the smaller share. The dilator pupillae and superior tarsal muscle receive adrenergic postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion, and the levator palpebrae is skeletal muscle supplied directly by the oculomotor nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus, oculomotor nerve, ciliary ganglion, short ciliary nerves to the ciliary muscle and sphincter pupillae. COMLEX: Oculomotor nerve lesions cause ipsilateral mydriasis plus paralysis of accommodation, blurring near vision.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 4 — craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":1,"stem":"During the near response, which structures provide the premotor signal that simultaneously drives medial rectus contraction for convergence and Edinger-Westphal activation for pupillary constriction?","choices":{"A":"Neurons in the supraoculomotor area and adjacent midbrain reticular formation, lateral to the oculomotor nucleus","B":"Neurons in the pretectal olivary nucleus, the same structure that relays the pupillary light reflex afferent signal","C":"Neurons in the paramedian pontine reticular formation, the same structure that drives horizontal saccades","D":"Neurons in the nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, the same structure that provides tonic horizontal gaze holding","E":"Neurons in the accessory optic system nuclei, the same structures that mediate the optokinetic reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Near-response premotor neurons located in the supraoculomotor area and the adjacent midbrain reticular formation, lateral to the oculomotor nucleus, project to both medial rectus motor neurons for convergence and to Edinger-Westphal preganglionic neurons for pupillary constriction, coordinating the near triad. The pretectal olivary nucleus relays the light reflex afferent signal, an entirely separate circuit from the near response, and is not the structure driving convergence and pupillary constriction together. The paramedian pontine reticular formation drives horizontal saccades, a distinct movement class from vergence, and is not the near-response premotor source. The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi provides tonic horizontal gaze holding, unrelated to the near triad's convergence and pupillary components. The accessory optic system nuclei mediate the optokinetic reflex's afferent limb, an entirely separate visual motor pathway from the near response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the supraoculomotor area as the shared premotor source for convergence and near-response pupillary constriction explains why these two components of the near triad are normally yoked together. COMLEX: light-near dissociation, an intact near response with an impaired light reflex, localizes to the pretectal light reflex pathway specifically, sparing this separate near-response circuit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the pupillary light reflex pathway already introduced alongside other cranial nerve reflexes, now traced in full anatomic detail","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has anisocoria with a larger left pupil, the larger left pupil reacts poorly to light shone in either eye while the right pupil constricts normally to light in either eye, and the left eyelid is mildly ptotic with the eye appearing slightly displaced downward and outward. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Left superior cervical ganglion","B":"Left optic nerve","C":"Left pretectal olivary nucleus","D":"Left oculomotor nerve","E":"Left ciliary ganglion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A left oculomotor nerve lesion produces ipsilateral mydriasis, from loss of parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor fibers, ptosis, from loss of levator palpebrae innervation, and a down-and-out eye position, from unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique action, while the right pupil still constricts to light in either eye because both afferent limbs and its own efferent limb are intact, matching this described pattern of anisocoria with ptosis and ocular malposition. A left superior cervical ganglion lesion, Horner syndrome, would produce miosis, not mydriasis, along with ptosis, the opposite pupillary finding from what is described. A left optic nerve lesion would produce a relative afferent pupillary defect with diminished responses when light is shone into that eye specifically, not this pattern of anisocoria with ptosis and ocular malposition. A left pretectal olivary nucleus lesion would tend to produce bilateral weakening of the light reflex rather than this unilateral mydriasis, ptosis, and down-and-out eye position. A left ciliary ganglion lesion produces Adie tonic pupil with light-near dissociation, not the ptosis and down-and-out ocular malposition described here, which require oculomotor nerve, not just ciliary ganglion, involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the triad of ipsilateral mydriasis, ptosis, and a down-and-out eye position is the classic localizing pattern for a complete oculomotor nerve palsy. COMLEX: always check pupillary involvement, since a \"pupil-sparing\" third nerve palsy suggests a different mechanism, typically microvascular ischemia, than a pupil-involving one, which suggests compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the midbrain board-vignette algorithm already built for eye movement and pupillary findings, now extended to the full visual motor system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports vertical diplopia that worsens when looking down and toward the nose, such as when reading or descending stairs. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Oculomotor nerve","B":"Trochlear nerve","C":"Abducens nerve","D":"Medial longitudinal fasciculus","E":"Frontal eye field"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The trochlear nerve supplies the superior oblique muscle, whose primary actions are depression, intorsion, and abduction, so a trochlear nerve palsy characteristically worsens with attempted downward and inward gaze, producing the classic reading or stair-descending complaint of vertical diplopia in this specific gaze position. An oculomotor nerve lesion would produce a much broader pattern of ophthalmoplegia affecting multiple muscles, ptosis, and pupillary findings, not this isolated, direction-specific vertical diplopia. An abducens nerve lesion produces horizontal, not vertical, diplopia from isolated lateral rectus weakness. A medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion produces adduction failure on conjugate horizontal gaze, internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not this specific down-and-in vertical diplopia pattern. A frontal eye field lesion affects voluntary saccade generation broadly and would not produce this isolated, muscle-specific vertical diplopia pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vertical diplopia worsening on downward and inward gaze, classically noticed reading or on stairs, localizes specifically to the trochlear nerve/superior oblique muscle. COMLEX: the head-tilt test, worsening diplopia with ipsilateral head tilt, is a classic bedside maneuver for confirming a trochlear nerve palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the midbrain board-vignette algorithm already built for eye movement and pupillary findings, now extended to the full visual motor system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has rightward-beating nystagmus, and vestibular testing is entirely normal, ruling out a peripheral vestibular cause. Which category of structure should be suspected as the source of this nystagmus?","choices":{"A":"The peripheral vestibular labyrinth exclusively, since normal caloric testing does not reliably exclude a peripheral cause","B":"Central ocular motor structures, such as the vestibular nuclei, cerebellum, or brainstem gaze pathways, rather than the peripheral vestibular apparatus","C":"The extraocular muscles themselves, since primary myopathy of these muscles commonly produces this nystagmus pattern","D":"The retina exclusively as the source, since primary retinal disease is by far the most common cause of pathologic nystagmus in adult patients generally","E":"The optic nerve, since demyelination of this structure is the leading cause of nystagmus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When formal vestibular testing is normal, a peripheral vestibular cause of nystagmus is effectively excluded, redirecting suspicion toward central structures, the vestibular nuclei, cerebellum, or brainstem/ocular motor gaze pathways, that can independently generate pathologic nystagmus. The peripheral vestibular labyrinth is not the answer here specifically because normal caloric and vestibular testing does reliably help exclude it as the source in this scenario. The extraocular muscles themselves are not a common primary source of nystagmus, myopathic extraocular disease typically produces weakness or restriction rather than this rhythmic oscillation pattern. Primary retinal disease is not the most common cause of pathologic nystagmus in this clinical context and would not be suggested simply by normal vestibular testing. Optic nerve demyelination is not the leading cause of nystagmus in this context, and normal vestibular testing does not specifically implicate the optic nerve as the source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal vestibular testing with persistent nystagmus should redirect the differential toward central, not peripheral, causes, a key localizing principle. COMLEX: central nystagmus can be purely vertical or change direction with gaze position, features not typically seen with peripheral vestibular nystagmus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the midbrain board-vignette algorithm already built for eye movement and pupillary findings, now extended to the full visual motor system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has a facial skin infection near the nose and develops fever, proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia from cavernous sinus thrombosis. Infection can spread into the dural venous sinuses in this way because these sinuses lack which feature?","choices":{"A":"Venous valves","B":"An endothelial lining","C":"A dural wall","D":"Venous outflow","E":"An arterial supply"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The dural venous sinuses have no valves, so infected venous blood from the face can flow retrograde into the cavernous sinus and cause thrombosis, producing proptosis and palsies of the nerves that pass through it. The sinuses are lined by endothelium and enclosed between dural layers, they drain blood from the eye and superficial cortex onward to the internal jugular vein, and the cavernous portion of the internal carotid artery runs within the cavernous sinus, so none of these is missing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cavernous sinus contents: CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI, postganglionic sympathetic fibers, and the internal carotid artery; causes of cavernous sinus syndrome include pituitary tumor, carotid-cavernous fistula, and thrombosis. COMLEX: Facial infections in the danger triangle need prompt treatment because of this valveless venous route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — the abducens nerve within the cavernous sinus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has a sudden, severe headache and a new right ptosis with a dilated, poorly reactive right pupil and the eye resting down and out. She is fully alert. Which lesion most likely compressed the oculomotor nerve?","choices":{"A":"Diabetic microvascular ischemia of the nerve core","B":"Pineal region tumor pressing on the posterior commissure","C":"Demyelinating plaque in the medial longitudinal fasciculus","D":"Posterior communicating artery aneurysm","E":"Ischemia of the paramedian pontine reticular formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic pupillary fibers run on the periphery of the oculomotor nerve and are affected first by compression, so a pupil-involving third nerve palsy with sudden headache in an alert patient suggests a posterior communicating artery aneurysm. Diabetic microvascular ischemia damages the core of the nerve and typically spares the pupil. A pineal tumor causes upgaze palsy and light-near dissociation, an MLF plaque causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia, and a PPRF lesion causes a horizontal gaze palsy, none with ptosis and a blown pupil.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN III compression (PCom aneurysm, uncal herniation) hits the peripheral parasympathetic fibers first, giving a blown pupil; ischemia (diabetes) hits the central motor fibers and spares the pupil. COMLEX: A pupil-involving third nerve palsy with headache is an emergency needing vascular imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 4 — preganglionic pupillary fibers traveling with CN III","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to vestibulo-ocular premotor neurons, one brainstem region contains optokinetic premotor neurons that receive accessory optic input and drive the extraocular motor nuclei. A lesion there produces severe deficits in both the vestibulo-ocular and optokinetic reflexes. Which region is this?","choices":{"A":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF","B":"The vestibular nuclei of the medulla and pons","C":"The frontal eye field of the middle frontal gyrus","D":"The Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus","E":"The superficial layer of the superior colliculus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Accessory optic system nuclei, driven by retinal slip, project to the vestibular nuclei, which contain optokinetic as well as vestibulo-ocular premotor neurons; lesions of the vestibular nuclei therefore produce severe deficits in both reflexes, and cerebellar pathways adjust the gains of the two. The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF is the vertical saccade burst generator, the frontal eye field directs voluntary saccades, the Edinger-Westphal preganglionic nucleus controls the lens and pupil, and the superficial superior colliculus is visual sensory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VOR compensates for head acceleration with latency under 15 ms; the optokinetic reflex compensates for continued motion signaled by whole-field retinal slip; both converge on the vestibular nuclei. COMLEX: Optokinetic nystagmus is named by its fast phase and is tested with a moving striped drum or cloth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 4 — the vestibular nuclei and their ocular motor projections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"34-year-old woman -> undergoes bedside eye movement testing as part of a routine neurologic exam -> examiner asks her to follow a slowly moving pen tip from left to right without moving her head -> her eyes track the pen smoothly and continuously. Which class of eye movement is being tested, and what is its approximate speed limit?","choices":{"A":"Saccade, which can follow targets moving up to approximately 100 degrees per second","B":"Vergence, which can follow targets moving up to approximately 100 degrees per second","C":"Smooth pursuit, which can follow targets moving up to approximately 100 degrees per second","D":"Smooth pursuit, which can follow targets moving up to approximately 700 degrees per second","E":"Optokinetic response, which can follow targets moving up to approximately 15 degrees per second"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Smooth pursuit is the conjugate eye movement that continuously follows a slow-moving, predictable target, and while it typically operates at 30 degrees per second or less for reliable tracking, it is capable of following targets at speeds up to approximately 100 degrees per second before breaking down into a series of catch-up saccades. A saccade is a rapid, discrete jump to a new target rather than a continuous following movement, so it would not smoothly track a moving pen tip. Vergence is a disconjugate movement adjusting for target distance, not a conjugate tracking movement of a target moving laterally in the same depth plane. Smooth pursuit does not reach speeds as high as 700 degrees per second, that figure describes the much faster saccadic movement class instead. The optokinetic response compensates for whole-field retinal slip during self-motion, not for tracking a single discrete target; its listed speed is also off, since the SDL 45 reading (Table 28.1) gives the optokinetic response a ceiling under about 100 degrees per second, while about 15 degrees per second is the vergence figure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing smooth pursuit, continuous target-following, from saccades, discrete rapid jumps, is a foundational bedside examination concept. COMLEX: testing smooth pursuit at the bedside with a slow-moving target is a standard component of the cranial nerve and cerebellar examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Smooth pursuit and saccades both ultimately converge on the same extraocular motor neurons, though they are generated by distinct premotor circuits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"58-year-old man -> rotates his head briskly to the right while fixating on a stationary target -> his eyes move smoothly to the left to remain on target, with a latency of less than 15 milliseconds -> vestibular testing is subsequently normal. Which class of eye movement was demonstrated, and what sensory input drives it?","choices":{"A":"Optokinetic response, driven by angular acceleration sensed by the vestibular labyrinth","B":"Smooth pursuit, driven by angular acceleration sensed by the vestibular labyrinth","C":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, driven by angular acceleration sensed by the vestibular labyrinth","D":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, driven by whole-field retinal slip sensed by the visual system","E":"Saccade, driven by whole-field retinal slip sensed by the visual system"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The vestibulo-ocular reflex compensates for head acceleration, sensed by the vestibular labyrinth, with an extremely short latency, less than 15 milliseconds, producing compensatory eye movements in the direction opposite the head turn to keep the eyes on target, exactly the pattern and latency described. The optokinetic response is driven by whole-field visual retinal slip, not vestibular labyrinthine input, so it would not match this description. Smooth pursuit follows a moving visual target and is driven by visual, not vestibular, input, and has a longer latency than described here. Describing the vestibulo-ocular reflex as driven by retinal slip misattributes its sensory input, which is specifically vestibular, not visual. A saccade is a discrete, rapid, visually or cognitively triggered movement, not this compensatory, extremely short-latency reflex driven by retinal slip.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vestibulo-ocular reflex's extremely short latency, under 15 milliseconds, distinguishes it from all other eye movement classes and reflects its direct three-neuron reflex arc. COMLEX: caloric testing, irrigating the ear canal with warm or cool water, is the classic bedside method for evaluating the vestibulo-ocular reflex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 6 — Both the vestibulo-ocular reflex and the pupillary light reflex are short-latency brainstem reflex arcs that bypass voluntary cortical control","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"45-year-old woman -> looks at a distant object across the room, then shifts her gaze to read a book held close to her face -> examiner notes simultaneous convergence of both eyes, increased lens curvature, and pupillary constriction occurring together. Which class of eye movement, and which coupled accommodative response, is being demonstrated?","choices":{"A":"Saccade, coupled with the near triad of accommodation and pupillary constriction","B":"Vergence, coupled with the optokinetic reflex and pupillary dilation","C":"Smooth pursuit, coupled with the vestibulo-ocular reflex and pupillary constriction","D":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex, coupled with the near triad of accommodation and pupillary dilation","E":"Vergence, coupled with the near triad of accommodation and pupillary constriction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Shifting gaze from a distant to a near target elicits vergence, a disconjugate movement in which the eyes converge, coupled with the near triad, increased lens curvature via ciliary muscle contraction and pupillary constriction, together optimizing focus and depth of field for the closer target. A saccade is a conjugate, rapid target-redirecting movement, not the disconjugate convergence movement paired with the near triad described here. Vergence is coupled with pupillary constriction, not dilation, as part of the near triad, so pairing it with dilation is incorrect. Smooth pursuit follows a moving target and is not the movement responsible for this near-target convergence response, nor is it coupled with the vestibulo-ocular reflex in this context. The vestibulo-ocular reflex compensates for head movement and is unrelated to this near-target convergence response, and it is not paired with pupillary dilation in the near triad, which specifically produces constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the near triad, convergence, accommodation, and pupillary constriction, occurring together defines vergence movements and is a classic bedside testing sequence. COMLEX: test the near triad by having the patient switch focus between a distant object and a target brought close to the nose while observing all three components.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 6 — The pupillary constriction component of the near triad is driven by the same supraoculomotor area premotor neurons that also drive convergence, distinct from the pretectal light reflex pathway","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"29-year-old man -> sustains a small ischemic stroke -> exam shows that when he attempts to look to the left, both eyes fail to move left, but leftward vestibulo-ocular reflex testing produces normal leftward eye movement -> convergence and vertical gaze are entirely normal. Which structure is most likely damaged, and why does the vestibulo-ocular reflex remain intact?","choices":{"A":"The left abducens nucleus, sparing the paramedian pontine reticular formation, which the vestibulo-ocular reflex uses as its final common pathway independent of the abducens nucleus","B":"The left medial longitudinal fasciculus, sparing the paramedian pontine reticular formation, which the vestibulo-ocular reflex uses as its final common pathway independent of the MLF","C":"The left oculomotor nucleus, sparing the paramedian pontine reticular formation, which the vestibulo-ocular reflex uses as its final common pathway independent of the oculomotor nucleus","D":"The right paramedian pontine reticular formation, sparing the abducens nucleus and nerve, which the vestibulo-ocular reflex uses as its final common pathway independent of the PPRF","E":"The left paramedian pontine reticular formation, sparing the abducens nucleus and nerve, which the vestibulo-ocular reflex uses as its final common pathway independent of the PPRF"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A left PPRF lesion abolishes voluntary and reflexive leftward saccadic gaze, since the PPRF generates the phasic pulse driving the abducens nucleus for horizontal gaze, but the vestibulo-ocular reflex reaches the same abducens and oculomotor motor neurons through a separate vestibular nuclei pathway that does not require the PPRF, so it remains intact, a classic dissociation localizing the lesion to the PPRF rather than the final common motor pathway itself. An abducens nucleus lesion would also abolish the vestibulo-ocular reflex's leftward movement, since the abducens nucleus is the shared final common pathway for both voluntary and reflexive horizontal gaze, contradicting the preserved VOR described. The oculomotor nucleus is not the structure responsible for leftward gaze failure in this horizontal gaze palsy, and it is not the specific structure whose sparing explains the intact VOR here. A right, rather than left, PPRF lesion would impair rightward, not leftward, gaze, the wrong side for this presentation. A left MLF lesion would selectively impair adduction of one eye during conjugate horizontal gaze, not this bilateral failure of leftward gaze with preserved VOR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: preserved vestibulo-ocular reflex despite absent voluntary horizontal gaze localizes the lesion to the supranuclear PPRF pathway rather than the abducens nucleus or nerve itself. COMLEX: the doll's eye maneuver, testing the VOR in an unconscious patient, is a classic bedside technique exploiting this same PPRF-versus-final-common-pathway distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — This same PPRF-versus-abducens-nucleus distinction parallels the abducens nerve-versus-nucleus localization logic used to differentiate isolated from combined gaze deficits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"52-year-old woman -> presents with a destructive lesion of the right frontal eye field from a stroke -> in the acute phase, examiners note that both eyes deviate toward the right side and the patient cannot voluntarily look to the left. Which mechanism best explains this acute gaze deviation?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the right frontal eye field removes its crossed excitatory drive to the left PPRF, leaving the unopposed left frontal eye field to drive gaze toward the right, the side of the lesion","B":"The right frontal eye field lesion directly damages the left PPRF, producing this gaze deviation through a single shared vascular territory","C":"Loss of the right frontal eye field removes inhibition of the right PPRF, causing unopposed rightward gaze deviation through disinhibition","D":"The right frontal eye field lesion damages the right abducens nucleus directly, producing this gaze deviation through local nuclear injury","E":"Loss of the right frontal eye field paradoxically increases excitatory drive to the right PPRF, producing this gaze deviation through increased ipsilateral output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The frontal eye field normally sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF to drive saccades toward the opposite side, so destruction of the right frontal eye field removes its crossed drive to the left PPRF, and the intact left frontal eye field's unopposed crossed drive to the right PPRF produces gaze deviation toward the right, the side of the lesion, a classic acute stroke finding, \"the eyes look toward the lesion.\" The right frontal eye field and left PPRF are not typically within the same vascular territory, and the mechanism is one of lost crossed excitatory input, not direct shared-territory infarction. The frontal eye field does not normally inhibit the ipsilateral PPRF, so its loss would not work through disinhibition of the right PPRF. The frontal eye field lesion described is cortical, not a direct pontine nuclear injury, so it would not directly damage the abducens nucleus. Loss of a structure cannot paradoxically increase its own excitatory output to another region, this describes a physiologically implausible mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"eyes look toward the lesion\" in an acute destructive frontal eye field stroke, versus \"eyes look away from the lesion\" with an irritative/seizure focus, is a classic testable distinction. COMLEX: this gaze deviation typically resolves within days as compensatory mechanisms restore some contralateral gaze capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 7 — This acute gaze deviation is itself a localizing sign, illustrating how eye position at rest can point directly to the side and type of underlying lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man recovers well from a stroke that destroyed one frontal eye field, with few remaining eye movement problems. Months later a second lesion destroys the superior colliculus on the same side, and he now has profound difficulty directing his eyes toward targets. What best explains this sequence?","choices":{"A":"The frontal eye field projects directly to the abducens motor neurons","B":"The superior colliculus is the sole source of voluntary saccades","C":"Each structure compensates for loss of the other but not both","D":"The second lesion also destroyed the nucleus prepositus hypoglossi","E":"The colliculus relays all visual input to the primary visual cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The frontal eye field directs voluntary and memory-guided saccades and the superior colliculus directs reflexive orienting saccades; after loss of either, the remaining structure compensates and few deficits persist, but loss of both produces profound visuomotor impairment. The frontal eye field acts through the colliculus and the gaze centers rather than directly on abducens motor neurons, and the colliculus is not the sole source of voluntary saccades. A prepositus lesion would cause gaze-evoked drift, and the colliculus projects to the lateral geniculate and pulvinar rather than relaying all visual input to cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortical eye fields (frontal, supplementary, parietal) and the superior colliculus all drive contralateral saccades through the PPRF and riMLF; loss of any one causes few lasting signs. COMLEX: An acute frontal eye field lesion causes transient gaze deviation toward the side of the lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — the frontal eye field among the cortical motor regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"44-year-old woman -> presents with binocular horizontal diplopia worse at distance -> exam shows the right eye fails to abduct past midline, but adduction, elevation, and depression are entirely normal in both eyes -> no other cranial nerve or brainstem finding is present. Which localization best fits, and what does the absence of any other finding suggest about lesion size?","choices":{"A":"Isolated right abducens nucleus lesion, suggesting a small, focal lesion sparing the abducens nerve and adjacent brainstem structures","B":"Right medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion, suggesting a small, focal lesion sparing the abducens nerve and nucleus","C":"Left abducens nerve palsy, suggesting a small, focal lesion sparing the abducens nucleus and adjacent brainstem structures","D":"Isolated right abducens nerve palsy, suggesting a small, focal lesion sparing the abducens nucleus and adjacent brainstem structures","E":"Combined right abducens nerve and oculomotor nerve palsy, suggesting a larger lesion involving the cavernous sinus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isolated failure of right abduction with entirely normal adduction, elevation, depression, and no other cranial nerve or brainstem finding localizes cleanly to an isolated right abducens nerve palsy, since the nerve carries only lateral rectus motor axons without the internuclear neurons that would also affect the contralateral eye if the nucleus were involved, and the absence of any other deficit suggests a small, focal process rather than a larger lesion affecting adjacent structures. An abducens nucleus lesion would also impair the contralateral eye's adduction on conjugate gaze, which is explicitly normal here, making this localization inconsistent with the findings. A right MLF lesion would impair right eye adduction on leftward gaze, not right eye abduction, an entirely different movement and mechanism. A left abducens nerve palsy would impair left, not right, abduction, the wrong eye for this presentation. Combined abducens and oculomotor involvement would produce additional findings, ptosis, mydriasis, or other extraocular movement restriction, none of which are present here, arguing against a larger cavernous sinus process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated, pure abduction deficit with an entirely normal remaining exam is the classic presentation of a focal abducens nerve palsy, often from microvascular ischemia in older patients with vascular risk factors. COMLEX: always complete a full cranial nerve exam even when the presenting complaint seems isolated, since additional findings would change the localization significantly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 8 — Isolated abducens nerve palsies can also result from cavernous sinus or Dorello canal pathology, given the nerve's long, vulnerable intracranial course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a pituitary macroadenoma has horizontal diplopia from a right abduction deficit, together with a slightly smaller right pupil and mild right ptosis. Facial sweating is normal. The other eye movements are intact. Where is a single lesion most likely?","choices":{"A":"Right abducens nucleus in the facial colliculus","B":"Right medial longitudinal fasciculus in the pons","C":"Right lateral medulla with descending sympathetic fibers","D":"Right superior cervical ganglion in the neck","E":"Right cavernous sinus, where sympathetics join CN VI"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers enter the cranium on the internal carotid artery and run briefly with the abducens nerve in the cavernous sinus before joining CN III and V, so a sinus lesion can combine a sixth nerve palsy with a postganglionic Horner pattern that spares facial sweating; pituitary tumors are a cause. An abducens nucleus lesion would add a contralateral medial rectus deficit, an MLF lesion causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia, a lateral medullary lesion brings crossed sensory and bulbar signs, and a superior cervical ganglion lesion would not explain the abduction deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abducens palsy plus Horner syndrome localizes to the cavernous sinus; cavernous sinus syndrome adds variable CN III, IV, V1, and V2 signs. COMLEX: Ptosis in Horner syndrome is partial because only the superior tarsal muscle is denervated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 7 — Horner syndrome localization along the sympathetic pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension has sudden horizontal diplopia. The left eye cannot abduct, but the right eye adducts normally on leftward gaze and convergence is intact. He also has weakness of the right arm and leg with hyperreflexia. Which lesion best explains this crossed pattern?","choices":{"A":"Left abducens nucleus in the dorsal pontine tegmentum","B":"Left medial longitudinal fasciculus near the abducens nucleus","C":"Left abducens nerve within the cavernous sinus","D":"Right corticospinal tract in the cervical spinal cord","E":"Left exiting abducens fibers and adjacent corticospinal tract"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Abducens fibers run ventrally from the nucleus to exit near the midline at the pontomedullary junction and pass beside the corticospinal tract, so a paramedian basilar branch infarct can paralyze ipsilateral abduction and cause contralateral hemiplegia, an alternating hemiplegia. A nucleus lesion would also impair the opposite medial rectus on gaze toward the lesion, which is normal here. An MLF lesion impairs adduction, a cavernous sinus lesion would not cause hemiparesis, and a cervical cord lesion could not affect an eye.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossed findings, an ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy with contralateral hemiplegia, localize to the brainstem at the level of that nerve; abducens fibers plus corticospinal tract mark the caudal ventral pons. COMLEX: An isolated lateral rectus palsy with intact contralateral adduction points to the nerve or its fascicles rather than the nucleus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — medial pontine syndromes combining CN VI and corticospinal deficits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"31-year-old woman -> known relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis -> new binocular diplopia -> exam confirms bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, with impaired adduction in whichever eye is on the adducting side during horizontal gaze testing in both directions, nystagmus of the abducting eye each time, and fully intact convergence bilaterally. What does this specific bilateral pattern most strongly suggest about the underlying process?","choices":{"A":"A demyelinating process affecting both medial longitudinal fasciculi, since bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia is particularly characteristic of multiple sclerosis compared with the more commonly unilateral vascular causes","B":"A single small vessel ischemic lesion affecting both medial longitudinal fasciculi, since small vessel disease characteristically produces bilateral rather than unilateral findings","C":"A structural compressive lesion at the pontomedullary junction, since compressive lesions typically produce symmetric, bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia","D":"A neuromuscular junction disorder affecting both medial rectus muscles independently, since bilateral neuromuscular disease classically produces this pattern","E":"A normal, physiologic variant of eye movement control with no pathologic significance, since bilateral findings of this kind are generally considered a benign, entirely normal finding in most patients"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, affecting both medial longitudinal fasciculi, is a particularly characteristic finding in multiple sclerosis, since demyelinating plaques can affect both sides of this midline-adjacent structure, whereas the vascular causes of internuclear ophthalmoplegia, typically small vessel ischemic disease, more commonly produce unilateral findings, making this bilateral pattern a useful clinical clue pointing toward a demyelinating rather than vascular etiology. Small vessel ischemic disease, while it can occasionally cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia, is not characteristically bilateral and more typically produces a unilateral lesion, making this an inconsistent explanation for the described bilateral pattern. A structural compressive lesion would need to affect two separate, non-contiguous midline structures nearly symmetrically, which is atypical for most compressive processes, structural lesions more often produce unilateral or asymmetric findings. A peripheral neuromuscular junction disorder such as myasthenia gravis can mimic internuclear ophthalmoplegia, but it does not follow the specific abducting-eye nystagmus pattern this precisely and would be expected to show fatigability rather than this fixed pattern. This bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia pattern is a genuine, clinically significant pathologic finding, not a benign or normal physiologic variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia strongly favoring multiple sclerosis over vascular causes is a frequently tested clinical pearl connecting anatomy to etiology. COMLEX: always ask about other neurologic symptoms suggestive of demyelinating disease, such as optic neuritis or sensory changes, when bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — This bilateral MLF pattern is distinguished from an abducens nucleus lesion by the fact that abduction itself remains entirely normal on both sides, only adduction is impaired","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"48-year-old man -> sustains a paramedian pontine infarct -> exam shows the right eye cannot move horizontally in either direction, while the left eye can abduct but cannot adduct on any attempted horizontal gaze -> vertical eye movements and convergence are both entirely normal in both eyes. What is this pattern called, and which two structures must be simultaneously damaged to produce it?","choices":{"A":"One-and-a-half syndrome, requiring simultaneous damage to the right oculomotor nucleus and the right abducens nerve as they pass through the same region","B":"One-and-a-half syndrome, requiring simultaneous damage to the right abducens nucleus and the crossing internuclear fibers from the left abducens nucleus as they pass through the same region","C":"Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, requiring simultaneous damage to both the right and left medial longitudinal fasciculi","D":"Parinaud syndrome, requiring simultaneous damage to the posterior commissure and both interstitial nuclei of Cajal in the dorsal midbrain","E":"Foville syndrome, requiring simultaneous damage to the right abducens fibers and the adjacent right corticospinal tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"One-and-a-half syndrome results from a single paramedian pontine lesion that simultaneously damages the right abducens nucleus, abolishing rightward gaze in both eyes (right-eye abduction and, via its internuclear neurons, left-eye adduction), and the already-crossed internuclear fibers from the left abducens nucleus ascending in the right MLF, abolishing right-eye adduction, leaving the left eye able only to abduct, one full horizontal gaze palsy plus one internuclear ophthalmoplegia, while vertical gaze and convergence, mediated by separate midbrain circuitry, remain untouched. The oculomotor nucleus and abducens nerve are not the two structures whose combined damage defines one-and-a-half syndrome, which specifically requires abducens nucleus plus crossing internuclear fiber involvement. Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia would impair adduction on both sides while sparing abduction bilaterally, not this pattern of complete right-sided horizontal gaze loss combined with left-sided adduction failure alone. Parinaud syndrome is a distinct, dorsal midbrain vertical gaze syndrome from posterior commissure compression, unrelated to this pontine horizontal gaze pattern. Foville syndrome combines abducens fiber and corticospinal tract damage to produce crossed hemiplegia with abduction paralysis, a different combination of structures than the one producing one-and-a-half syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: one-and-a-half syndrome requires damage to two specific, anatomically adjacent structures in the paramedian pons, the abducens nucleus and the crossing contralateral internuclear fibers, tested together in a single lesion. COMLEX: the surviving movement, abduction of the eye contralateral to the lesion, is the single most important clue for recognizing this pattern on exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — One-and-a-half syndrome can be understood as a right abducens nucleus lesion, producing the nucleus-level pattern, combined with an additional left MLF-type deficit from the crossing fibers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"36-year-old woman -> presents with horizontal diplopia -> exam shows normal adduction and abduction of both eyes when tested with smooth pursuit and vestibulo-ocular reflex maneuvers, but abnormal adduction of the right eye specifically during rapid, voluntary conjugate horizontal saccades to the left -> convergence is fully normal. What does this dissociation, present only during saccades, best suggest about the underlying lesion?","choices":{"A":"A complete oculomotor nerve palsy, since this pattern is a classic presentation of complete third nerve dysfunction affecting only saccadic eye movements","B":"A complete abducens nerve palsy, since this pattern is a classic presentation of complete sixth nerve dysfunction affecting only saccadic eye movements","C":"A structural lesion of the extraocular muscle itself, since myopathic disease characteristically spares smooth pursuit while impairing only saccadic movements in this presentation","D":"A partial or early medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion, since internuclear ophthalmoplegia can selectively slow saccadic adduction, sometimes with relative preservation of adduction driven by smooth pursuit or vestibular inputs","E":"A normal finding with no localizing significance, since minor discrepancies between saccadic and pursuit-driven movements are expected in healthy individuals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Early or partial internuclear ophthalmoplegia can produce a subtle, saccade-selective slowing or mild impairment of adduction that may be less apparent, or even appear relatively preserved, when adduction is instead driven by smooth pursuit or vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways, since these systems can partially compensate through parallel premotor routes to the same medial rectus motor neurons, making saccadic testing specifically the most sensitive way to unmask a subtle MLF lesion. A complete oculomotor nerve palsy would impair adduction across all movement types, saccades, pursuit, and vestibulo-ocular testing alike, not selectively during saccades only, since the final common motor pathway itself would be affected regardless of which premotor system drives it. A complete abducens nerve palsy would impair abduction, not adduction, and would also affect all movement types equally, not just saccades. Extraocular myopathy typically produces a fairly consistent restriction or weakness across movement types rather than this specific saccade-selective sparing pattern tied to premotor pathway redundancy. This saccade-selective dissociation is a genuine, clinically meaningful localizing finding for early MLF pathology, not a normal, insignificant variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: saccade-selective adduction slowing, with relative sparing during smooth pursuit or vestibulo-ocular reflex testing, is a subtle but classic sign of early or partial internuclear ophthalmoplegia. COMLEX: when internuclear ophthalmoplegia is suspected but not obvious on routine testing, specifically ask the patient to make rapid saccades between two targets to unmask subtle adduction slowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — This dissociation reflects the fact that saccades, smooth pursuit, and the vestibulo-ocular reflex reach the same motor neurons through partially separate premotor pathways converging on the MLF","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":2,"stem":"Saccades are often accompanied by orienting movements of the head. Neurons in or near the PPRF, the rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF, and the interstitial nucleus of Cajal receive collicular and cortical input and project both to extraocular motor neurons and to the cervical spinal cord. What do these spinal projections provide?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic drive to the pupillary dilator through the ciliospinal center","B":"Reticulospinal and interstitiospinal control of head with eye movement","C":"Corticospinal control of fine finger movements during visually guided reaching","D":"Ascending proprioceptive feedback from the neck to the vertical gaze center","E":"Parasympathetic drive to the lacrimal gland during sustained upward fixation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells in or near the PPRF, riMLF, and interstitial nucleus of Cajal project to the cervical cord as reticulospinal and interstitiospinal fibers, coordinating head movements with saccades so that orienting gaze shifts combine eye and head. Sympathetic pupillary drive arises from preganglionic neurons in the upper thoracic cord, and fine finger control is corticospinal. These projections are descending motor fibers rather than ascending proprioceptive pathways, and lacrimal secretion is driven by the facial nerve, not by the gaze centers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertical gaze: riMLF supplies the burst (pulse), interstitial nucleus of Cajal the tonic hold (step); crossed connections run through the posterior commissure. COMLEX: Gaze-center lesions can disturb head posture along with eye movements, such as abnormal head tilt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — brainstem descending pathways to the cervical cord, including tectospinal and reticulospinal tracts","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a small infarct confined to the left paramedian pontine reticular formation, sparing the abducens nucleus and the rostral midbrain. When asked to look up and to the left with a single quick eye movement, what is expected?","choices":{"A":"Neither the upward nor the leftward component occurs","B":"Both components occur normally because of the riMLF","C":"The upward component occurs but the leftward component fails","D":"The leftward component occurs but the upward component fails","E":"The eyes move up and to the right instead of to the left"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oblique saccades are produced by the vertical and horizontal gaze centers working in concert; the riMLF in the rostral midbrain still generates the vertical burst, but the damaged left PPRF can no longer generate the burst for a leftward saccade, so the eyes move up without the leftward component. Loss of both components would require additional rostral midbrain damage, and a normal leftward component is impossible without the left PPRF. A rightward deviation is not expected, because the intact right PPRF is not being asked to fire and the lesion removes drive rather than reversing it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Horizontal gaze center, PPRF in the pons; vertical gaze center, riMLF at the midbrain-diencephalic junction; oblique saccades need both. COMLEX: PPRF lesions abolish ipsilateral horizontal saccades but spare vertical saccades and vergence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — the PPRF as the horizontal gaze center in the pathway to the abducens nucleus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":2,"stem":"63-year-old man -> presents with an isolated lesion confined to one interstitial nucleus of Cajal, sparing the riMLF and posterior commissure bilaterally -> exam shows that vertical saccades are generated with normal speed and amplitude, but the eyes drift back toward primary position after each vertical saccade, requiring repeated corrective movements to maintain eccentric vertical gaze. Which specific component of vertical gaze is disrupted by this lesion?","choices":{"A":"The phasic burst signal that generates the initial pulse of vertical saccadic movement itself, entirely unrelated to gaze holding","B":"The crossed connections that coordinate bilateral vertical gaze between the two sides of the brainstem","C":"The afferent limb of the pupillary light reflex, which shares this anatomic pathway with vertical gaze","D":"The tonic step signal that normally converts the phasic saccadic pulse into sustained motor neuron activity maintaining eccentric vertical eye position","E":"The supranuclear cortical input from the frontal eye field that initiates voluntary vertical saccades"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The interstitial nucleus of Cajal provides the tonic step signal that converts the phasic saccadic pulse into sustained motor neuron activity needed to hold eccentric vertical eye position, so its lesion produces normal-appearing vertical saccades, since the riMLF-generated pulse is intact, but the eyes drift back toward center afterward, generating vertical gaze-evoked nystagmus, exactly this presentation. The phasic burst signal is intact in this scenario, since the saccades themselves are generated with normal speed and amplitude, so a burst deficit does not explain this specific drift-back phenomenon. The crossed connections coordinating bilateral vertical gaze travel through the posterior commissure, which is explicitly spared in this vignette, and their disruption would produce combined bilateral deficits or torsion, not this unilateral drift pattern. The pupillary light reflex afferent limb travels through the optic nerve, tract, and pretectum, an entirely separate pathway from the interstitial nucleus of Cajal, and is not affected by this lesion. The frontal eye field's supranuclear input initiates voluntary saccades broadly but is not the specific structure responsible for maintaining eccentric gaze position once a saccade has already occurred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal saccade generation with subsequent drift back to center localizes specifically to loss of the tonic step function, here from the interstitial nucleus of Cajal for vertical gaze. COMLEX: vertical gaze-evoked nystagmus from this mechanism can be distinguished from horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus, from nucleus prepositus hypoglossi dysfunction, purely by testing which direction of gaze provokes the drift.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Comparing this vertical tonic integrator failure to the horizontal nucleus prepositus hypoglossi failure demonstrates the parallel pulse-and-step organization shared by both gaze systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl45_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a right homonymous hemianopia from a lesion confined to the left optic tract. Light is shone into each eye in turn. Which pupillary finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Direct and consensual responses are present from either eye","B":"Both pupils fail to constrict when light enters the left eye","C":"Both pupils fail to constrict when light enters the right eye","D":"The left pupil fails to constrict to light shone in either eye","E":"The right pupil is fixed and dilated to light in either eye"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because roughly half of the pupillary fibers cross in the chiasm and the olivary pretectal nucleus projects bilaterally, a lesion of one optic tract or pretectum does not abolish either the direct or consensual response; the reflexes may be weaker, which is not easily seen clinically. Loss of both responses from one eye occurs with a complete optic nerve lesion, a relative afferent defect with partial optic nerve damage, and loss of both responses in one eye with an oculomotor nucleus or nerve lesion, none of which is present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pupillary light reflex is a four-neuron arc: retinal ganglion cell, olivary pretectal nucleus, Edinger-Westphal preganglionic neuron, ciliary ganglion neuron. COMLEX: A large dorsal midbrain lesion weakens pupils bilaterally, whereas an optic tract lesion leaves them clinically intact.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 6 — afferent versus efferent pupillary defects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":2,"stem":"71-year-old woman -> presents with anisocoria noted incidentally -> exam shows both pupils constrict normally and equally when light is shone into either eye individually, with normal direct and consensual responses bilaterally, but the left pupil is persistently larger than the right at baseline in dim light -> full oculomotor and eyelid examination is entirely normal. Which conclusion about the pupillary light reflex pathway is best supported by these findings?","choices":{"A":"This pattern indicates a relative afferent pupillary defect in the left eye, requiring urgent formal optic nerve evaluation","B":"This pattern indicates a right oculomotor nerve lesion affecting the efferent limb of the pupillary light reflex on the right","C":"This pattern indicates bilateral pretectal olivary nucleus dysfunction affecting the relay structure of the light reflex on both sides equally","D":"The pupillary light reflex pathway is fully intact bilaterally, and this anisocoria most likely reflects a benign asymmetry of sympathetic tone or physiologic anisocoria rather than a light reflex defect","E":"This pattern indicates a lesion of the posterior commissure disrupting the decussating fibers of the pupillary light reflex pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because both pupils show normal, equal direct and consensual responses to light in either eye, the entire four-neuron pupillary light reflex arc, afferent retinal input, pretectal relay, and bilateral efferent Edinger-Westphal/oculomotor/ciliary ganglion output, is functioning normally, so this benign, size-only anisocoria without any reflex abnormality is more consistent with physiologic anisocoria or mild sympathetic tone asymmetry than any structural light reflex lesion. A relative afferent pupillary defect would produce asymmetric, diminished direct and consensual responses specifically when testing the affected eye, not this pattern of fully normal, equal responses bilaterally. A right oculomotor nerve lesion would be expected to impair the right pupil's constriction to light and would typically also produce ptosis or other extraocular findings, none of which are present, and the exam is explicitly normal on the right side. Bilateral pretectal olivary nucleus dysfunction would be expected to weaken the light reflex responses themselves, not leave them entirely normal as described here. A posterior commissure lesion disrupting decussating light reflex fibers would be expected to weaken pupillary responses, particularly consensual ones, not leave the entire reflex arc entirely intact and normal as found on this exam.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that a fully intact, symmetric light reflex with isolated baseline pupil size difference favors a benign, physiologic cause over a structural lesion is an important clinical reasoning skill. COMLEX: physiologic anisocoria, a small baseline size difference with normal reflexes, is present in a meaningful minority of the general population and should not automatically trigger extensive workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — Systematically testing each component of the pupillary reflex arc, rather than baseline size alone, is exactly the localization skill this related objective emphasizes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":2,"stem":"As a patient shifts gaze from a distant sign to her own hand, her eyes converge to point both foveae at the nearer target. Besides blur and the vergence angle, which visual cue detected by binocular cells in visual cortex is an appropriate signal for controlling vergence?","choices":{"A":"Whole-field retinal slip across the retina","B":"Overall luminance falling on the retina","C":"Retinal disparity between the two images","D":"Color contrast between target and background","E":"Melanopsin activation in ganglion cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Retinal disparity, the difference in where an image falls on each retina, activates binocular cells in visual cortex and is an appropriate signal for vergence, along with blur and the vergence angle itself, which is used to adjust accommodation. Whole-field retinal slip drives the optokinetic reflex, overall luminance and melanopsin ganglion cells drive the pupillary light reflex, and color contrast is not described as a vergence cue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Near triad: convergence, increased lens curvature, pupillary constriction, organized by premotor neurons in the supraoculomotor area and midbrain reticular formation. COMLEX: Vergence and accommodation can be dissociated by closing one eye and bringing the target straight toward the open eye.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 3 — the retinogeniculostriate pathway carrying binocular input to visual cortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"54-year-old man -> sudden-onset vertigo and horizontal diplopia -> exam shows left-beating nystagmus, inability to abduct the left eye, left facial weakness, and left-sided hearing loss, with normal pupils bilaterally -> MRI confirms an infarct. Using nystagmus direction, diplopia pattern, and associated cranial nerve findings, which vessel territory best localizes this presentation?","choices":{"A":"PICA territory, since the combination of left abduction failure, left facial weakness, and left hearing loss localizes to the lateral pons, the PICA territory, with normal pupils arguing against oculomotor or midbrain involvement","B":"SCA territory, since the combination of left abduction failure, left facial weakness, and left hearing loss localizes to the lateral pons, the SCA territory, with normal pupils arguing against oculomotor or midbrain involvement","C":"AICA territory, since the combination of left abduction failure, left facial weakness, and left hearing loss localizes to the lateral pons, the AICA territory, with normal pupils arguing against oculomotor or midbrain involvement","D":"Posterior cerebral artery territory, since the combination of left abduction failure, left facial weakness, and left hearing loss localizes to the midbrain, with normal pupils arguing against oculomotor involvement","E":"Anterior spinal artery territory, since the combination of left abduction failure, left facial weakness, and left hearing loss localizes to the paramedian medulla, with normal pupils arguing against oculomotor involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AICA supplies the lateral pons, where the abducens fascicles, facial nerve fascicles, and vestibulocochlear nerve all travel together, so an AICA territory infarct produces exactly this combination of ipsilateral abduction failure, facial weakness, and hearing loss with vestibular nystagmus, while normal pupils are expected since AICA territory does not include the midbrain oculomotor structures. PICA territory supplies the posterolateral medulla, producing lateral medullary syndrome with dysphagia, hoarseness, and crossed sensory findings, not this specific combination of abducens, facial, and cochlear nerve involvement, which points to the pons rather than the medulla. SCA territory supplies the superior cerebellum and deep nuclei, producing severe ataxia with Horner syndrome and crossed pain/temperature loss, not this specific triad of cranial nerve VI, VII, and VIII findings. The posterior cerebral artery supplies occipital cortex and thalamic/midbrain structures, and while some midbrain branches can affect the oculomotor nerve, this vessel does not explain facial weakness or hearing loss, and the presentation described localizes to the pons, not the midbrain. The anterior spinal artery supplies the paramedian medulla, producing tongue weakness and contralateral limb weakness, not this specific combination of lateral pontine cranial nerve findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combining abducens palsy, facial weakness, and hearing loss with vertigo and nystagmus is the classic AICA territory triad, distinguishing it from the medullary PICA pattern and the nuclear SCA pattern. COMLEX: normal pupils are a useful negative finding here, helping exclude midbrain oculomotor nerve or nucleus involvement from the differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — This same AICA territory pattern was already established for the cerebellum, now extended here to its characteristic cranial nerve and ocular motor findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a small infarct of the right oculomotor nucleus. He has weakness of the right medial rectus, inferior rectus, and inferior oblique, but also weak elevation of the left eye and ptosis of both upper lids. Which feature of the oculomotor nucleus explains the findings outside the right eye?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectus motor neurons project contralaterally and levator neurons bilaterally","B":"Medial rectus motor neurons project contralaterally and levator neurons bilaterally","C":"Inferior oblique motor neurons project contralaterally and levator neurons ipsilaterally","D":"Edinger-Westphal neurons project bilaterally to both ciliary ganglia","E":"Abducens internuclear neurons cross in the nucleus to reach both eyes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oculomotor motor neurons project ipsilaterally except that superior rectus motor neurons project to the contralateral eye and levator palpebrae neurons, grouped in the dorsal midline caudal central subdivision, project bilaterally; so a nuclear lesion weakens elevation of the opposite eye and causes bilateral ptosis. Medial rectus and inferior oblique neurons project ipsilaterally, Edinger-Westphal preganglionic fibers travel with the ipsilateral nerve, and abducens internuclear axons reach the contralateral medial rectus subnucleus through the MLF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN III nucleus: ipsilateral MR, IR, IO; contralateral SR; bilateral levator (caudal central subdivision). COMLEX: Bilateral ptosis or contralateral elevation weakness distinguishes a nuclear third nerve lesion from a fascicular or peripheral one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 3 — localizing lesions of CN III and its nucleus in the midbrain","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"42-year-old woman -> presents with binocular diplopia and reports that images are displaced vertically and appear tilted relative to one another, worse when she looks down and to the left -> she has learned to tilt her head to the left to minimize the diplopia -> pupils are normal and there is no ptosis or other extraocular deficit. Using the specific gaze position that worsens diplopia and the compensatory head tilt, which cranial nerve is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Right trochlear nerve","B":"Right oculomotor nerve","C":"Right abducens nerve","D":"Left trochlear nerve","E":"Left oculomotor nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A right trochlear nerve palsy weakens the right superior oblique muscle, whose actions include depression and intorsion, so diplopia characteristically worsens on downward and inward (down and to the left, with the right eye adducted) gaze, and patients often adopt a compensatory head tilt away from the affected side, to the left, to reduce the resulting vertical and torsional image misalignment, precisely matching this presentation with entirely normal pupils and no ptosis since only this one isolated small muscle is affected. A right oculomotor nerve lesion would be expected to produce ptosis, pupillary mydriasis, and a much broader pattern of ophthalmoplegia affecting multiple muscles, not this isolated, direction-specific vertical/torsional diplopia with an entirely normal pupil and eyelid exam. A right abducens nerve lesion produces purely horizontal diplopia from isolated lateral rectus weakness, not this vertical, torsional pattern worsened by downward and inward gaze. A left trochlear nerve palsy would be expected to produce diplopia worsened by downward and inward gaze of the left eye, with a compensatory head tilt to the right, the opposite side from what is described here. A left oculomotor nerve lesion would, like a right-sided oculomotor lesion, produce ptosis and pupillary involvement on the left, findings that are explicitly absent in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vertical/torsional diplopia worsened by downward and inward gaze, with a compensatory head tilt away from the affected side, is the classic bedside presentation of a trochlear nerve palsy. COMLEX: the three-step head-tilt test, identifying which eye is hypertropic, which gaze direction worsens it, and which head tilt worsens it, is the definitive bedside localization method for trochlear nerve palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — This vignette applies the same nystagmus/diplopia/pupil-based localization framework this objective is built around, using gaze position and head tilt as the key diagnostic clues","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":2,"stem":"19-year-old woman -> acute-onset diplopia and unsteady gait over two days -> exam shows bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia with preserved convergence, and separately, an unrelated episode of vision loss in one eye three months earlier that resolved spontaneously -> MRI shows multiple periventricular white matter lesions. Which high-yield clinical pattern and underlying diagnosis best fit this combined presentation?","choices":{"A":"Parinaud syndrome, and given the bilateral pattern, prior optic neuritis episode, and periventricular white matter lesions, this is most consistent with multiple sclerosis","B":"Cavernous sinus syndrome, and given the bilateral pattern, prior optic neuritis episode, and periventricular white matter lesions, this is most consistent with multiple sclerosis","C":"Uncal herniation, and given the bilateral pattern, prior optic neuritis episode, and periventricular white matter lesions, this is most consistent with multiple sclerosis","D":"Vestibular nystagmus from peripheral vestibular disease, and given the bilateral pattern, prior optic neuritis episode, and periventricular white matter lesions, this is most consistent with multiple sclerosis","E":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia, and given the bilateral pattern, prior optic neuritis episode, and periventricular white matter lesions, this is most consistent with multiple sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia with preserved convergence, combined with a prior episode of optic neuritis and periventricular white matter lesions on MRI, forms a classic constellation strongly suggestive of multiple sclerosis, since both the bilateral MLF involvement and prior optic nerve demyelination reflect the same underlying disseminated demyelinating process. Parinaud syndrome involves upward gaze palsy, eyelid retraction, and light-near dissociation from dorsal midbrain compression, an entirely different clinical pattern than the adduction-selective deficit with preserved convergence described here. Cavernous sinus syndrome would be expected to produce combined multiple cranial nerve deficits, III, IV, VI, and trigeminal sensory loss, together, not this isolated bilateral MLF pattern with normal convergence and no facial sensory involvement. Uncal herniation produces a progressively worsening, pupil-involving oculomotor palsy in an acutely deteriorating patient, not this bilateral, adduction-selective pattern with normal pupils and a subacute, relapsing course. Vestibular nystagmus from peripheral disease would be expected to show a peripheral vertigo pattern with normal extraocular movements otherwise, not this specific bilateral adduction failure with preserved abduction and convergence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia plus a history of optic neuritis as a classic combined presentation of multiple sclerosis ties together two separate high-yield findings into one diagnosis. COMLEX: this same combination, optic neuritis plus internuclear ophthalmoplegia, is sometimes highlighted as part of a broader multiple sclerosis symptom cluster on board exams.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 4 — The bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia mechanism itself was established in detail under this related objective, now applied here within its most classic clinical context","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old man with Parkinson disease has a striking lack of spontaneous eye movements and a reduced blink rate. GABAergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars reticulata normally fire spontaneously and pause just before each saccade. Which projection explains his paucity of spontaneous saccades?","choices":{"A":"Unmodulated nigrostriatal excitation of the caudate nucleus","B":"Loss of pallidothalamic inhibition of the ventral lateral nucleus","C":"Excess cerebellar inhibition of the vestibular nuclei","D":"Unmodulated nigrotectal inhibition of the superior colliculus","E":"Loss of corticonuclear drive to the oculomotor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nigrotectal neurons of the pars reticulata tonically inhibit the intermediate layer of the superior colliculus and pause before saccades, releasing collicular saccade activity; in Parkinson disease this inhibition becomes unmodulated, producing a dearth of spontaneous eye movements, and parkinsonism also lowers the blink rate. The nigrostriatal pathway is dopaminergic, not a saccade gate, and pallidothalamic and cerebellovestibular changes do not explain absent spontaneous saccades. The oculomotor nucleus receives no direct corticonuclear input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superior colliculus: superficial layer visual sensory; intermediate layer holds a map of saccades and is gated by nigrotectal GABAergic input. COMLEX: Reduced blinking and few spontaneous eye movements contribute to the masked face of parkinsonism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 7 — Parkinson disease as basal nuclei circuit dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":2,"stem":"76-year-old woman -> found unresponsive after a fall, with a rapidly expanding epidural hematoma on imaging -> over the next hour, her right pupil becomes progressively dilated and fixed, followed by complete right oculomotor palsy with ptosis and inability to move the right eye except for intact abduction -> her level of consciousness continues to decline. Which high-yield clinical pattern is occurring, and what is its clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"Uncal herniation with progressive right oculomotor nerve compression, a neurosurgical emergency indicating imminent brainstem compression that requires immediate intervention","B":"Parinaud syndrome from dorsal midbrain compression, a chronic, slowly progressive finding that does not require any emergent neurosurgical intervention","C":"Cavernous sinus syndrome from venous thrombosis, a finding that typically evolves over weeks rather than within a single hour","D":"Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia from demyelinating disease, a finding unrelated to the acute trauma and epidural hematoma","E":"Peripheral vestibular nystagmus from labyrinthine concussion, a benign finding that does not indicate any need for urgent neurosurgical evaluation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This progressive, unilateral, pupil-involving oculomotor palsy evolving over an hour in the setting of a rapidly expanding epidural hematoma is the classic presentation of uncal herniation, in which the medial temporal lobe is forced over the tentorial edge, compressing the oculomotor nerve, whose superficially located pupillary fibers are affected first, followed by progressive complete third nerve palsy as compression worsens, a neurosurgical emergency signaling imminent brainstem compression that demands immediate decompression. Parinaud syndrome is a chronic, slowly evolving dorsal midbrain compression pattern, not this acute, rapidly progressive presentation tied directly to an expanding epidural hematoma. Cavernous sinus syndrome from venous thrombosis typically evolves over days to weeks, not within a single hour, making the time course described here inconsistent with this diagnosis. Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia from demyelinating disease is unrelated to acute trauma and would not present with this progressive pupil-involving unilateral oculomotor palsy pattern following a head injury. Peripheral vestibular nystagmus from labyrinthine concussion would not produce this progressive pupillary and oculomotor palsy pattern, and mislabeling this life-threatening presentation as benign would be a dangerous clinical error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing uncal herniation as a rapidly evolving, pupil-first, then complete oculomotor palsy following head trauma is one of the most critical, must-not-miss patterns in all of clinical neurology. COMLEX: any patient with a new, progressively dilating and fixed pupil after head trauma requires emergent neuroimaging and neurosurgical consultation without delay.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — The specific vulnerability of the superficially located pupillary parasympathetic fibers within the oculomotor nerve, established under this related objective, is exactly why the pupil dilates before other oculomotor functions are lost","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":3,"stem":"An emergency physician evaluating a patient with suspected alcohol intoxication asks the patient to look rapidly back and forth between two targets. The physician explains that peak saccadic velocity is a sensitive bedside marker of sedative or alcohol effect. Which property of saccades makes this a useful screening test?","choices":{"A":"Saccades normally reach very low peak velocities, so sedative or alcohol-related slowing produces an easily detectable deviation from this expected slow baseline","B":"Saccades are entirely unaffected by sedative or alcohol exposure, so this bedside test is not actually useful for detecting intoxication in practice","C":"Saccades require visual fixation on a moving target, so this test instead measures smooth pursuit function rather than any saccadic velocity","D":"Saccades normally reach very high peak velocities, so sedative or alcohol-related slowing produces an easily detectable deviation from this expected fast baseline","E":"Saccades are generated purely at the level of the extraocular muscles themselves, so this test cannot reflect any central sedative effect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Saccades are normally the fastest class of eye movement, reaching several hundred degrees per second, so sedatives and alcohol, which slow central processing, produce a readily detectable reduction in peak saccadic velocity against this fast expected baseline, making rapid alternating gaze testing a sensitive bedside screening tool. Saccades do not normally have a low peak velocity, that description fits slower movement classes instead. Saccadic velocity is well documented to be affected by sedating substances, contradicting the claim that this test is not useful. This test uses discrete target-to-target jumps, not a continuously moving target, so it measures saccades, not pursuit. Saccades are centrally generated by brainstem burst neurons under cortical control, not purely by the extraocular muscles themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced peak saccadic velocity is a recognized, testable bedside clue for sedative, alcohol, or anticonvulsant toxicity. COMLEX: this same bedside principle underlies field sobriety and toxicology screening approaches used in clinical and forensic settings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes, tested here as a board-style functional and mechanistic vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":3,"stem":"A target moves across a patient's visual field at 150 degrees per second, well above the roughly 100 degree per second ceiling of smooth pursuit. The examiner notices the eyes fall behind the target and then jump forward periodically to catch up. Which mechanism best explains this observed pattern?","choices":{"A":"Smooth pursuit alone easily keeps pace with a target moving this quickly, so no catch-up saccades should appear","B":"Smooth pursuit alone cannot keep pace with a target moving this quickly, so intermittent catch-up saccades realign the eyes with the target","C":"The vestibulo-ocular reflex alone is responsible for this entire tracking pattern, since smooth pursuit plays no role in tracking any moving target","D":"The optokinetic response alone is responsible for this entire tracking pattern, since smooth pursuit plays no role in tracking a single moving target","E":"This pattern indicates a pathological cerebellar lesion, since a healthy nervous system should never fall behind a moving target"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Smooth pursuit has a velocity ceiling of roughly 100 degrees per second, so when a target exceeds this speed, pursuit falls behind and the visual system periodically inserts fast catch-up saccades to realign the fovea with the target, a normal, physiologic combination of two eye movement classes working together rather than a single pure movement type. Smooth pursuit cannot keep pace above its velocity ceiling, contradicting the claim that no catch-up saccades should appear. The vestibulo-ocular reflex responds to head movement, not to a moving visual target with the head stationary, so it is not the mechanism here. The optokinetic response compensates for whole-field scene motion during self-movement, not for tracking a single small target, so it is not the mechanism either. This catch-up pattern is a normal physiologic response to exceeding pursuit's velocity limit, not evidence of a pathological cerebellar lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: catch-up saccades supplementing smooth pursuit above its velocity ceiling is a normal physiologic finding, not itself a pathologic sign, an important distinction from pathologic saccadic intrusions. COMLEX: recognizing normal catch-up saccades prevents over-interpreting a fast-target bedside tracking test as abnormal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes, tested here as a board-style functional and mechanistic vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify major eye movements as saccades, pursuit, vestibulo-ocular reflex, optokinetic responses, vergence, and pupillary/accommodation reflexes.","batch":3,"stem":"While testing the near response, an examiner separately evaluates lens curvature change using a handheld autorefractor while the patient shifts focus from a distant to a near target, deliberately isolating this component from convergence and pupillary constriction. Which specific component of the near triad is being isolated by this measurement?","choices":{"A":"Accommodation, the ciliary muscle-mediated increase in lens curvature that sharpens focus on a near target","B":"Convergence, the ciliary muscle-mediated increase in lens curvature that sharpens focus on a near target","C":"Miosis, the ciliary muscle-mediated increase in lens curvature that sharpens focus on a near target","D":"Vergence, the ciliary muscle-mediated increase in lens curvature that sharpens focus on a near target","E":"Optokinetic response, the ciliary muscle-mediated increase in lens curvature that sharpens focus on a near target"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Accommodation is the specific near triad component in which parasympathetically mediated ciliary muscle contraction relaxes zonular fiber tension, increasing lens curvature and refractive power to sharpen focus on a near target, and it can be measured in isolation using instruments like an autorefractor separate from the other two near triad components. Convergence is the disconjugate eye movement component of the near triad, mediated by medial rectus contraction, not lens curvature change. Miosis is the pupillary constriction component of the near triad, mediated by iris sphincter contraction, not lens curvature change. Vergence is the broader eye movement category that includes convergence, again not the lens curvature component being measured here. The optokinetic response is an unrelated visually driven compensatory eye movement, not a component of the near triad at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the near triad has three separately testable components, accommodation, convergence, and miosis, each mediated by a distinct effector even though they are normally yoked together. COMLEX: accommodation loss with age, presbyopia, reflects declining lens elasticity rather than a neurologic near-response deficit, an important clinical distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes, tested here as a board-style functional and mechanistic vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"Between saccades, while a patient steadily fixates on a stationary target, a population of pontine neurons remains tonically active, continuously suppressing the burst neurons that would otherwise generate an unwanted saccade. Which cell population is responsible for this ongoing suppression?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory burst neurons, which tonically inhibit the saccadic burst neurons except during a deliberately triggered saccade","B":"The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, which tonically inhibits the saccadic burst neurons except during a deliberately triggered saccade","C":"Abducens internuclear neurons, which tonically inhibit the saccadic burst neurons except during a deliberately triggered saccade","D":"Omnipause cells, which tonically inhibit the saccadic burst neurons except during a deliberately triggered saccade","E":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF, which tonically inhibits the saccadic burst neurons except during a deliberately triggered saccade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Omnipause cells fire tonically during steady fixation and inhibit the saccadic burst neurons, preventing unwanted saccades, and this inhibition briefly pauses just before and during a deliberately triggered saccade, allowing the burst neurons to fire and generate the movement, a gating mechanism essential for stable fixation between voluntary gaze shifts. Excitatory burst neurons themselves generate the saccadic pulse rather than suppressing it, the opposite of the role described. The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi provides the tonic step signal that maintains eccentric eye position after a saccade, not the fixation-period suppression of burst neurons. Abducens internuclear neurons coordinate contralateral medial rectus activation during conjugate gaze, not the burst-neuron gating function described here. The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF generates the phasic burst signal for vertical saccades, not the tonic suppression of horizontal saccadic burst neurons during fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: omnipause cells as the tonic gatekeeper preventing unwanted saccades during fixation is a useful addition to the pulse-and-step framework already built for horizontal gaze. COMLEX: dysfunction of omnipause cell gating is implicated in certain saccadic intrusion disorders, such as opsoclonus, where unwanted saccades escape suppression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — the horizontal gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style circuit-component vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"During a clean rightward saccade, the right PPRF must simultaneously drive the right lateral rectus into contraction while ensuring the right medial rectus, the antagonist for that eye, relaxes rather than co-contracts. Which two populations within the PPRF together accomplish this paired activation and relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory burst neurons acting entirely alone, since inhibitory burst neurons play no functional role in horizontal saccade generation","B":"Omnipause cells and the nucleus prepositus hypoglossi acting together, since these two populations jointly generate the saccadic pulse of activity itself","C":"Abducens internuclear neurons and excitatory burst neurons acting together, since these two populations jointly generate the saccadic pulse of activity","D":"Excitatory burst neurons, which drive the agonist abducens motor neurons, and inhibitory burst neurons, which silence the antagonist, acting together","E":"Inhibitory burst neurons acting entirely alone, since excitatory burst neurons play no functional role in horizontal saccade generation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A clean saccade requires reciprocal innervation, excitatory burst neurons drive the agonist motor neurons for rapid contraction while inhibitory burst neurons simultaneously suppress the antagonist motor neurons, preventing co-contraction that would slow or distort the movement, and both populations act together within the PPRF to produce this paired activation-and-relaxation pattern. Excitatory burst neurons alone cannot explain antagonist relaxation, inhibitory burst neurons are a distinct, functionally necessary population, not an irrelevant one. Omnipause cells and the nucleus prepositus hypoglossi serve fixation-suppression and tonic position-holding roles respectively, not the paired agonist-antagonist pulse generation described here. Abducens internuclear neurons coordinate the contralateral eye's medial rectus for conjugate gaze, a separate function from the local agonist-antagonist pulse generation within the PPRF itself. Inhibitory burst neurons alone cannot explain agonist activation, excitatory burst neurons are equally necessary, not functionally irrelevant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal innervation via paired excitatory and inhibitory burst neurons is the same general principle seen in spinal reflex circuits, now applied to saccade generation. COMLEX: this reciprocal innervation concept reinforces why isolated excitatory or inhibitory burst neuron dysfunction can produce distinct, distinguishable saccadic abnormalities.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — the horizontal gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style circuit-component vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Trace the horizontal gaze pathway from frontal eye field to PPRF, abducens nucleus, MLF, and oculomotor nucleus.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher distinguishes cortical eye fields by function: one region generates voluntary, intentional saccades toward remembered or verbally instructed targets, while a separate parietal region generates faster, reflexive saccades toward a sudden, unexpected visual stimulus. Which structure corresponds to the voluntary saccade-generating region already traced to the PPRF?","choices":{"A":"The frontal eye field, which generates voluntary saccades and sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF","B":"The parietal eye field, which generates voluntary saccades and sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF","C":"The primary visual cortex, which generates voluntary saccades and sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF","D":"The superior colliculus alone, which generates voluntary saccades and sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF","E":"The supplementary motor area, which generates voluntary saccades and sends crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The frontal eye field is the cortical region responsible for voluntary, intentional saccades, sending crossed excitatory projections to the contralateral PPRF as already established for the horizontal gaze pathway, while the parietal eye field instead specializes in faster, reflexive saccades triggered by sudden, unexpected visual stimuli, a functionally distinct but complementary cortical saccade-generating system. The parietal eye field is the reflexive, not voluntary, saccade generator described in the stem as the separate comparison region, so it does not fit the voluntary role being asked about here. Primary visual cortex processes visual information but is not itself a saccade-generating premotor region with direct crossed projections to the PPRF. The superior colliculus integrates both voluntary and reflexive signals and relays to brainstem gaze centers, but it is not the cortical voluntary saccade generator being described. The supplementary motor area contributes to complex, sequenced voluntary movements more broadly but is not the primary cortical structure already traced to the PPRF for voluntary horizontal saccades.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing the frontal eye field, voluntary saccades, from the parietal eye field, reflexive saccades, adds useful cortical nuance to the horizontal gaze pathway already built around the frontal eye field. COMLEX: this voluntary-versus-reflexive distinction has practical relevance in conditions affecting frontal lobe function, where reflexive saccades toward novel stimuli may become disproportionately prominent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — the horizontal gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style circuit-component vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a large intracranial mass and papilledema develops bilateral abduction failure, but no other cranial nerve or brainstem finding is present, and the abduction deficit does not reliably localize the mass itself. Which principle explains why an isolated abducens palsy can be a misleading, nonlocalizing sign in the setting of raised intracranial pressure?","choices":{"A":"The abducens nerve is the shortest of all cranial nerves, so even mild pressure changes anywhere in the skull reliably and precisely pinpoint the mass location itself","B":"The abducens nucleus is uniquely sensitive to any elevation in intracranial pressure, so its dysfunction always localizes accurately to a pontine mass","C":"The abducens nerve has a long, vulnerable intracranial course, so raised intracranial pressure can stretch it regardless of mass location, a false localizing sign","D":"Elevated intracranial pressure affects the oculomotor nerve exclusively among the cranial nerves, making abducens involvement a coincidental finding","E":"Abducens palsy in this setting always indicates direct mass compression of the nerve itself, precisely localizing to the exact site of the underlying tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The abducens nerve travels an unusually long course along the skull base and can be stretched or compressed against bony structures by diffusely elevated intracranial pressure regardless of where the causative mass actually sits, so an isolated abducens palsy in this setting is a well-recognized false localizing sign rather than a reliable indicator of local mass location. The abducens nerve is not the shortest cranial nerve, its unusually long course is precisely why it is vulnerable in this way. The abducens nucleus itself is not the structure typically stretched by generalized pressure elevation, the vulnerability described here is specific to the nerve's long intracranial course, not heightened nuclear sensitivity that would localize to the pons specifically. Elevated intracranial pressure is not limited to affecting only the oculomotor nerve, and framing this abducens finding as coincidental ignores the well-established false localizing sign mechanism. This abducens palsy specifically does not reliably indicate direct local compression at the mass site, that is exactly what makes it a false, rather than a true, localizing sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abducens palsy as a false localizing sign of raised intracranial pressure, from generalized nerve stretch rather than direct local compression, is a classic, frequently tested exception to standard localization logic. COMLEX: recognizing this exception prevents incorrectly attributing an isolated abducens palsy to a specific pontine or orbital lesion when a diffuse process is actually responsible.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — CN VI nerve-versus-nucleus-versus-MLF localization, tested here as a board-style mimicker vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient appears to have bilateral abduction weakness on examination, but the examiner notices that both pupils constrict briskly whenever the patient attempts to look to either side, and abduction improves substantially when the patient is asked to focus on a distant object instead. Which condition best explains this apparent, rather than true, bilateral abducens dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Spasm of the near reflex, in which excessive convergence and accommodation, with accompanying miosis, mimics bilateral abduction weakness","B":"Bilateral abducens nerve palsy, in which excessive convergence and accommodation, with accompanying miosis, mimics bilateral abduction weakness","C":"Bilateral abducens nucleus lesions, in which excessive convergence and accommodation, with accompanying miosis, mimics bilateral abduction weakness","D":"Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, in which excessive convergence and accommodation, with accompanying miosis, mimics bilateral abduction weakness","E":"Bilateral oculomotor nerve palsy, in which excessive convergence and accommodation, with accompanying miosis, mimics bilateral abduction weakness"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spasm of the near reflex is a functional, often psychogenic or seizure-related condition in which excessive, involuntary activation of the convergence, accommodation, and miosis triad pulls both eyes inward, mimicking bilateral abduction weakness, but the accompanying miosis during attempted lateral gaze and improved abduction with distant fixation, which removes the drive for convergence, reveal the true mechanism and distinguish it from a genuine structural lesion. True bilateral abducens nerve palsy would not produce this pupillary miosis specifically tied to attempted lateral gaze, nor would it improve with distant fixation, since the nerve itself would remain structurally impaired regardless of fixation distance. True bilateral abducens nucleus lesions would likewise not resolve with distance fixation and would typically also impair contralateral adduction, an inconsistent picture with this presentation. True bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia impairs adduction, not abduction, making it an inconsistent explanation for this specific abduction-weakness presentation. True bilateral oculomotor nerve palsy would be expected to produce ptosis, mydriasis, and broader ophthalmoplegia, not this specific miosis-associated, fixation-distance-dependent pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spasm of the near reflex mimicking bilateral abducens palsy, with miosis and fixation-distance dependence as the distinguishing clues, is a classic functional mimicker worth recognizing to avoid unnecessary structural workup. COMLEX: eliciting miosis during attempted lateral gaze testing is the single most useful bedside clue for unmasking this functional condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — CN VI nerve-versus-nucleus-versus-MLF localization, tested here as a board-style mimicker vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate a CN VI lesion from an abducens nucleus lesion and from an MLF lesion.","batch":3,"stem":"An infant is noted to have limited abduction of one eye since birth, along with narrowing of the palpebral fissure and retraction of the globe into the orbit specifically when attempting adduction of that same eye. There is no history of trauma, stroke, or demyelinating disease. Which congenital condition best explains this lifelong pattern, and what is its underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"An acquired abducens nerve palsy, from congenital absence of the abducens nucleus with aberrant oculomotor innervation of the lateral rectus, causing co-contraction","B":"An acquired abducens nucleus lesion, from congenital absence of the abducens nucleus with aberrant oculomotor innervation of the lateral rectus, causing co-contraction","C":"An acquired medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion, from congenital absence of the abducens nucleus with aberrant oculomotor innervation of the lateral rectus","D":"An acquired myasthenic process, from congenital absence of the abducens nucleus with aberrant oculomotor innervation of the lateral rectus, causing co-contraction","E":"Duane retraction syndrome, from congenital absence of the abducens nucleus with aberrant oculomotor innervation of the lateral rectus, causing co-contraction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duane retraction syndrome is a congenital cranial dysinnervation disorder in which the abducens nucleus fails to develop and the lateral rectus instead receives aberrant innervation from a branch of the oculomotor nerve, so on attempted adduction, both the medial and lateral rectus co-contract, retracting the globe and narrowing the palpebral fissure, a lifelong pattern present since birth rather than an acquired lesion. This is a congenital, not acquired, abducens nerve process, the nerve and nucleus are absent from development rather than damaged later by an acquired lesion. Likewise, this is not an acquired abducens nucleus lesion, the nucleus never formed in the first place. It is not an acquired MLF lesion, the MLF is not the structure responsible for this specific co-contraction and globe retraction pattern. It is not a myasthenic process, which would be acquired, fluctuating, and would not classically produce this fixed, lifelong retraction pattern present since birth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Duane retraction syndrome, a congenital cranial dysinnervation disorder causing globe retraction on adduction, is a classic pediatric mimicker of acquired abducens palsy that tests whether history, lifelong versus acquired, was used correctly. COMLEX: recognizing a lifelong, since-birth history is key to avoiding unnecessary acute stroke or demyelinating disease workup in this specific presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — CN VI nerve-versus-nucleus-versus-MLF localization, tested here as a board-style mimicker vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia also has both eyes drifting outward at rest, exotropia, in addition to the expected bilateral adduction failure on conjugate horizontal gaze. Which named variant of bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia describes this combined presentation?","choices":{"A":"One-and-a-half syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, combining bilateral adduction failure with exotropia at rest","B":"Parinaud syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, combining bilateral adduction failure with exotropia at rest","C":"WEBINO syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, combining bilateral adduction failure with exotropia at rest","D":"Claude syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, combining bilateral adduction failure with exotropia at rest","E":"Foville syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, combining bilateral adduction failure with exotropia at rest"},"correct":"C","explanation":"WEBINO syndrome, wall-eyed bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, describes bilateral MLF damage severe enough to produce both the expected bilateral adduction failure on conjugate gaze and a resting exotropia, an outward drift of both eyes, reflecting more extensive bilateral paramedian brainstem involvement than typical bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia alone. One-and-a-half syndrome describes a unilateral horizontal gaze palsy combined with a contralateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia from a single paramedian pontine lesion, an entirely different combination of findings than this bilateral, symmetric exotropic pattern. Parinaud syndrome is a dorsal midbrain vertical gaze and pupillary syndrome, unrelated to this horizontal adduction and resting exotropia pattern. Claude syndrome combines an oculomotor palsy with contralateral ataxia from red nucleus involvement, an entirely different midbrain pattern than this bilateral MLF presentation. Foville syndrome combines abducens and corticospinal tract involvement in the pons, producing crossed hemiplegia with abduction paralysis, not this specific bilateral adduction failure with exotropia pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: WEBINO syndrome as a more severe named variant of bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, adding resting exotropia to the expected adduction failure, is a useful extension of the standard bilateral INO teaching. COMLEX: recognizing WEBINO as still fitting within the broader bilateral MLF disease category, most often multiple sclerosis, prevents unnecessary additional diagnostic consideration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 4 — internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome, tested here as a board-style named-variant vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"In addition to the expected horizontal adduction failure of internuclear ophthalmoplegia, a patient is also found to have a vertical misalignment between the two eyes, with one eye positioned slightly higher than the other, from disruption of otolithic, utricular-derived vestibular projections that pass near the MLF. Which additional finding is being described?","choices":{"A":"Nystagmus of the abducting eye, a vertical ocular misalignment from disrupted otolithic vestibular input traveling near the medial longitudinal fasciculus","B":"Convergence-retraction nystagmus, a vertical ocular misalignment from disrupted otolithic vestibular input traveling near the medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Downbeat nystagmus, a vertical ocular misalignment from disrupted otolithic vestibular input traveling near the medial longitudinal fasciculus","D":"Opsoclonus, a vertical ocular misalignment from disrupted otolithic vestibular input traveling near the medial longitudinal fasciculus","E":"Skew deviation, a vertical ocular misalignment from disrupted otolithic vestibular input traveling near the medial longitudinal fasciculus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Skew deviation is a vertical misalignment between the two eyes that can accompany internuclear ophthalmoplegia when otolithic, utricular-derived vestibular projections traveling near the MLF are also disrupted, reflecting the close anatomic proximity of these vestibular pathways to the classic horizontal adduction circuitry. Nystagmus of the abducting eye is the classic accompanying horizontal finding of internuclear ophthalmoplegia itself, not a separate vertical misalignment finding. Convergence-retraction nystagmus is a distinct finding associated with dorsal midbrain, Parinaud syndrome, pathology, not this MLF-adjacent vertical misalignment. Downbeat nystagmus localizes to the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures, an entirely different anatomic region than the MLF-adjacent otolithic pathway described here. Opsoclonus describes chaotic, multidirectional saccadic intrusions from omnipause cell or cerebellar dysfunction, not a vertical misalignment from otolithic pathway disruption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: skew deviation as an additional finding that can accompany internuclear ophthalmoplegia, from nearby otolithic pathway involvement, adds useful nuance beyond the classic horizontal-only teaching. COMLEX: recognizing skew deviation as a vestibular, rather than purely ocular motor nerve, finding helps distinguish it from a fourth or third nerve palsy causing vertical misalignment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 4 — internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome, tested here as a board-style named-variant vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings of internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with known thyroid eye disease has restricted adduction of one eye that superficially resembles internuclear ophthalmoplegia, but convergence is also impaired in that same eye, unlike true internuclear ophthalmoplegia where convergence is spared. Which bedside maneuver would best confirm a mechanical, restrictive cause rather than a true MLF lesion?","choices":{"A":"Forced duction testing, in which the examiner manually attempts to rotate the anesthetized globe and encounters mechanical resistance from restrictive fibrosis","B":"Repeat neurologic examination alone, in which the examiner manually attempts to rotate the anesthetized globe and encounters mechanical resistance from restrictive fibrosis","C":"Formal visual field testing alone, in which the examiner manually attempts to rotate the anesthetized globe and encounters mechanical resistance from restrictive fibrosis","D":"Pharmacologic pupil testing alone, in which the examiner manually attempts to rotate the anesthetized globe and encounters mechanical resistance from restrictive fibrosis","E":"Caloric vestibular testing alone, in which the examiner manually attempts to rotate the anesthetized globe and encounters mechanical resistance from restrictive fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Forced duction testing, manually rotating the anesthetized globe with forceps, directly detects mechanical restriction from fibrotic or infiltrated extraocular muscles, as occurs in thyroid eye disease, distinguishing a restrictive myopathy from a true neurologic internuclear ophthalmoplegia, in which the globe rotates freely when passively moved since the muscle itself is structurally normal. Repeat neurologic examination alone would not distinguish a mechanical restriction from a true central lesion, since both can produce a similar-appearing active adduction deficit on standard testing. Formal visual field testing evaluates the visual field, not extraocular muscle mechanics, and would not help distinguish a restrictive from a neurologic adduction deficit. Pharmacologic pupil testing evaluates pupillary innervation, an entirely separate system from extraocular muscle mechanics, and is not useful for this specific distinction. Caloric vestibular testing evaluates the vestibulo-ocular reflex pathway, not local muscle mechanics, and would not detect restrictive fibrosis of the medial rectus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: forced duction testing is the definitive bedside maneuver for distinguishing a mechanical, restrictive cause of impaired eye movement from a true neurologic, MLF-based internuclear ophthalmoplegia. COMLEX: thyroid eye disease characteristically restricts the inferior and medial rectus muscles, producing this specific pattern of impaired adduction and elevation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 4 — internuclear ophthalmoplegia and one-and-a-half syndrome, tested here as a board-style named-variant vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a Chiari malformation, in which the cerebellar tonsils herniate through the foramen magnum, is found to have nystagmus that beats in the downward direction on primary gaze, worsening on lateral gaze. Which localization best fits this specific nystagmus pattern?","choices":{"A":"The rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, producing downbeat nystagmus distinct from the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures","B":"The interstitial nucleus of Cajal, producing downbeat nystagmus distinct from the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures","C":"The posterior commissure, producing downbeat nystagmus distinct from the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures","D":"The paramedian pontine reticular formation, producing downbeat nystagmus distinct from the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures","E":"The craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures, producing downbeat nystagmus distinct from the riMLF or interstitial nucleus of Cajal vertical gaze circuitry"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Downbeat nystagmus classically localizes to the craniocervical junction and cervicomedullary structures, a distinct anatomic region from the rostral midbrain vertical gaze circuitry, and is a recognized finding in Chiari malformation, where tonsillar herniation compresses this cervicomedullary region. The riMLF is the rostral midbrain vertical saccade burst center and is not the classic localization for downbeat nystagmus specifically. The interstitial nucleus of Cajal provides the tonic vertical gaze-holding signal in the midbrain and is likewise not the classic downbeat nystagmus localization. The posterior commissure coordinates bilateral vertical gaze and pupillary light reflex fibers in the midbrain, an entirely different region than the cervicomedullary junction implicated in downbeat nystagmus. The paramedian pontine reticular formation is the horizontal, not vertical, gaze center and is unrelated to this specific vertical nystagmus localization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: downbeat nystagmus localizing to the craniocervical junction, classically from Chiari malformation, is a distinct anatomic pattern from the rostral midbrain vertical gaze structures already emphasized under this objective. COMLEX: downbeat nystagmus should prompt evaluation of the craniocervical junction with MRI, given its strong association with structural cervicomedullary pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 5 — the vertical gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style nystagmus and supranuclear-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":3,"stem":"A separate patient has nystagmus that beats in the upward direction on primary gaze, localizing instead to the medullary or pontine tegmentum rather than the craniocervical junction. Which comparison best distinguishes this pattern from downbeat nystagmus?","choices":{"A":"Upbeat nystagmus localizes more caudally, to the medullary or pontine tegmentum, while downbeat nystagmus localizes to the craniocervical junction, a more rostral structure","B":"Upbeat and downbeat nystagmus localize to the same structure in every case, distinguished only by which direction the fast phase happens to beat","C":"Upbeat nystagmus is a purely normal physiologic variant, while downbeat nystagmus is always pathologic and requires urgent structural imaging","D":"Upbeat nystagmus localizes to the rostral midbrain riMLF specifically, while downbeat nystagmus localizes to the interstitial nucleus of Cajal","E":"Upbeat nystagmus localizes more rostrally, to the medullary or pontine tegmentum, while downbeat nystagmus localizes to the craniocervical junction, a more caudal structure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Upbeat and downbeat nystagmus localize to different vertical gaze-related structures, upbeat nystagmus to the medullary or pontine tegmentum and downbeat nystagmus to the more caudal craniocervical junction, so distinguishing beat direction provides genuine, distinct localizing information rather than pointing to the same structure. Describing upbeat nystagmus as more caudal and downbeat as more rostral reverses the actual relative localization. Upbeat and downbeat nystagmus do not localize to the exact same structure, that claim ignores their genuinely distinct anatomic correlates. Upbeat nystagmus is not a normal physiologic variant, both upbeat and downbeat nystagmus are pathologic findings that warrant further evaluation. Neither upbeat nor downbeat nystagmus localizes specifically to the riMLF or interstitial nucleus of Cajal, those rostral midbrain structures are associated with different vertical gaze deficits, not with these specific nystagmus patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: upbeat, medullary/pontine tegmentum, versus downbeat, craniocervical junction, nystagmus is a useful comparative pair for localizing central vertical nystagmus. COMLEX: both upbeat and downbeat nystagmus are considered central findings requiring further neurologic evaluation, distinct from peripheral vestibular nystagmus patterns.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 5 — the vertical gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style nystagmus and supranuclear-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the basic localization of vertical gaze pathways in the rostral midbrain.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a supranuclear vertical gaze palsy cannot voluntarily look upward, but when the examiner passively rotates the patient's head downward, the eyes reflexively move upward relative to the head, demonstrating an intact oculocephalic, doll's eye, reflex. What does this preserved reflex indicate about the level of the lesion?","choices":{"A":"The lesion lies within the oculomotor nucleus itself, sparing the final motor pathway, since the reflex arc bypasses the impaired voluntary pathway","B":"The lesion lies above the oculomotor nucleus, sparing the final motor pathway, since the reflex arc bypasses the impaired voluntary pathway","C":"The lesion lies within the oculomotor nerve itself, sparing the final motor pathway, since the reflex arc bypasses the impaired voluntary pathway","D":"The lesion lies within the extraocular muscles themselves, sparing the final motor pathway, since the reflex arc bypasses the impaired voluntary pathway","E":"The preserved reflex indicates no true vertical gaze palsy is actually present, despite the patient's inability to look upward voluntarily on command"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A preserved oculocephalic reflex despite absent voluntary vertical gaze indicates a supranuclear lesion, one located above the oculomotor nucleus and its final brainstem motor connections, since the vestibulo-ocular reflex arc reaches the same motor neurons through a separate, more caudal pathway that bypasses the higher, damaged supranuclear structures responsible for voluntary gaze. If the lesion were within the oculomotor nucleus itself, the reflex would also be impaired, since the nucleus is the shared final common pathway for both voluntary and reflexive movement. Likewise, a lesion within the oculomotor nerve would impair both voluntary and reflexive movement together, since the nerve is the final motor outflow regardless of which system drives it. A lesion within the extraocular muscles themselves would also impair the reflexive movement, since the muscles are the final effector for any driving signal. The preserved reflex does not mean no gaze palsy exists, the patient still cannot voluntarily look upward, it specifically clarifies that the lesion is supranuclear rather than nuclear or infranuclear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a preserved oculocephalic reflex despite absent voluntary gaze is the key bedside maneuver distinguishing a supranuclear gaze palsy, as in progressive supranuclear palsy, from a nuclear or infranuclear lesion. COMLEX: this same doll's eye principle is routinely applied in the comatose patient to assess brainstem integrity when voluntary movement cannot be tested.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 5 — the vertical gaze pathway, tested here as a board-style nystagmus and supranuclear-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":3,"stem":"During a swinging flashlight test, an examiner shines light into the healthy eye, both pupils constrict, and then quickly swings the light to the affected eye with a partial optic nerve lesion. Instead of maintaining or further constricting, both pupils paradoxically dilate. Which principle explains this paradoxical dilation?","choices":{"A":"The affected eye's stronger afferent signal produces more net pupilloconstrictor drive than the consensual input it had just been receiving, so the swing effectively reduces stimulation and both pupils dilate","B":"The efferent pathway to the affected eye is completely severed, so the swing produces no pupillary response rather than paradoxical dilation","C":"The affected eye's weaker afferent signal produces less net pupilloconstrictor drive than the consensual input it had just been receiving, so the swing effectively reduces stimulation and both pupils dilate","D":"The pretectal olivary nucleus is bypassed during this bedside test, so neither pupil should change in response to the swinging light","E":"The unaffected eye's consensual pathway fatigues with repeated light exposure, causing paradoxical dilation through peripheral rather than central mechanisms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When light swings from a healthy eye to an eye with a partial optic nerve lesion, the damaged afferent pathway transmits a weaker signal than the eye had just been receiving via the consensual pathway from the healthy eye, so the net stimulus effectively decreases and both pupils dilate rather than constrict, the relative afferent pupillary defect, or Marcus Gunn pupil, that the swinging flashlight test is designed to detect. The affected eye's afferent signal is weaker, not stronger, than the consensual input it had been receiving, that reversal is precisely why paradoxical dilation, rather than further constriction, occurs. This is an afferent, not efferent, defect, and the efferent pathway to the affected eye is not completely severed, some afferent signal still reaches the midbrain, just less than the consensual input. The pretectal olivary nucleus is the essential relay station for this entire response, it is not bypassed, and the test specifically depends on comparing input through this structure from each eye. This phenomenon reflects a genuine difference in afferent signal strength between the two eyes, not peripheral fatigue of the consensual pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swinging flashlight test and the resulting relative afferent pupillary defect are among the most clinically important, frequently tested pupillary examination findings. COMLEX: a relative afferent pupillary defect should prompt evaluation for optic nerve or severe retinal pathology on the affected side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — the pupillary light reflex arc, tested here as a board-style pharmacologic and bedside-testing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has a fixed, dilated pupil. Full-strength pilocarpine drops are applied and the pupil does not constrict at all. Dilute, weak pilocarpine drops are then tried on a separate occasion and also produce no constriction. Which cause of mydriasis is most consistent with this specific pharmacologic testing pattern?","choices":{"A":"Pharmacologic mydriasis from anticholinergic exposure, which competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so that neither full-strength nor dilute pilocarpine can produce constriction","B":"A third nerve palsy from oculomotor nerve compression, which competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so that neither full-strength nor dilute pilocarpine can produce constriction","C":"Adie tonic pupil from ciliary ganglion damage, which competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so that neither full-strength nor dilute pilocarpine can produce constriction","D":"Argyll Robertson pupil from tabes dorsalis, which competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so that neither full-strength nor dilute pilocarpine can produce constriction","E":"Physiologic anisocoria with no underlying pathology, which competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so that neither full-strength nor dilute pilocarpine can produce constriction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pharmacologic mydriasis, from anticholinergic eye drops or accidental exposure, competitively blocks the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors so completely that even full-strength pilocarpine cannot overcome the blockade and produce constriction, distinguishing it from other causes of mydriasis. A third nerve palsy from compression typically still responds to full-strength pilocarpine, since the receptor itself is not pharmacologically blocked, only the nerve's own input is interrupted, so the muscle retains its ability to respond to a direct cholinergic agonist at sufficient concentration. Adie tonic pupil, from ciliary ganglion damage, characteristically shows denervation supersensitivity and therefore constricts to dilute, weak pilocarpine specifically, the opposite pattern from the complete non-response described here. Argyll Robertson pupil produces small, not dilated, pupils with light-near dissociation and would not present as this fixed, dilated pupil at all. Physiologic anisocoria reflects normal, non-pathologic pupil size variation with an entirely normal, intact receptor and innervation status, so it would be expected to constrict normally to pilocarpine testing, not fail to respond at either concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing pharmacologic mydriasis, no response to any concentration of pilocarpine, from third nerve palsy, responds to full strength, and Adie tonic pupil, responds to dilute strength, is a classic three-way pharmacologic testing framework. COMLEX: always ask about potential anticholinergic exposure, medications, plants, or eye drops, when pharmacologic mydriasis is suspected on testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — the pupillary light reflex arc, tested here as a board-style pharmacologic and bedside-testing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex and near response.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with longstanding neurosyphilis has small, irregular pupils that fail to constrict to light shone in either eye but constrict briskly and normally during the near response. Which mechanism explains this light-near dissociation with small, briskly accommodating pupils, in contrast to the tonic pattern of Adie pupil?","choices":{"A":"A selective lesion of the supraoculomotor near-response circuit spares the separate pretectal light reflex pathway, so the light response remains brisk while the near response is lost","B":"A selective lesion of the pretectal light reflex pathway spares the separate supraoculomotor near-response circuit, so the near response remains brisk while the light response is lost","C":"Complete bilateral oculomotor nerve damage explains both the light and near response abnormalities together, since both circuits converge entirely on this single shared final pathway structure","D":"Bilateral damage limited to the ciliary ganglion explains this light-near dissociation pattern, exactly as it does in Adie tonic pupil","E":"This specific light-near dissociation pattern is pharmacologically produced by tertiary syphilis treatment itself rather than by the underlying neurosyphilis disease process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Argyll Robertson pupil results from a selective lesion, classically at the pretectal region, that damages the light reflex pathway while sparing the anatomically separate supraoculomotor near-response circuit, so the light response is lost while the near response, driven by this distinct circuit, remains intact and brisk, unlike Adie tonic pupil, in which the pupil is large and its near response is slow and tonic. Reversing which circuit is spared, near-response damage with light pathway sparing, would produce a slow, poorly reactive near response with a preserved light response, the opposite of what is described here. Complete bilateral oculomotor nerve damage would impair both the light and near responses together along with other findings like ptosis, not this selective preservation of the near response alone. Ciliary ganglion damage explains Adie tonic pupil's pattern, tonic near response with poor light response, not this Argyll Robertson pattern of brisk near response with absent light response. This finding reflects a lesion of the underlying neurosyphilis disease process itself, not a pharmacologic effect of its treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Argyll Robertson pupil's light-near dissociation, light response lost, near response preserved, differs from Adie tonic pupil in lesion site (pretectal versus ciliary ganglion), pupil size, and briskness of the near response, a frequently tested comparison. COMLEX: \"prostitute's pupil,\" accommodates but does not react, is a classic mnemonic for Argyll Robertson pupil in the context of neurosyphilis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — the pupillary light reflex arc, tested here as a board-style pharmacologic and bedside-testing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with acute vertigo is evaluated using a bedside battery that tests the head impulse response, the pattern of nystagmus, and the presence of skew deviation, together used to distinguish a benign peripheral cause from a dangerous central one. What is this three-part bedside battery called?","choices":{"A":"The doll's eye maneuver, head impulse, nystagmus, and test of skew, used to distinguish peripheral from central vertigo at the bedside","B":"The forced duction test, head impulse, nystagmus, and test of skew, used to distinguish peripheral from central vertigo at the bedside","C":"The three-step head-tilt test, head impulse, nystagmus, and test of skew, used to distinguish peripheral from central vertigo at the bedside","D":"The HINTS exam, head impulse, nystagmus, and test of skew, used to distinguish peripheral from central vertigo at the bedside","E":"The swinging flashlight test, head impulse, nystagmus, and test of skew, used to distinguish peripheral from central vertigo at the bedside"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The HINTS exam, head impulse, nystagmus pattern, and test of skew, is a validated three-part bedside battery used in patients with acute, continuous vertigo to distinguish a benign peripheral vestibular cause from a dangerous central, typically posterior circulation stroke, cause, and is considered more sensitive than early MRI for this purpose in the acute setting. The doll's eye maneuver, the oculocephalic reflex, is a related but distinct single test evaluating brainstem integrity, not this specific three-part combined battery. Forced duction testing evaluates mechanical restriction of the extraocular muscles, unrelated to vertigo localization. The three-step head-tilt test is used specifically to localize a trochlear nerve palsy, not to distinguish central from peripheral vertigo. The swinging flashlight test evaluates the pupillary light reflex for a relative afferent pupillary defect, unrelated to vertigo localization as well.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the HINTS exam is a high-yield, validated bedside tool for distinguishing central from peripheral vertigo, complementing the general central-versus-peripheral nystagmus reasoning already established under this objective. COMLEX: a normal head impulse test paradoxically raises suspicion for a central cause in a vertiginous patient, since an intact vestibulo-ocular reflex argues against a peripheral vestibular lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — visual motor localization, tested here as a board-style bedside-battery and named-syndrome vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with sudden-onset vertigo, dysphagia, and hoarseness is also found to have ipsilateral ptosis and miosis, plus a skew deviation with the ipsilateral eye positioned lower than the contralateral eye. Which vascular territory and mechanism best explain this combination of ocular motor findings alongside the more classic lateral medullary features?","choices":{"A":"AICA territory, in which the lateral medullary infarct damages descending sympathetic fibers, producing ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and disrupts nearby otolithic pathways, producing skew deviation","B":"SCA territory, in which the lateral medullary infarct damages descending sympathetic fibers, producing ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and disrupts nearby otolithic pathways, producing skew deviation","C":"PICA territory, in which the lateral medullary infarct damages descending sympathetic fibers, producing ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and disrupts nearby otolithic pathways, producing skew deviation","D":"Anterior spinal artery territory, in which the lateral medullary infarct damages descending sympathetic fibers, producing ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and disrupts nearby otolithic pathways, producing this deviation","E":"Posterior cerebral artery territory, in which the lateral medullary infarct damages descending sympathetic fibers, producing ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and disrupts nearby otolithic pathways, producing this deviation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PICA territory infarction produces the lateral medullary, Wallenberg, syndrome, whose classic dysphagia and hoarseness from nucleus ambiguus involvement is accompanied here by ipsilateral Horner syndrome, from damage to descending sympathetic fibers coursing through the lateral medulla, and by skew deviation, from disruption of nearby otolithic vestibular projections, together forming a recognized ocular motor extension of the classic Wallenberg presentation. AICA territory produces lateral pontine, not medullary, findings, abducens palsy, facial weakness, and hearing loss, which do not match this dysphagia and hoarseness presentation localizing to the medulla. SCA territory produces severe cerebellar ataxia with Horner syndrome but does not classically produce the dysphagia and hoarseness described here, which specifically localize to the medullary nucleus ambiguus, not the SCA's more rostral pontomesencephalic territory. Anterior spinal artery territory supplies the paramedian, not lateral, medulla, producing tongue weakness and contralateral limb weakness rather than this lateral medullary pattern of findings. The posterior cerebral artery supplies occipital and thalamic/midbrain structures, not the lateral medulla, and would not explain this combination of bulbar and lateral medullary findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing Horner syndrome and skew deviation as additional ocular motor findings that can accompany classic Wallenberg syndrome reinforces the PICA lateral medullary pattern from an ophthalmologic angle. COMLEX: ipsilateral Horner syndrome combined with crossed pain and temperature loss is a key distinguishing feature separating lateral medullary from lateral pontine brainstem stroke syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — visual motor localization, tested here as a board-style bedside-battery and named-syndrome vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use nystagmus, diplopia, pupil findings, and associated neurologic signs to localize visual motor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a left Bell palsy cannot close the left eye and has lost the left blink reflex, yet he still raises the left upper lid when looking up and lowers it when looking down, and there is no ptosis. What explains the preserved lid movements?","choices":{"A":"Corticonuclear input to the upper face is bilateral","B":"The levator palpebrae and its oculomotor supply are intact","C":"The right facial nerve supplies the left orbicularis oculi","D":"Trigeminal blink pathways project bilaterally to the lids","E":"Sympathetic input to the tarsal muscle raises the lid with gaze"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lid elevation and the lid movements that follow vertical gaze are produced by the levator palpebrae, supplied by the oculomotor nerve and working with the superior rectus; the orbicularis oculi, supplied by the facial nerve, closes the lid and is not involved, so a facial nerve lesion abolishes the blink but causes no ptosis, whereas an oculomotor lesion does the opposite. Bilateral corticonuclear input does not help a peripheral facial nerve lesion, the right facial nerve does not supply the left orbicularis, and trigeminal blink input still needs the damaged facial nerve to close the lid. The tarsal muscle helps keep the lid open but does not track gaze.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corneal blink reflex: trigeminal afferents to the spinal trigeminal nucleus, then facial motor neurons to orbicularis oculi, with inhibition of levator motor neurons. COMLEX: Bell palsy needs eye protection because blink and closure fail on the affected side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — lower motor neuron facial palsy versus upper motor neuron facial weakness","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with diffusely elevated intracranial pressure develops bilateral small, sluggish pupils progressing over hours to fixed, midposition pupils, along with hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations, a pattern distinct from the unilateral, progressively dilating pupil of uncal herniation. Which herniation pattern best explains this different, bilateral progression?","choices":{"A":"Uncal herniation, in which diffuse downward displacement produces bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression along with the Cushing reflex","B":"Cerebellar tonsillar herniation in isolation, in which diffuse downward displacement produces bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression along with the Cushing reflex","C":"Subfalcine herniation in isolation, in which diffuse downward displacement produces bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression along with the Cushing reflex","D":"Central, or transtentorial, herniation, in which diffuse downward displacement produces bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression along with the Cushing reflex","E":"Extracranial herniation through a surgical defect, in which diffuse downward displacement produces bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression along with the Cushing reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Central, or transtentorial, herniation involves diffuse downward displacement of both cerebral hemispheres and the diencephalon, producing bilateral, symmetric brainstem compression that manifests as bilateral small, sluggish pupils progressing to fixed midposition pupils, along with the Cushing reflex, hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations, a distinctly bilateral and symmetric pattern compared with the unilateral, asymmetric progression of uncal herniation. Uncal herniation specifically produces unilateral, ipsilateral pupillary findings from focal oculomotor nerve compression, not this bilateral, symmetric progression, making it an inconsistent match for this presentation. Cerebellar tonsillar herniation alone primarily compresses the medulla, producing respiratory and cardiovascular compromise, but is not typically described with this specific bilateral pupillary progression pattern as its defining ocular feature. Subfalcine herniation displaces cingulate gyrus tissue under the falx cerebri and primarily produces anterior cerebral artery compromise, not this specific bilateral pupillary and brainstem compression pattern. Extracranial herniation through a surgical defect is a distinct, less common phenomenon following craniectomy and does not describe this diffuse, symmetric downward pattern from elevated intracranial pressure alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: contrasting central, transtentorial herniation, bilateral and symmetric, with uncal herniation, unilateral and asymmetric, is a high-yield distinction for recognizing different herniation syndromes. COMLEX: the Cushing reflex, hypertension with bradycardia, is a late, ominous sign indicating critically elevated intracranial pressure requiring immediate intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the high-yield ocular motor patterns, tested here as a board-style alternate-pattern and synthesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a mass at the orbital apex has the expected diplopia, ptosis, and facial numbness of cavernous sinus or superior orbital fissure involvement, but also has progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect, findings not explained by either of those syndromes alone. Which additional structure's involvement explains this expanded presentation, and what is it called?","choices":{"A":"Cavernous sinus syndrome alone, in which optic nerve involvement adds progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ocular motor and trigeminal findings","B":"Superior orbital fissure syndrome alone, in which optic nerve involvement adds progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ocular motor and trigeminal findings","C":"Orbital apex syndrome, in which optic nerve involvement adds progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ocular motor and trigeminal findings","D":"Parinaud syndrome, in which optic nerve involvement adds progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ocular motor and trigeminal findings","E":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia, in which optic nerve involvement adds progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ocular motor and trigeminal findings"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Orbital apex syndrome occurs when a mass extends far enough posteriorly to involve the optic nerve in addition to the same cranial nerves affected in superior orbital fissure or cavernous sinus syndrome, adding progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect to the expected ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, and trigeminal sensory findings, distinguishing it as a more extensive process than either syndrome alone. Cavernous sinus syndrome alone, without additional optic nerve involvement, does not classically produce this specific combination of progressive visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect. Superior orbital fissure syndrome alone likewise does not include the optic nerve, which passes through a separate canal, so it would not explain this visual loss and afferent pupillary defect. Parinaud syndrome is a dorsal midbrain vertical gaze and pupillary syndrome unrelated to this anterior orbital apex mass presentation. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from an isolated MLF lesion and does not produce this combination of ptosis, trigeminal numbness, and optic nerve dysfunction from an orbital mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: orbital apex syndrome as a superset of superior orbital fissure syndrome, adding optic nerve involvement and visual loss, is a useful anatomic extension of the cavernous sinus and superior orbital fissure teaching already established. COMLEX: the presence of visual loss and a relative afferent pupillary defect alongside ophthalmoplegia should specifically prompt consideration of orbital apex, rather than isolated cavernous sinus, involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the high-yield ocular motor patterns, tested here as a board-style alternate-pattern and synthesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl45_bloom_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize high-yield clinical patterns including Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation CN III compression, cavernous sinus lesions, vestibular nystagmus, and INO.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has bilateral upward gaze palsy, eyelid retraction, and light-near pupillary dissociation, with no ptosis, no facial numbness, and normal peripheral vestibular testing. Applying elimination logic across Parinaud syndrome, uncal herniation, cavernous sinus syndrome, peripheral vestibular nystagmus, and internuclear ophthalmoplegia, which single pattern remains consistent with every finding described?","choices":{"A":"Uncal herniation, since bilateral upward gaze palsy with lid retraction and light-near dissociation, without ptosis, facial numbness, or vestibular abnormality, uniquely fits only this pattern","B":"Parinaud syndrome, since bilateral upward gaze palsy with lid retraction and light-near dissociation, without ptosis, facial numbness, or vestibular abnormality, uniquely fits only this pattern","C":"Cavernous sinus syndrome, since bilateral upward gaze palsy with lid retraction and light-near dissociation, without ptosis, facial numbness, or vestibular abnormality, uniquely fits only this pattern","D":"Peripheral vestibular nystagmus, since bilateral upward gaze palsy with lid retraction and light-near dissociation, without ptosis, facial numbness, or vestibular abnormality, uniquely fits only this pattern","E":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia, since bilateral upward gaze palsy with lid retraction and light-near dissociation, without ptosis, facial numbness, or vestibular abnormality, uniquely fits only this pattern"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uncal herniation is eliminated because it produces a progressive, unilateral, pupil-involving oculomotor palsy with ptosis, not this bilateral vertical gaze and lid pattern. Cavernous sinus syndrome is eliminated because it requires trigeminal facial numbness alongside ocular motor findings, which is explicitly absent here. Peripheral vestibular nystagmus is eliminated because vestibular testing is explicitly normal, and it would not explain vertical gaze palsy, lid retraction, or light-near dissociation in any case. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is eliminated because it produces a horizontal adduction deficit, not this bilateral upward gaze palsy, and does not classically produce lid retraction or light-near dissociation. Parinaud syndrome alone, from dorsal midbrain and posterior commissure compression, accounts for the complete triad of bilateral upward gaze palsy, eyelid retraction, and light-near pupillary dissociation while remaining fully consistent with the absence of ptosis, facial numbness, and vestibular abnormality, making it the only pattern that fits every finding described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: working through this five-pattern elimination exercise reinforces that each high-yield syndrome under this objective has a distinct, non-overlapping fingerprint of findings. COMLEX: systematically ruling out competing patterns, rather than pattern-matching on a single feature alone, is the safest approach to board-style localization vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the high-yield ocular motor patterns, tested here as a board-style alternate-pattern and synthesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}]};
